ine_0: ##   0:00:50  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:51  Status: failed\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:52 (52)\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:39 (39)\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: insn_beq_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_insn_check_sv_198_153 at rvfi_insn_check.sv:198.6-198.31 step 20\\nSBY 14:20:41 [insn_beq_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-003-r9s2/formal/last_run-36910.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-luna-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260710-003-r9s2/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-36910/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 9, \"scribe_skipped\": \"TimeoutError: scribe timed out after 240s\"}\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Split the RVFI retirement order counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The retirement order is maintained by a single 64-bit incrementer in the\\nwriteback/RVFI boundary.  That counter is not part of architectural\\nexecution, but its feedback path can still consume a long carry chain and\\nLUT routing resources on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The retirement order is maintained by a single 64-bit incrementer in the\\nwriteback/RVFI boundary.  That counter is not part of architectural\\nexecution, but its feedback path can still consume a long carry chain and\\nLUT routing resources on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the monolithic 64-bit order increment with separate low and high\\n32-bit counters.  On each valid retirement, increment the low half and\\nincrement the high half only when the low half is 0xffffffff; expose their\\nconcatenation as the unchanged 64-bit rvfi_order_0.  This should shorten\\nthe common feedback path to a 32-bit carry chain and reduce RVFI-side LUT\\npressure without changing retirement timing, pipeline behavior, or the\\nrequired exact +1 sequence across 64-bit rollover.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Split rvfi_order_q into low/high 32-bit state with explicit rollover carry, preserving reset behavior and the exact 64-bit RVFI order output.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Split rvfi_order_q into low/high 32-bit state with explicit rollover carry, preserving reset behavior and the exact 64-bit RVFI order output.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 325.08, \"delta_pct\": -29.73, \"vs_baseline\": 14.94, \"fmax_mhz\": 141.4, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10166, \"ff\": 1981, \"seeds\": [141.4, 135.67, 144.32], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:35:04.081415Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 (regression, -29.73%): Splitting the RVFI 64-bit counter into low/high halves did not guarantee a shorter synthesized feedback path, and the low-half terminal compare plus high-half enable can add enough FPGA control/routing cost to overwhelm the intended carry-chain reduction.\"}\n30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Response-gated load-use execute bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The five-stage core currently inserts a full front-end bubble for every\\nload-use pair, even when the data bus returns the load in its first\\navailable cycle.  CoreMark's pointer-heavy code creates enough dependent\\nloads that this costs iterations/cycle, while the existing EX/MEM-to-EX\\nbypass already provides the natural structural point for a response path.\\nThe prior direct-load-bypass attempt was unsafe because it released the\\ninterlock without proving that a usable same-cycle response existed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage core currently inserts a full front-end bubble for every\\nload-use pair, even when the data bus returns the load in its first\\navailable cycle.  CoreMark's pointer-heavy code creates enough dependent\\nloads that this costs iterations/cycle, while the existing EX/MEM-to-EX\\nbypass already provides the natural structural point for a response path.\\nThe prior direct-load-bypass attempt was unsafe because it released the\\ninterlock without proving that a usable same-cycle response existed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the unconditional one-cycle load-use bubble with a response-gated\\ninterlock.  Let the dependent instruction enter ID/EX, then hold that\\ninstruction and the outstanding EX/MEM load only while io_dmemReady is low.\\nWhen the load is valid, aligned, addressed to a nonzero destination, and\\nio_dmemReady is high, forward the already extracted load value directly\\nfrom MEM to the dependent EX operands; otherwise retain the stall or use\\nthe normal register/MEM-WB path.  Keep EX/MEM ALU forwarding disabled for\\nloads, qualify the bypass with the exact memory response and load width,\\nand preserve misalignment traps, redirect behavior, RVFI ordering, and\\nstore/load bus side effects.  Removing the avoidable load-use bubble should\\nraise CoreMark IPC with only a small response mux and control change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with a response-aware hold that stalls only when an EX/MEM load has no valid dmem response, while retaining backend stall and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a distinct EX/MEM load-response forwarding selection and prevent the ordinary ALU-result bypass from forwarding a load address as data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a highest-priority, response-valid load-data operand path for dependent rs1/rs2 values, falling back safely when the load is misaligned or the bus is stalled.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the existing width/sign/alignment-correct load extraction as a same-cycle bypass value, qualified by a valid aligned load response.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect dmemReady, EX/MEM load metadata, the MEM response bypass, and the retimed hazard/forwarding controls without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with a response-aware hold that stalls only when an EX/MEM load has no valid dmem response, while retaining backend stall and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a distinct EX/MEM load-response forwarding selection and prevent the ordinary ALU-result bypass from forwarding a load address as data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a highest-priority, response-valid load-data operand path for dependent rs1/rs2 values, falling back safely when the load is misaligned or the bus is stalled.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the existing width/sign/alignment-correct load extraction as a same-cycle bypass value, qualified by a valid aligned load response.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect dmemReady, EX/MEM load metadata, the MEM response bypass, and the retimed hazard/forwarding controls without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10}\n31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Confidence-gated loop target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches currently resolve in EX, so a taken loop branch\\ndiscards younger instructions from the two-entry fetch queue.  Static\\nbackward prediction mispredicts exits, while the earlier BTB/BHT attempt\\nput an expensive lookup on the fetch timing path.  CoreMark's repeated\\nloops make a small, trained target cache a narrower opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Conditional branches currently resolve in EX, so a taken loop branch\\ndiscards younger instructions from the two-entry fetch queue.  Static\\nbackward prediction mispredicts exits, while the earlier BTB/BHT attempt\\nput an expensive lookup on the fetch timing path.  CoreMark's repeated\\nloops make a small, trained target cache a narrower opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry tagged loop-target table trained only by EX-resolved\\nconditional branches.  Each entry stores the branch PC, its resolved\\ntaken target, and a saturating confidence state; predict taken only after\\nrepeated same-PC taken outcomes, and stop predicting after a not-taken\\noutcome has decayed the confidence.  The queue-head lookup uses the\\nregistered target from the table rather than computing a PC-plus-immediate\\nadder on the ordinary imem address path.  On a confident hit, atomically\\nreplace younger queue contents and advance fetch to the stored target;\\ncarry the prediction with the branch and let EX recover only when the\\nresolved next PC differs.  Keep reset, imem stalls, alignment traps,\\nRVFI pc_next, and predictor training coordinated with the same queue\\ntransaction so untrained, exit, and unstable branches remain sequential.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a resettable two-entry tagged loop-target table with saturating confidence, stored targets, queue-head lookup, and EX outcome training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-valid, predicted-taken, and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the predictor, including queue-head lookup, EX training, prediction mismatch recovery, and complete RTL source integration.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform an atomic confident-loop prediction transaction that flushes younger entries and updates fetch_pc_q from the stored target while preserving redirect and imem-ready priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate loop prediction metadata alongside the consumed instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the stored prediction with the resolved conditional-branch next PC and redirect only on mismatch, preserving traps and RVFI semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a resettable two-entry tagged loop-target table with saturating confidence, stored targets, queue-head lookup, and EX outcome training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-valid, predicted-taken, and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the predictor, including queue-head lookup, EX training, prediction mismatch recovery, and complete RTL source integration.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform an atomic confident-loop prediction transaction that flushes younger entries and updates fetch_pc_q from the stored target while preserving redirect and imem-ready priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate loop prediction metadata alongside the consumed instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the stored prediction with the resolved conditional-branch next PC and redirect only on mismatch, preserving traps and RVFI semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 196.39, \"delta_pct\": -57.55, \"vs_baseline\": -30.56, \"fmax_mhz\": 85.7, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4363691, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 11393, \"ff\": 2156, \"seeds\": [85.7, 93.35, 84.61], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:42:31.493583Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -57.55%): Confidence gating did not make a two-entry loop-target predictor cheap enough because queue-head `if_head_*` lookup, `predict_fire` target selection, and carried prediction metadata still widen the fetch/redirect control path; predictor selectivity cannot offset front-end timing overhead unless the prediction transaction is isolated from ordinary `imem_addr` selection.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | [.id,.outcome,(.delta_pct // \\\"\\\"),.title] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nCHANGES: \\\" + ((.changes // .hypothesis.changes // []) | map(.file+\\\" — \\\"+.description) | join(\\\"; \\\"))' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | [.id,.outcome,(.delta_pct // \\\"\\\"),.title] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl && jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"micro_opt\\\") | \\\"\\\\n\\\" + .id + \\\"\\\\n\\\" + (.hypothesis.hypothesis // \\\"\\\") + \\\"\\\\nCHANGES: \\\" + ((.changes // .hypothesis.changes // []) | map(.file+\\\" — \\\"+.description) | join(\\\"; \\\"))' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"baseline-bench-a2934e6\timprovement\t0.0\tBaseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20260710-001-r1s0\timprovement\t43.11\tShare the multiplier across RV32M high-half operations\nhyp-20260710-001-r2s0\tbroken\t\tSource-aware load-use hazard gating\nhyp-20260710-001-r3s0\tregression\t-27.84\tShare the RV32M divide datapath\nhyp-20260710-001-r4s0\tregression\t-25.79\tConstrain the shared multiplier to 32-bit operands\nhyp-20260710-001-r5s0\tregression\t-17.77\tFactor the EX control-flow target adder\nhyp-20260710-001-r6s0\tregression\t-36.41\tCollapse EX forwarding into direct operand bypasses\nhyp-20260710-001-r7s0\tregression\t-7.59\tUse explicit memory lane selection\nhyp-20260710-001-r8s0\tregression\t-16.09\tShare the EX branch comparator\nhyp-20260710-001-r9s0\tregression\t-19.0\tCarry PC plus four into execute\nhyp-20260710-001-r10s0\tregression\t-29.73\tSplit the RVFI retirement order counter\n\nbaseline-bench-a2934e6\n\nCHANGES: \n\nhyp-20260710-001-r1s0\nReplace the three parallel products with one unsigned 32x32 64-bit product.\nDerive MULH and MULHSU high halves by subtracting the appropriate sign\ncorrection terms from that product, while retaining the same low-half MUL\nresult and the existing ALTOPS behavior. Sharing the multiplier should\nreduce area and routing congestion, improving Fmax without adding cycles or\nchanging the pipeline's RV32M latency.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/alu.sv — Use one unsigned 64-bit product and sign-correction logic for MULH and MULHSU instead of three parallel multipliers, preserving ALTOPS and exact RV32M results.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r2s0\nAdd precise source-use metadata for the instruction in IF/ID and gate the\nload-use comparison with it: compare rs1 only for instructions that read\nrs1, rs2 only for instructions that read rs2, and neither for instructions\nwith no register sources.  Preserve the existing x0 exclusion, redirect\nflushing, and dmem-stall priority.  Removing false bubbles should improve\nCoreMark iterations per cycle with negligible area and timing cost while\nleaving the architectural forwarding behavior unchanged.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/hazard_unit.sv — Gate load-use matching with decoded rs1/rs2 source-use signals so immediate and source-free instructions do not stall unnecessarily.; rtl/decoder.sv — Produce precise source-use metadata for each validated RV32I/RV32M instruction class, defaulting unused sources off.; rtl/id_stage.sv — Expose the decoder's IF/ID source-use metadata to the top-level hazard-control wiring.; rtl/core.sv — Wire source-use metadata from decode into the load-use hazard unit without changing the core IO contract.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r3s0\nReplace the four independent `/` and `%` expressions with one unsigned\ndivide/remainder datapath selected by the current signedness and remainder\noperation.  Convert signed operands to magnitudes before division, then apply\nthe architectural quotient and remainder signs; explicitly retain divide-by-\nzero and INT_MIN/-1 overflow behavior.  Keep the existing one-cycle ALU\ninterface and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.  Sharing the\ndivider logic should reduce area and routing congestion and improve Fmax\nwithout changing instruction throughput or pipeline timing.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/alu.sv — Share a single unsigned quotient/remainder datapath across DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU, with sign correction and explicit RV32M edge-case handling while preserving ALTOPS behavior.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r4s0\nRewrite the shared unsigned product expression so synthesis sees two\nexplicitly 32-bit unsigned operands while retaining the full 64-bit result\nneeded by MULH, MULHU, and the existing sign-correction equations for\nMULHSU.  Preserve the real RV32M behavior, divide paths, and the\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions exactly.  A true 32x32 implementation\nshould reduce multiplier resources and routing pressure without adding a\npipeline cycle or changing forwarding and retirement timing.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/alu.sv — Constrain the shared unsigned multiplier operands to 32 bits while preserving the full product, high-half sign corrections, ALTOPS branch, and RV32M edge behavior.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r5s0\nFactor the PC-plus-immediate result into one explicitly shared target\ndatapath and reuse it for conditional branches and direct JALs; retain a\nseparate rs1-plus-immediate path only for JALR, with bit-zero alignment\nmasking applied after target formation.  Preserve the existing branch\ncomparisons, misalignment traps, redirect priority, RVFI pc_wdata, and\npipeline latency.  Removing redundant target arithmetic should lower EX\nLUT/routing pressure and improve worst-seed Fmax without changing the\nretired instruction stream or CoreMark cycle count.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/ex_stage.sv — Explicitly share the PC-plus-immediate control-flow target between branches and direct JALs, retain correct JALR target formation and alignment-trap behavior, and keep the existing EX/MEM and redirect interfaces unchanged.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r6s0\nReplace the encoded forwarding selects with direct priority-encoded operand\nbypass values: choose EX/MEM first, MEM/WB second, and the ID/EX value last\ninside the forwarding network, then feed those values directly to EX.  Keep\nthe existing producer-valid and x0 checks, including the one-cycle load-use\ninterlock and EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority, so load, branch, and ALU\ndependencies retain identical architectural behavior.  Removing the\nselect-decode stage should reduce LUT depth and routing on the EX critical\npath without adding pipeline latency or changing CoreMark instruction\ncount.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/forward_unit.sv — Emit directly selected rs1/rs2 bypass values using the existing EX/MEM-first, MEM/WB-second dependency priority and x0 exclusions.; rtl/ex_stage.sv — Consume the direct forwarded operand values, remove the encoded-select operand muxes, and preserve all ALU, branch, jump, trap, and EX/MEM behavior.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r7s0\nReplace the variable load shift and mask shifts with explicit cases on\naddress low bits and access width.  Select the required byte or halfword\ndirectly from the returned word, and emit the equivalent constant lane\nmasks while retaining the existing sign extension, misalignment gating,\nRVFI aligned address/data fields, and zero-wait/stalled bus behavior.\nThis should shorten the memory-result path and reduce LUT/routing pressure\nwithout adding state, changing retirement timing, or altering CoreMark\ninstruction cycles.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/mem_stage.sv — Use explicit address/width lane cases for load extraction and byte masks, preserving RV32I alignment traps, sign extension, dmem outputs, and RVFI memory fields.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r8s0\nReplace the separate branch comparisons with one shared 33-bit subtract\nand derive equality, unsigned less-than, and signed less-than from its\nzero, borrow, and sign information.  Select only the resulting predicate\nfor the decoded branch operation, while preserving all six RV32I branch\nsemantics, forwarding inputs, branch-target computation, misalignment\ntraps, redirect timing, and RVFI pc_next behavior.  Sharing the compare\ndatapath should reduce EX logic and routing without adding a pipeline\nstage or changing retirement behavior.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/ex_stage.sv — Implement a shared 33-bit branch subtract/zero-detect comparator for BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU, preserving forwarding, redirect, misalignment, and RVFI behavior.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r9s0\nCompute PC+4 once while the instruction is entering ID and carry that\nvalue in the ID/EX bundle.  Use the registered value for sequential\npc_next, JAL's rd writeback value, and the sequential side of the existing\nmisalignment-trap bookkeeping, while leaving branch and JALR target\nformation, forwarding, flushes, and retirement unchanged.  Moving this\nadder out of EX should shorten the critical path with only a small amount\nof pipeline storage and no IPC or ISA change.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/core_pkg.sv — Add a PC+4 field to the ID/EX pipeline bundle so execute receives the precomputed sequential next-PC value.; rtl/id_stage.sv — Compute and register in.pc + 32'd4 alongside the decoded instruction, resetting it with the existing ID/EX bubble state.; rtl/ex_stage.sv — Replace EX-stage sequential PC and JAL return-address additions with the carried PC+4 value while preserving branch/JALR targets, traps, and RVFI pc_next behavior.\n\nhyp-20260710-001-r10s0\nReplace the monolithic 64-bit order increment with separate low and high\n32-bit counters.  On each valid retirement, increment the low half and\nincrement the high half only when the low half is 0xffffffff; expose their\nconcatenation as the unchanged 64-bit rvfi_order_0.  This should shorten\nthe common feedback path to a 32-bit carry chain and reduce RVFI-side LUT\npressure without changing retirement timing, pipeline behavior, or the\nrequired exact +1 sequence across 64-bit rollover.\n\nCHANGES: rtl/core.sv — Split rvfi_order_q into low/high 32-bit state with explicit rollover carry, preserving reset behavior and the exact 64-bit RVFI order output.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
