.sv`, add a small cold helper for\\\\nthe high-half multiply variants, reusing the existing long-op stall pattern\\\\nused by DIV/REM so those rare instructions remain architecturally correct\\\\nwithout capping global Fmax. This should preserve CoreMark IPC much better\\\\nthan making all multiply ops iterative, while still buying timing slack by\\\\ndeleting the widest multiply logic from the common EX res... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-Deep Stalled-Store Retirement Slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core already got its biggest measured win by shortening the EX hot\\\\npath with the DIV/REM side unit, and the two failed bench structural attempts\\\\nboth showed that adding more logic to IF is a bad trade on this snapshot.\\\\nThe cleaner remaining structural loss is on the data side: every\\\\n`io_dmemReady=0` cycle for a STORE still freezes the whole pipeline even\\\\nthough the store has no register result and its address, byte mask, and write\\\\ndata are already known in MEM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core already got its biggest measured win by shortening the EX hot\\\\npath with the DIV/REM side unit, and the two failed bench structural attempts\\\\nboth showed that adding more logic to IF is a bad trade on this snapshot.\\\\nThe cleaner remaining structural loss is on the data side: every\\\\n`io_dmemReady=0` cycle for a STORE still freezes the whole pipeline even\\\\nthough the store has no register result and its address, byte mask, and write\\\\ndata are already known in MEM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only for legal,\\\\naligned normal-RAM stores when they reach MEM and the external dmem port is\\\\nstalled. Keep the existing zero-wait store fast path unchanged. On a stalled\\\\nRAM store, capture the store's byte address, replicated write data, byte\\\\nmask, and RVFI-visible memory fields into the slot, retire that store once on\\\\nchannel 0, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while the slot\\\\ndrains oldest-first onto dmem. Loads, later memory operations, and all\\\\nMMIO/UART/marker accesses must still wait behind the slot so ordering,\\\\nCoreMark timing markers, and RVFI semantics stay intact. This targets the\\\\ntournament's random dStall bubbles without reopening the frontend critical\\\\npath or putting load data onto the EX operand muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"con... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny PC-Indexed Taken BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The last predictor attempt lost fitness because it lengthened the frontend\\\\ncritical path with `imem_data` decode and branch-immediate construction in IF.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core waits until EX to correct any\\\\ntaken control flow, so hot loops and repeated calls keep paying wrong-path\\\\nfetch bubbles. The most promising predictor angle is therefore one that keeps\\\\nprediction state PC-indexed and decode-free on the fetch path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The last predictor attempt lost fitness because it lengthened the frontend\\\\ncritical path with `imem_data` decode and branch-immediate construction in IF.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core waits until EX to correct any\\\\ntaken control flow, so hot loops and repeated calls keep paying wrong-path\\\\nfetch bubbles. The most promising predictor angle is therefore one that keeps\\\\nprediction state PC-indexed and decode-free on the fetch path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer that is consulted by PC\\\\nalone and predicts only previously-taken control-flow sites. IF should use a\\\\n`valid/tag/target` BTB hit to fetch the remembered target instead of `pc+4`,\\\\nwithout decoding the fetched instruction or rebuilding immediates on the hot\\\\npath. EX remains the point of truth for branch direction, JAL/JALR targets,\\\\ntraps, and RVFI; it simply compares the resolved next PC against the carried\\\\nprediction metadata, redirects on misses, and updates or invalidates the BTB\\\\nentry. This should recover much of the loop/call bubble cost while avoiding\\\\nthe timing regression seen from instruction-driven IF prediction logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with compact prediction metadata so EX can validate a BTB hit against the... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share One RV32M Multiplier Datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still pays for three parallel multiply datapaths on\\\\nevery EX cycle: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. The\\\\nlast MULH offload attempt showed that adding new long-op control can erase\\\\nthe timing win, but the existing `alu.sv` structure is still a strong Fmax\\\\nrisk because it duplicates wide multiply hardware even though CoreMark's hot\\\\nloop mostly needs plain low-half `MUL`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still pays for three parallel multiply datapaths on\\\\nevery EX cycle: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. The\\\\nlast MULH offload attempt showed that adding new long-op control can erase\\\\nthe timing win, but the existing `alu.sv` structure is still a strong Fmax\\\\nrisk because it duplicates wide multiply hardware even though CoreMark's hot\\\\nloop mostly needs plain low-half `MUL`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework `rtl/alu.sv` so all four multiply-family ops stay single-cycle, but\\\\nare implemented from one correctly-sized shared 32x32 product datapath plus\\\\nsmall sign-correction logic for the high-half variants instead of three\\\\nseparate products. This keeps the divider/store-slot wins intact, avoids new\\\\nEX stall or result-retime machinery, and attacks the same remaining RV32M\\\\ntiming cost that motivated the failed MULH split with a much lighter control\\\\nfootprint. If Yosys/nextpnr currently map the three products into duplicated\\\\nDSP or LUT fabric, collapsing them into one multiplier should improve Fmax\\\\nand area with essentially no CoreMark IPC cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the current three-parallel multiply implementation with one shared RV32M product path and signed-high correction logic, while preserving exact MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU results and the existing ALTO... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-Deep RAM Store Retirement Queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The first MEM-side store slot already proved that this snapshot gains more\\\\nfrom data-side decoupling than from extra frontend logic. The remaining\\\\nstructural bottleneck is that the current one-entry pending-store design\\\\nstill serializes every second normal-RAM store behind the first pending\\\\none, even though both stores already have fully computed address, mask, and\\\\nwrite data and neither produces a register result.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The first MEM-side store slot already proved that this snapshot gains more\\\\nfrom data-side decoupling than from extra frontend logic. The remaining\\\\nstructural bottleneck is that the current one-entry pending-store design\\\\nstill serializes every second normal-RAM store behind the first pending\\\\none, even though both stores already have fully computed address, mask, and\\\\nwrite data and neither produces a register result.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-entry pending-store slot in `mem_stage.sv` with a tiny\\\\ntwo-entry oldest-first queue for legal, aligned normal-RAM stores. Keep the\\\\nzero-wait store fast path unchanged, retire a store once when it is\\\\nenqueued, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while one or two\\\\nqueued stores drain onto dmem. Loads, MMIO/marker accesses, and any later\\\\nmemory op must still wait behind the queue for ordering and RVFI\\\\ncorrectness. This extends the only structural direction that has already\\\\nimproved fitness, while staying off the timing-sensitive IF and EX paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single pending-store slot with a two-entry FIFO of buffered store metadata plus enqueue, drain, and full/empty control, while keeping buffered retirement restricted to legal aligned normal-RAM stores and preserving strict ordering for loads and MMIO.\\\"}... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-Entry Loop Target Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core still pays a full control-flow bubble whenever a hot loop\\\\nbackedge is taken, but the two prior predictor attempts showed that this\\\\ndesign cannot afford much extra logic on the fetch critical path. The next\\\\npredictor should therefore recover only the highest-value target predictions\\\\nwhile keeping IF hardware smaller than both an instruction-driven predictor\\\\nand a multi-entry BTB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core still pays a full control-flow bubble whenever a hot loop\\\\nbackedge is taken, but the two prior predictor attempts showed that this\\\\ndesign cannot afford much extra logic on the fetch critical path. The next\\\\npredictor should therefore recover only the highest-value target predictions\\\\nwhile keeping IF hardware smaller than both an instruction-driven predictor\\\\nand a multi-entry BTB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry loop-target predictor that remembers only the most recent\\\\ntaken backward branch and predicts again only on an exact PC match. IF would\\\\nconsult one registered `{valid, branch_pc, target_pc}` entry and choose the\\\\nremembered target instead of `pc+4` on a hit, with no `imem_data` decode,\\\\nimmediate construction, or table RAM lookup on the hot path. EX remains the\\\\npoint of truth: it validates the carried prediction, redirects only on\\\\nmispredicts, and updates or invalidates the single entry when a backward\\\\nbranch is taken or falls through. This keeps the predictor focused on\\\\nCoreMark's dominant tight loops while making the IF cost much smaller than\\\\nthe previously-regressing BTB approach.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with compact prediction metadata so EX can validate a loop-target hit against the resolved next PC.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n\\n(End of file - total 10 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"{\\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline-bench-a2dc641\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Baseline retest for cores/bench\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"improvement\\\", \\\"fitness\\\": 282.82, \\\"delta_pct\\\": 0.0, \\\"vs_baseline\\\": 0.0, \\\"fmax_mhz\\\": 127.03, \\\"ipc_coremark\\\": 2e-06, \\\"cycles\\\": 4491485, \\\"iterations\\\": 10, \\\"lut4\\\": 9563, \\\"ff\\\": 1866, \\\"seeds\\\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \\\"formal_passed\\\": true, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": true, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"implementation_notes\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"2026-05-15T01:37:19.468113Z\\\", \\\"round_id\\\": 0, \\\"slot\\\": 0}\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Move DIV/REM Off The ALU Critical Path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU is fully combinational and includes RV32M divide\\\\nand remainder operators in its worst-case path. On the Tang Nano 20K that\\\\nlogic is likely much slower than the add/shift/branch hot path, yet\\\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by list/state/matrix work and appears\\\\nto use division only in cold setup code. If the divider stops limiting Fmax,\\\\na small stall cost on rare DIV/REM instructions should buy a net fitness\\\\ngain.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU is fully combinational and includes RV32M divide\\\\nand remainder operators in its worst-case path. On the Tang Nano 20K that\\\\nlogic is likely much slower than the add/shift/branch hot path, yet\\\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by list/state/matrix work and appears\\\\nto use division only in cold setup code. If the divider stops limiting Fmax,\\\\na small stall cost on rare DIV/REM instructions should buy a net fitness\\\\ngain.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-cycle combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU logic with a small\\\\niterative divider in EX, while keeping ADD/logic/shift/branch and the\\\\nmultiply family on the existing fast path. EX would assert a busy condition\\\\nonly for divide/remainder ops, hold younger stages until the result is ready,\\\\nthen release a single normal retirement with the same RV32M corner-case and\\\\nRVFI semantics. This should shorten the worst timing path, trim LUT pressure,\\\\nand preserve CoreMark/MHz because the hot benchmark loop is not divider\\\\nheavy.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove DIV/REM from the always-hot combinational ALU path while keeping fast ALU ops and MUL-family behavior unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an iterative divider path in EX and hold EX/MEM advance until DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU com... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Stall-Only Fetch Replay Buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core turns every random `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a frontend\\\\nbubble even when the same hot-loop PC has already been fetched many times.\\\\nUnder the tournament's fixed ~22% instruction-stall model, recovering even a\\\\nfraction of those lost fetches should raise iterations/cycle without\\\\nwidening retirement or touching the already timing-sensitive EX datapath.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core turns every random `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a frontend\\\\nbubble even when the same hot-loop PC has already been fetched many times.\\\\nUnder the tournament's fixed ~22% instruction-stall model, recovering even a\\\\nfraction of those lost fetches should raise iterations/cycle without\\\\nwidening retirement or touching the already timing-sensitive EX datapath.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny direct-mapped instruction replay buffer in IF that records\\\\n`{pc, instr}` on successful live fetches and is consulted only when the\\\\nexternal imem port is not ready. On an imem-stall hit, IF should supply the\\\\ncached instruction and assert an effective fetch-ready signal so decode\\\\nadvances instead of seeing a forced bubble. Keep the zero-wait path direct\\\\nwhen `io_imemReady=1`, and preserve the current redirect, reset, and RVFI\\\\nretirement behavior. This targets the benchmark's synthetic iStall bubbles\\\\ndirectly while avoiding new pressure on the ALU, load-use path, or dmem\\\\ntiming.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a small stall-only instruction replay table keyed by fetch PC, update it on successful live fetches, and return cached instructions on imem-stall hits while preserving the current direct fast path and redirect/flush semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Expose an IF-stage fetch-available signal to the top level and route it into hazar... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static Backward-Branch IF Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core resolves every conditional branch in EX and always\\\\nfetches `pc+4` until that point, so taken loop branches pay a two-bubble\\\\nwrong-path recovery cost. CoreMark spends much of its timed region in short\\\\nbackward loops, making a tiny static branch predictor a strong IPC lever\\\\nwithout the LUT and routing cost of a learned table, BTB, or return stack.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core resolves every conditional branch in EX and always\\\\nfetches `pc+4` until that point, so taken loop branches pay a two-bubble\\\\nwrong-path recovery cost. CoreMark spends much of its timed region in short\\\\nbackward loops, making a tiny static branch predictor a strong IPC lever\\\\nwithout the LUT and routing cost of a learned table, BTB, or return stack.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-light IF-stage predictor that recognizes legal B-type branches\\\\nand predicts backward branches taken when the branch immediate is negative.\\\\nIF computes the B immediate and predicted target, carries\\\\n`predicted_taken/predicted_target` through the existing pipeline metadata,\\\\nand EX redirects only when the resolved branch next PC disagrees with that\\\\nprediction. JAL and JALR stay on the current EX redirect path so the change\\\\nstays focused on the hottest control-flow class while keeping timing and LUT\\\\ngrowth small.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend `if_id_t` and `id_ex_t` with `predicted_taken` and `predicted_target` so IF can pass branch-prediction metadata into EX for validation.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add decode-light backward-branch prediction in fetch: recognize legal B-type encodings, form the branch immediate and target, and choose `pc+imm` instead of `pc+4` for predicted-taken backward branches when the target is wor... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Move Cold MULH Ops Off The EX Hot Path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still computes three separate sign/zero-extended\\\\nmultiply products in the one-cycle EX datapath, and each product is written\\\\nas a 64-bit-by-64-bit expression even though the ISA operands are only 32\\\\nbits wide. That is a strong candidate for the remaining Fmax limiter after\\\\nDIV/REM was moved out of the hot path. CoreMark's timed region is multiply\\\\nheavy because of the matrix kernel, but it overwhelmingly cares about plain\\\\nlow-half `MUL`; the high-half multiply variants are correctness-critical yet\\\\nbenchmark-cold.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still computes three separate sign/zero-extended\\\\nmultiply products in the one-cycle EX datapath, an