title\\\": \\\"Elastic Fetch Skid Buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The baseline frontend has no real IF/ID storage: instruction data flows\\\\ndirectly from io_imemData into decode, and hazard_unit globally freezes the\\\\nPC on load-use, dmem stalls, and imem stalls. Under the tournament's ~22%\\\\nrandom imem backpressure, every not-ready fetch becomes a visible decode\\\\nbubble. Under dmem backpressure, the independent imem port is also idle even\\\\nthough it could be fetching sequential instructions for after the stall.\\\\nCoreMark's IPC is already close to the single-issue limit, so the best\\\\nstructural win is to recover wasted frontend cycles without widening the\\\\ncore or lengthening the EX critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the combinational IF/ID handoff with an elastic 2-entry fetch skid\\\\nbuffer that decouples instruction fetch from decode. The fetch side advances\\\\nthe PC and captures sequential instructions whenever io_imemReady is high\\\\nand the buffer is not full, even while ID/EX is held by a dmem stall. The\\\\ndecode side consumes one buffered instruction per non-stalled cycle. Preserve\\\\nthe baseline zero-latency behavior with an empty-buffer bypass from\\\\nio_imemData to decode when the backend is ready and imem is ready, so the\\\\nno-stall path and branch target... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260427-003-r1s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Backward-Branch Taken Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core resolves all branches and jumps in EX and fetches strictly pc+4 until that resolution, so every taken loop branch in CoreMark pays a two-instruction wrong-path flush. CoreMark's hot loops are dominated by short backward conditional branches whose direction is highly biased toward taken. A small static predictor can remove most loop-back redirect bubbles without adding a learned-state table, RAM, or speculative architectural state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a decode-light static branch predictor in IF that recognizes B-type instructions and predicts backward branches taken when the sign bit of the branch immediate is set. Compute the B immediate in IF, redirect the next PC to pc+imm for predicted-taken backward branches, and carry predicted_taken plus predicted_target through IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX, compare the actual branch result and target against the prediction, redirect only on mispredict, and set rvfi pc_wdata to the actual next PC as today. JAL/JALR remain resolved in EX to keep the predictor slot focused on conditional branches and avoid adding a return-address stack or BTB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8.0, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline bundles with predicted_taken and predicted_target fields so EX can distinguish correct predictions f... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260427-004-r2s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Single Shared RV32M Multiplier\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current ALU builds three independent wide products every cycle:\\\\nsigned*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. Those products feed\\\\nthe same EX-stage result mux as ordinary integer operations, so their area and\\\\nrouting pressure can lower Fmax even when most instructions are ADD/logic,\\\\nbranches, loads, and stores. CoreMark does use MUL, so making multiplication\\\\nmulti-cycle risks losing IPC; however all RV32M multiply variants can be\\\\nimplemented by one signedness-controlled 33x33 product with no latency change.\\\\nThis is a narrower and lower-risk micro-optimization than the prior failed\\\\nDIV/REM interlock attempt because it leaves divider behavior, pipeline stalls,\\\\nand RVFI ordering untouched.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the three unconditional multiplier expressions in rtl/alu.sv with a\\\\nsingle shared 33-bit-by-33-bit product. For MUL, zero-extend both operands and\\\\nuse the low 32 bits; for MULH, sign-extend both operands; for MULHSU,\\\\nsign-extend rs1 and zero-extend rs2; for MULHU, zero-extend both operands.\\\\nSelect product[63:32] for the high-half operations and product[31:0] for MUL.\\\\nKeep the RISC... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260427-005-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"MEM-to-EX Load-Use Bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage pipeline still has the textbook structural load-use gap: a load in EX always forces the immediately following consumer to wait one extra cycle, even when the dmem bus is ready and the loaded word is already available combinationally in MEM. CoreMark's list, matrix, and state kernels contain many load-to-use pairs, so this fixed bubble directly lowers iterations per cycle. The recent predictor win improved control flow without changing the backend; the next most promising structural angle is to add the missing MEM-to-EX load-data path and remove the mandatory interlock while preserving the single-issue in-order retirement contract.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a direct load-result forwarding path from the MEM stage's existing sign/zero-extended load_data to the EX operand muxes. When an instruction in EX consumes the rd of a load currently in MEM, select this MEM load result for rs1 and/or rs2 instead of stalling for the value to appear in MEM/WB. If io_dmemReady is low for that load, keep the current dmem hold behavior and suppress any younger EX redirect until the load data is valid; once ready, the dependent instruction executes in the same cycle the load ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260427-006-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"IF Direct-Jump Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best RTL already predicts backward conditional branches in IF,\\\\nbut ex_stage still asserts a redirect for every JAL/JALR. That leaves\\\\nunconditional direct jumps, calls, and compiler-generated `j` control-flow\\\\nskips paying the same wrong-path flush as an unpredicted taken branch, even\\\\nthough a JAL target is fully encoded in the fetched instruction. CoreMark's\\\\noptimized RV32IM image contains many direct JAL/J sites, so eliminating those\\\\nredirect bubbles should improve iterations/cycle with much less timing risk\\\\nthan adding a BTB or learned direction table to the IF path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing IF-stage predictor from backward conditional branches to\\\\ndirect JAL targets. Recognize opcode 1101111 in IF, form the J immediate,\\\\npredict pc+imm as the next PC when the target is word-aligned, and carry the\\\\nexisting predicted_taken/predicted_target metadata with the instruction. In\\\\nEX, make JAL redirect mispredict-only by comparing the resolved jump target\\\\nagainst the carried prediction; JALR remains unpredicted and still redirects\\\\nfrom EX. This should remove the two-cycle recovery penalty from correctly\\\\npredicted direct jumps and calls while preserving RVFI pc_wdata, misaligned\\\\njump traps, and the single-retire c... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Operand-Aware Load-Use Interlock\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use hazard check is deliberately conservative: every load\\\\nin ID/EX stalls the next instruction when the load destination matches either\\\\nraw rs1 or raw rs2 bitfield in IF/ID. That creates real bubbles for true\\\\nload consumers, but it also creates false bubbles for common CoreMark\\\\ninstructions whose rs fields are immediates or destinations rather than\\\\nsource operands, especially OP-IMM shifts/arithmetic, LOAD, JAL, LUI, and\\\\nAUIPC. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass recovered many load-use cycles\\\\nbut cost too much Fmax because it added a new MEM-data path into EX. A\\\\ndecode-light operand-use filter should recover the false-stall subset with\\\\nalmost no new datapath depth and no change to branch prediction, memory\\\\ntiming, or RVFI retirement.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Refine the hazard_unit load-use predicate so it only compares the load rd\\\\nagainst source fields that the IF/ID instruction actually reads. Compute\\\\nsimple uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits from the IF/ID opcode: rs1 is used by\\\\nOP, OP-IMM, LOAD, STORE, BRANCH, and JALR; rs2 is used only by... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-Entry Retiring Store Buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core treats every dmem-not-ready cycle on a store as a\\\\nfull pipeline freeze, even though a store's data and byte mask are already\\\\nknown in MEM and no register result is needed by younger ALU/control\\\\ninstructions. With the tournament's random dmem stalls, this turns store bus\\\\nbackpressure into lost frontend and execute bandwidth. The prior MEM-to-EX\\\\nload-use bypass proved that backend bubbles matter, but it put dmem read data\\\\non the EX operand critical path and lost too much Fmax. A small in-order store\\\\nbuffer targets the store-only part of the same structural bottleneck while\\\\nkeeping load data, branch compare, and ALU timing out of the new path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a two-entry FIFO for normal RAM stores. When a legal aligned store to the\\\\nRAM address range reaches MEM and the external dmem port is not immediately\\\\navailable, retire the store into the FIFO with its address, replicated write\\\\ndata, byte mask, and RVFI metadata, then let younger non-memory instructions\\\\ncontinue through the pipeline while the FIFO drains oldest-first on the dmem\\\\nport. Loads, stores when the FIFO is full, and all MMIO/marker/UART st... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static-Biased Branch Agree Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best frontend already removes most obvious control bubbles by\\\\npredicting backward conditional branches taken and direct JAL targets in IF.\\\\nThe remaining predictor loss is now dominated by conditional branches that\\\\nstatic BTFNT handles poorly: biased forward branches, backward branches with\\\\nnon-loop behavior, and alias-heavy branch sites in CoreMark's list and state\\\\nkernels. A small learned direction predictor can attack those residual\\\\nmispredicts without adding a BTB, a target adder beyond the existing branch\\\\nimmediate path, or any speculative architectural state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a 64-entry, PC-indexed, two-bit \\\\\\\"agree\\\\\\\" predictor for conditional branch\\\\ndirection. IF still computes the existing static BTFNT direction and branch\\\\ntarget from the fetched instruction; the table counter predicts whether the\\\\ndynamic outcome will agree with that static direction. Initialize counters to\\\\nstrongly agree so cold branches behave exactly like the current predictor.\\\\nFor legal aligned B-type branches, predict taken when the table prediction\\\\ncombined with the static direction says taken, and carry the existing\\\\npredicted_taken/predicted_target metadata into EX. Direct JAL prediction\\\\nremains unchanged, and JALR remains ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-004-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Hot-Bit ALU Result Mux\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core is now Fmax-sensitive in the EX-stage ALU path. The\\\\nALU operation encoding is dense across both common RV32I operations and the\\\\nRV32M operations, and rtl/alu.sv selects every result through one broad case\\\\nstatement. Recent experiments showed that touching the multiplier primitive\\\\nitself is risky: the 33x33 shared-multiplier rewrite preserved IPC but badly\\\\nhurt Fmax. A safer micro-optimization is to leave every arithmetic primitive\\\\nand pipeline handshake unchanged, but make the ALU control encoding expose a\\\\nhot/cold split so synthesis can build a shallower result mux for the common\\\\ninteger/address/branch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core is now Fmax-sensitive in the EX-stage ALU path. The\\\\nALU operation encoding is dense across both common RV32I operations and the\\\\nRV32M operations, and rtl/alu.sv selects every result through one broad case\\\\nstatement. Recent experiments showed that touching the multiplier primitive\\\\nitself is risky: the 33x33 shared-multiplier rewrite preserved IPC but badly\\\\nhurt Fmax. A safer micro-optimization is to leave every arithmetic primitive\\\\nand pipeline handshake unchanged, but make the ALU control encoding expose a\\\\nhot/cold split so synthesis can build a shallower result mux for the common\\\\ninteger/address/branch work.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Re-encode ALU op constants so bit 4 is a registered \\\\\\\"M-extension operation\\\\\\\"\\\\ndiscriminator: all RV32I/simple operations keep op[4]=0, while MUL/MULH/\\\\nMULHSU/MULHU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU move to op[4]=1 with op[2:0] matching the\\\\nRV32M funct3 order. Refactor rtl/alu.sv into a hot simple-result mux for\\\\nADD/SUB/logic/compare/shift/LUI and a separate M-result mux for the existing\\\\nmultiply/divide/remainder expressions, then select between them with op[4].\\\\nThis should reduce LUT depth and routing on the common EX/MEM alu_result\\\\npath without cha... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-005-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-Stage JALR Redirect\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core predicts backward conditional branches and direct JALs\\\\nin IF, but JALR remains structurally resolved only in EX. Every return and\\\\nindirect jump therefore still injects the same wrong-path recovery bubbles\\\\nthat direct-JAL prediction just removed. CoreMark's timed path has many\\\\ncall/return edges around the list, matrix, state, CRC, and benchmark harness\\\\nroutines, so unresolved returns are now one of the cleaner remaining control\\\\nbubbles. A small decode-stage JALR resolver targets that specific structural\\\\nlatency without adding a predictor table, widening retirement, or putting\\\\nload data on the EX operand path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add an early JALR redirect path in ID for cases where the rs1 base is already\\\\navailable from the regfile or same-cycle WB write-first bypass. ID computes\\\\nthe JALR target from rs1 plus the I-immediate, verifies the aligned target,\\\\nand sends a PC redirect to IF in the same cycle the JALR enters ID/EX. The\\\\nID/EX prediction metadata is overwritten with that resolved target so the\\\\nlater EX-stage instance of the same JALR does not redirect a second time.\\\\nWhen rs1 is waiting on an older instruction in ID/EX or EX/MEM, or when the\\\\nbackend is ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-006-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Return-Address Stack JALR Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, which removes most loop-back and direct-call\\\\ncontrol bubbles. The remaining obvious predictor hole is JALR: every return\\\\nstill resolves in EX and forces the same wrong-path flush even though CoreMark\\\\nuses direct calls paired with register-indirect returns. A tiny return-address\\\\nstack targets that specific loss with lower timing risk than another indexed\\\\nbranch table, because IF only needs to recognize the canonical return\\\\nencoding and mux in a registered stack top.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, which removes most loop-back and direct-call\\\\ncontrol bubbles. The remaining obvious predictor hole is JALR: every return\\\\nstill resolves in EX and forces the same wrong-path flush even though CoreMark\\\\nuses direct calls paired with register-indirect returns. A tiny return-address\\\\nstack targets that specific loss with lower timing risk than another indexed\\\\nbranch table, because IF only needs to recognize the canonical return\\\\nencoding and mux in a registered stack top.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a small non-speculative return-address stack for link-register control\\\\nflow. When a valid non-trapping JAL or JALR call with rd=x1 or rd=x5 advances\\\\nthrough EX, push pc+4 into a 4-entry stack. When a valid canonical return\\\\nJALR with rd=x0, rs1=x1 or rs1=x5, funct3=000, and imm=0 advances through EX,\\\\npop one entry. In IF, recognize the same canonical return instruction and,\\\\nwhen the stack is non-empty, predict the next PC as the stack top using the\\\\nexisting predicted_taken/predicted_target metadata path. Change JALR redirect\\\\ngeneration in EX to the same resolved-next-PC mismatch check used for\\\\npredicted JALs: unpredicted or incorrectly predicted JA... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-007-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Explicit MEM Byte-Lane Steering\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best RTL still leaves a small but likely timing-relevant barrel\\\\nshifter in the MEM stage: load data is selected with `dmem_rdata >>\\\\n(addr[1:0] * 8)`, and store byte masks are formed with variable shifts.\\\\nOn an FPGA this is usually inferred as wider mux/shifter logic than needed,\\\\neven though RV32I memory accesses only need four fixed byte lanes and two\\\\nlegal halfword lanes. CoreMark is load/store heavy, and the dmem read-data\\\\nto MEM/WB path can compete with the EX ALU path for Fmax. A lane-case rewrite\\\\nis a narrow micro-optimization that should not affect IPC, branch prediction,\\\\nmemory ordering, RVFI retirement, or any RV32IM arithmetic behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best RTL still leaves a small but likely timing-relevant barrel\\\\nshifter in the MEM stage: load data is selected with `dmem_rdata >>\\\\n(addr[1:0] * 8)`, and store byte masks are formed with variable shifts.\\\\nOn an FPGA this is usually inferred as wider mux/shifter logic than needed,\\\\neven though RV32I memory accesses only need four fixed byte lanes and two\\\\nlegal halfword lanes. CoreMark is load/store heavy, and the dmem read-data\\\\nto MEM/WB path can compete with the EX ALU path for Fmax. A lane-case rewrite\\\\nis a narrow micro-optimization that should not affect IPC, branch prediction,\\\\nmemory ordering, RVFI retirement, or any RV32IM arithmetic behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite `rtl/mem_stage.sv` to decode `in.alu_result[1:0]` explicitly for\\\\nbyte/halfword steering instead of using variable shifts. Build `byte_val`,\\\\n`hword_val`, `byte_mask`, and replicated store data from small `case` tables\\\\nover the low address bits and access width. Preserve the existing misalignment\\\\ntrap rules, dmem gating on traps, RVFI aligned `mem_addr`, raw `mem_rdata`,\\\\nreplicated `mem_wdata`, and exact load sign/zero extension semantics. The\\\\nexpected gain is a mode... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-008-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"MEM-Stage Store-Data Forwarding\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current five-stage core still treats every immediate load-use match as a\\\\nfull one-cycle interlock, including the common case where the younger\\\\ninstruction is a STORE that only needs the loaded register as store data\\\\n(rs2), not as the address base (rs1). That bubble is structurally avoidable:\\\\nstore data is not consumed by the EX ALU, and the actual dmem write data is\\\\nformed one stage later in MEM. The prior full MEM-to-EX load bypass showed\\\\nthat CoreMark has meaningful load-use pressure, but it lost too much Fmax by\\\\nrouting dmem load data into the EX operand muxes. A late store-data bypass\\\\ntargets a useful subset of those bubbles while keeping load data off the ALU,\\\\nbranch compare, and JALR critical paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Allow a legal STORE immediately after a LOAD to proceed without the normal\\\\nload-use bubble when the load destination matches only the STORE rs2 data\\\\nfield and not the STORE rs1 address field. The store computes its address in\\\\nEX as usual, carries its rs2 register number into MEM, and selects the final\\\\nstore data in MEM from the MEM/WB writeback value when that older i... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-009-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny Forward-Branch Override Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, reaching 324.48 fitness. The 64-entry agree\\\\npredictor experiment showed that residual conditional-branch misses still\\\\nmatter: it reduced CoreMark cycles from 4,259,699 to 4,194,484, but the\\\\nlarger always-consulted table cost enough Fmax to regress overall fitness.\\\\nThat result points to a narrower predictor: keep the proven BTFNT and direct\\\\nJAL behavior intact, but learn only the static predictor's most expensive\\\\nblind spot, biased forward conditional branches. A tiny forward-only override\\\\nshould retain much of the cycle-count gain with far less table area, fanout,\\\\nand IF routing pressure than the full agree predictor.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best frontend already predicts backward conditional branches and\\\\ndirect JAL targets in IF, reaching 324.48 fitness. The 64-entry agree\\\\npredictor experiment showed that residual conditional-branch misses still\\\\nmatter: it reduced CoreMark cycles from 4,259,699 to 4,194,484, but the\\\\nlarger always-consulted table cost enough Fmax to regress overall fitness.\\\\nThat result points to a narrower predictor: keep the proven BTFNT and direct\\\\nJAL behavior intact, but learn only the static predictor's most expensive\\\\nblind spot, biased forward conditional branches. A tiny forward-only override\\\\nshould retain much of the cycle-count gain with far less table area, fanout,\\\\nand IF routing pressure than the full agree predictor.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a 16-entry PC-indexed two-bit saturating override table that is consulted\\\\nonly for legal, aligned, forward B-type conditional branches. Backward\\\\nbranches keep the current static taken prediction, and direct JAL prediction\\\\nremains unchanged. Forward branches cold-start as strongly not-taken so the\\\\nreset behavior exactly matches the current... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-010-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Cold Multi-Cycle DIV/REM Unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\\\ncombinational EX-stage ALU result path as ADD, address generation, branch\\\\ncompare support, and MUL. The generated FPGA critical paths are dominated by\\\\nlong EX-stage carry/mux structures, while the CoreMark timed loop is\\\\neffectively division-cold: the static binary has only a few DIVU sites, and\\\\nthose are in setup/printing paths rather than the hot matrix/list/state\\\\niteration body. Prior multiplier and MEM-lane rewrites showed that reshaping\\\\nalready-mapped hot datapaths can badly hurt placement; the better\\\\nmicro-optimization is to remove the cold divider/remainder hardware from the\\\\none-cycle ALU path entirely while leaving multiply and all RV32I operations\\\\nsingle-cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core still keeps DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU in the same\\\\ncombinational EX-stage ALU result path as ADD, address generation, branch\\\\ncompare support, and MUL. The generated FPGA critical paths are dominated by\\\\nlong EX-stage carry/mux structures, while the CoreMark timed loop is\\\\neffectively division-cold: the static binary has only a few DIVU sites, and\\\\nthose are in setup/printing paths rather than the hot matrix/list/state\\\\niteration body. Prior multiplier and MEM-lane rewrites showed that reshaping\\\\nalready-mapped hot datapaths can badly hurt placement; the better\\\\nmicro-optimization is to remove the cold divider/remainder hardware from the\\\\none-cycle ALU path entirely while leaving multiply and all RV32I operations\\\\nsingle-cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the real combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases with a small\\\\niterative divider/remainder unit that is only entered for those four ALU\\\\nopcodes. Normal RV32I operations, branches, jumps, loads/stores, and all MUL\\\\nvariants remain one-cycle and keep the current forwarding behavior. When a\\\\ndivide-class instruction reaches... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-011-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Registered Fetch/Decode Cutpoint\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best core still has no real IF/ID register: the PC drives imem, the returned instruction is decoded, the register file is read, and the ID/EX register captures the result in one cycle. That preserves a short frontend latency, but it also leaves a large structural timing path through instruction memory data, IF prediction decode, full instruction decode, immediate generation, and regfile read fanout. Prior elastic-fetch work kept an empty-buffer bypass to avoid latency, so it retained much of this path and lost Fmax. A cleaner structural bet is to deliberately insert the missing fetch/decode cutpoint and trade one extra frontend pipeline stage for a higher median FPGA clock.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Convert the IF/ID handoff into a real registered pipeline boundary with no zero-latency imem-data-to-decode bypass. IF captures {pc, instr, predicted_taken, predicted_target, valid} when io_imemReady is high and the decode side can accept an entry; ID decodes only the registered entry on the following cycle. Redirects and reset flush the IF/ID register and restart fetch at the resolved target, while load-use and dmem stalls hold the re... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-012-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260428-012-r6s2.yaml\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"broken\\\", \\\"formal_passed\\\": false, \\\"cosim_passed\\\": false, \\\"error\\\": \\\"schema_error: 1.5 is not of type 'integer'\\\\n\\\\nFailed validating 'type' in schema['properties']['expected_impact']['properties']['fitness_delta_pct']:\\\\n    {'type': 'integer'}\\\\n\\\\nOn instance['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']:\\\\n    1.5\\\", \\\"slot\\\": 2, \\\"round_id\\\": 6, \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best predictor stack is static backward-branch taken plus IF\\\\ndirect-JAL prediction. Attempts to add broader learned conditional-branch\\\\npredictors improved CoreMark cycle count but lost more Fmax than they gained,\\\\nwhile the 4-entry RAS also regressed because it added stack state, push/pop\\\\nbookkeeping, and enough IF routing to lower median Fmax. The useful signal\\\\nfrom the RAS experiment is that returns exist in the timed path, but the\\\\npredictor must be much narrower and cheaper than a general stack.\\\\n\\\\nA one-entry last-return target predictor targets the same remaining JALR\\\\nhole with less state and less control. CoreMark's hot call/return behavior is\\\\ntypically dominated by repeated returns from stable call sites, so a single\\\\ncached canonical-return target can remove many return flushes without a BTB,\\\\nbranch table, multi-entry stack, or speculative architectural state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry return target cache in IF. When EX resolves a valid,\\\\nnon-trapping canonical return JALR (`jalr x0, 0(x1)` or `jalr x0, 0(x5)`),\\\\nrecord the return instruction PC and its resolved next PC. When IF later\\\\nfetches the same canonical return PC and the entry is valid, predict the\\\\ncached target through the existing `predicted_taken` / `predicted_target`\\\\nmetadata path. Change JALR redirect generation to the same resolved-next-PC\\\\nmismatch check used for predicted JALs, so correctly predicted cached returns\\\\ndo not flush and all other JALRs retain the current EX re... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260428-013-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Cold High-Half Multiply Unit\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best result came from moving cold DIV/REM hardware out of the\\\\nuniversal EX-stage ALU path: CoreMark cycles barely changed, while median\\\\nFmax rose sharply and LUT pressure dropped. The same shape remains in the\\\\nmultiply path, but with a narrower target. `rtl/alu.sv` still builds three\\\\nindependent 64-bit products every cycle for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\\\nCoreMark's timed kernels need ordinary low-half MUL, but high-half multiply\\\\nvariants are normally absent or cold in this benchmark. The previous 33x33\\\\nshared-multiplier rewrite proved that reshaping the hot MUL primitive itself\\\\ncan badly hurt placement, so the better micro-optimization is to leave the\\\\nexisting low-half MUL fast path intact and remove only the cold high-half\\\\nmultiplier variants from the one-cycle ALU.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current best result came from moving cold DIV/REM hardware out of the\\\\nuniversal EX-stage ALU path: CoreMark cycles barely changed, while median\\\\nFmax rose sharply and LUT pressure dropped. The same shape remains in the\\\\nmultiply path, but with a narrower target. `rtl/alu.sv` still builds three\\\\nindependent 64-bit products every cycle for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\\\nCoreMark's timed kernels need ordinary low-half MUL, but high-half multiply\\\\nvariants are normally absent or cold in this benchmark. The previous 33x33\\\\nshared-multiplier rewrite proved that reshaping the hot MUL primitive itself\\\\ncan badly hurt placement, so the better micro-optimization is to leave the\\\\nexisting low-half MUL fast path intact and remove only the cold high-half\\\\nmultiplier variants from the one-cycle ALU.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep ALU_MUL as the only single-cycle hardware multiply in the hot ALU path,\\\\nusing the existing unsigned 32x32 product low half. Move MULH, MULHU, and\\\\nMULHSU into a small long-latency `mulh_unit` launched only for those three\\\\no... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\",\"truncated\":true,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778810667593,\"end\":1778810667607}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_06702f48793617f6016a067f2a3a7c819b9391d3355dfd87fd\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2960bea6001aUXX437xuitCws\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d6a0e847ffeDUqiATilNC60cC\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e29608bd3001CK1a3ykLdtvrHP\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep1/agent.log:253:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778810757473,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d6a0e847ffeDUqiATilNC60cC\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_meHa8K612q5IJtfrbEM3gzE9\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.a