/REM was moved out of the hot path. CoreMark's timed region is multiply\\\\nheavy because of the matrix kernel, but it overwhelmingly cares about plain\\\\nlow-half `MUL`; the high-half multiply variants are correctness-critical yet\\\\nbenchmark-cold.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep plain `MUL` as the hot single-cycle multiply, but remove `MULH`,\\\\n`MULHU`, and `MULHSU` from the always-hot combinational ALU path. In\\\\n`rtl/alu.sv`, collapse the current triple-product implementation so the fast\\\\npath only needs one correctly-sized low-half multiply for `MUL` plus the\\\\nexisting simple ALU ops. In `rtl/ex_stage.sv`, add a small cold helper for\\\\nthe high-half multiply variants, reusing the existing long-op stall pattern\\\\nused by DIV/REM so those rare instructions remain architecturally correct\\\\nwithout capping global Fmax. This should preserve CoreMark IPC much better\\\\nthan making all multiply ops iterative, while still buying timing slack by\\\\ndeleting the widest multiply logic from the common EX res... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-Deep Stalled-Store Retirement Slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core already got its biggest measured win by shortening the EX hot\\\\npath with the DIV/REM side unit, and the two failed bench structural attempts\\\\nboth showed that adding more logic to IF is a bad trade on this snapshot.\\\\nThe cleaner remaining structural loss is on the data side: every\\\\n`io_dmemReady=0` cycle for a STORE still freezes the whole pipeline even\\\\nthough the store has no register result and its address, byte mask, and write\\\\ndata are already known in MEM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core already got its biggest measured win by shortening the EX hot\\\\npath with the DIV/REM side unit, and the two failed bench structural attempts\\\\nboth showed that adding more logic to IF is a bad trade on this snapshot.\\\\nThe cleaner remaining structural loss is on the data side: every\\\\n`io_dmemReady=0` cycle for a STORE still freezes the whole pipeline even\\\\nthough the store has no register result and its address, byte mask, and write\\\\ndata are already known in MEM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only for legal,\\\\naligned normal-RAM stores when they reach MEM and the external dmem port is\\\\nstalled. Keep the existing zero-wait store fast path unchanged. On a stalled\\\\nRAM store, capture the store's byte address, replicated write data, byte\\\\nmask, and RVFI-visible memory fields into the slot, retire that store once on\\\\nchannel 0, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while the slot\\\\ndrains oldest-first onto dmem. Loads, later memory operations, and all\\\\nMMIO/UART/marker accesses must still wait behind the slot so ordering,\\\\nCoreMark timing markers, and RVFI semantics stay intact. This targets the\\\\ntournament's random dStall bubbles without reopening the frontend critical\\\\npath or putting load data onto the EX operand muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"con... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n{\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny PC-Indexed Taken BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The last predictor attempt lost fitness because it lengthened the frontend\\\\ncritical path with `imem_data` decode and branch-immediate construction in IF.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core waits until EX to correct any\\\\ntaken control flow, so hot loops and repeated calls keep paying wrong-path\\\\nfetch bubbles. The most promising predictor angle is therefore one that keeps\\\\nprediction state PC-indexed and decode-free on the fetch path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The last predictor attempt lost fitness because it lengthened the frontend\\\\ncritical path with `imem_data` decode and branch-immediate construction in IF.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core waits until EX to correct any\\\\ntaken control flow, so hot loops and repeated calls keep paying wrong-path\\\\nfetch bubbles. The most promising predictor angle is therefore one that keeps\\\\nprediction state PC-indexed and decode-free on the fetch path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer that is consulted by PC\\\\nalone and predicts only previously-taken control-flow sites. IF should use a\\\\n`valid/tag/target` BTB hit to fetch the remembered target instead of `pc+4`,\\\\nwithout decoding the fetched instruction or rebuilding immediates on the hot\\\\npath. EX remains the point of truth for branch direction, JAL/JALR targets,\\\\ntraps, and RVFI; it simply compares the resolved next PC against the carried\\\\nprediction metadata, redirects on misses, and updates or invalidates the BTB\\\\nentry. This should recover much of the loop/call bubble cost while avoiding\\\\nthe timing regression seen from instruction-driven IF prediction logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with compact prediction metadata so EX can validate a BTB hit against the... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[]},\"title\":\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778812092847,\"end\":1778812092852}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0b76c7ec6ec2182b016a0684bc3bb48199b76df5b7dab21a0e\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e29767fa80016URz8nPvDBnVT8\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d689d06affeh6qe8LNtYq1ltf\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e29766e4b0015rQe3q6pL4n2tW\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep1/agent.log:310:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778813706862,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_IFD2dAFijEow1Mksh2r2KCjn\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20260514-002-r2s1|hyp-20260514-002-r3s1|hyp-20260514-003-r2s2|hyp-20260514-003-r3s2|hyp-20260514-001-r1s0|hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/experiments\",\"include\":\"log.jsonl\"},\"output\":\"Found 7 matches\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\n  Line 2: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r1s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Move DIV/REM Off The ALU Critical Path\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU is fully combinational and includes RV32M divide\\\\nand remainder operators in its worst-case path. On the Tang Nano 20K that\\\\nlogic is likely much slower than the add/shift/branch hot path, yet\\\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by list/state/matrix work and appears\\\\nto use division only in cold setup code. If the divider stops limiting Fmax,\\\\na small stall cost on rare DIV/REM instructions should buy a net fitness\\\\ngain.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current EX-stage ALU is fully combinational and includes RV32M divide\\\\nand remainder operators in its worst-case path. On the Tang Nano 20K that\\\\nlogic is likely much slower than the add/shift/branch hot path, yet\\\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by list/state/matrix work and appears\\\\nto use division only in cold setup code. If the divider stops limiting Fmax,\\\\na small stall cost on rare DIV/REM instructions should buy a net fitness\\\\ngain.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the single-cycle combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU logic with a small\\\\niterative divider in EX, while keeping ADD/logic/shift/branch and the\\\\nmultiply family on the existing fast path. EX would assert a busy condition\\\\nonly for divide/remainder ops, hold younger stages until the result is ready,\\\\nthen release a single normal retirement with the same RV32M corner-case and\\\\nRVFI semantics. This should shorten the worst timing path, trim LUT pressure,\\\\nand preserve CoreMark/MHz because the hot benchmark loop is not divider\\\\nheavy.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove DIV/REM from the always-hot combinational ALU path while keeping fast ALU ops and MUL-family behavior unchanged.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add an iterative divider path in EX and hold EX/MEM advance until DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU com...\\n  Line 3: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r1s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Stall-Only Fetch Replay Buffer\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core turns every random `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a frontend\\\\nbubble even when the same hot-loop PC has already been fetched many times.\\\\nUnder the tournament's fixed ~22% instruction-stall model, recovering even a\\\\nfraction of those lost fetches should raise iterations/cycle without\\\\nwidening retirement or touching the already timing-sensitive EX datapath.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core turns every random `io_imemReady=0` cycle into a frontend\\\\nbubble even when the same hot-loop PC has already been fetched many times.\\\\nUnder the tournament's fixed ~22% instruction-stall model, recovering even a\\\\nfraction of those lost fetches should raise iterations/cycle without\\\\nwidening retirement or touching the already timing-sensitive EX datapath.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny direct-mapped instruction replay buffer in IF that records\\\\n`{pc, instr}` on successful live fetches and is consulted only when the\\\\nexternal imem port is not ready. On an imem-stall hit, IF should supply the\\\\ncached instruction and assert an effective fetch-ready signal so decode\\\\nadvances instead of seeing a forced bubble. Keep the zero-wait path direct\\\\nwhen `io_imemReady=1`, and preserve the current redirect, reset, and RVFI\\\\nretirement behavior. This targets the benchmark's synthetic iStall bubbles\\\\ndirectly while avoiding new pressure on the ALU, load-use path, or dmem\\\\ntiming.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 8, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a small stall-only instruction replay table keyed by fetch PC, update it on successful live fetches, and return cached instructions on imem-stall hits while preserving the current direct fast path and redirect/flush semantics.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Expose an IF-stage fetch-available signal to the top level and route it into hazar...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-Deep Stalled-Store Retirement Slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core already got its biggest measured win by shortening the EX hot\\\\npath with the DIV/REM side unit, and the two failed bench structural attempts\\\\nboth showed that adding more logic to IF is a bad trade on this snapshot.\\\\nThe cleaner remaining structural loss is on the data side: every\\\\n`io_dmemReady=0` cycle for a STORE still freezes the whole pipeline even\\\\nthough the store has no register result and its address, byte mask, and write\\\\ndata are already known in MEM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core already got its biggest measured win by shortening the EX hot\\\\npath with the DIV/REM side unit, and the two failed bench structural attempts\\\\nboth showed that adding more logic to IF is a bad trade on this snapshot.\\\\nThe cleaner remaining structural loss is on the data side: every\\\\n`io_dmemReady=0` cycle for a STORE still freezes the whole pipeline even\\\\nthough the store has no register result and its address, byte mask, and write\\\\ndata are already known in MEM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only for legal,\\\\naligned normal-RAM stores when they reach MEM and the external dmem port is\\\\nstalled. Keep the existing zero-wait store fast path unchanged. On a stalled\\\\nRAM store, capture the store's byte address, replicated write data, byte\\\\nmask, and RVFI-visible memory fields into the slot, retire that store once on\\\\nchannel 0, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while the slot\\\\ndrains oldest-first onto dmem. Loads, later memory operations, and all\\\\nMMIO/UART/marker accesses must still wait behind the slot so ordering,\\\\nCoreMark timing markers, and RVFI semantics stay intact. This targets the\\\\ntournament's random dStall bubbles without reopening the frontend critical\\\\npath or putting load data onto the EX operand muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"con...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny PC-Indexed Taken BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The last predictor attempt lost fitness because it lengthened the frontend\\\\ncritical path with `imem_data` decode and branch-immediate construction in IF.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core waits until EX to correct any\\\\ntaken control flow, so hot loops and repeated calls keep paying wrong-path\\\\nfetch bubbles. The most promising predictor angle is therefore one that keeps\\\\nprediction state PC-indexed and decode-free on the fetch path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The last predictor attempt lost fitness because it lengthened the frontend\\\\ncritical path with `imem_data` decode and branch-immediate construction in IF.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core waits until EX to correct any\\\\ntaken control flow, so hot loops and repeated calls keep paying wrong-path\\\\nfetch bubbles. The most promising predictor angle is therefore one that keeps\\\\nprediction state PC-indexed and decode-free on the fetch path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer that is consulted by PC\\\\nalone and predicts only previously-taken control-flow sites. IF should use a\\\\n`valid/tag/target` BTB hit to fetch the remembered target instead of `pc+4`,\\\\nwithout decoding the fetched instruction or rebuilding immediates on the hot\\\\npath. EX remains the point of truth for branch direction, JAL/JALR targets,\\\\ntraps, and RVFI; it simply compares the resolved next PC against the carried\\\\nprediction metadata, redirects on misses, and updates or invalidates the BTB\\\\nentry. This should recover much of the loop/call bubble cost while avoiding\\\\nthe timing regression seen from instruction-driven IF prediction logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with compact prediction metadata so EX can validate a BTB hit against the...\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share One RV32M Multiplier Datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still pays for three parallel multiply datapaths on\\\\nevery EX cycle: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. The\\\\nlast MULH offload attempt showed that adding new long-op control can erase\\\\nthe timing win, but the existing `alu.sv` structure is still a strong Fmax\\\\nrisk because it duplicates wide multiply hardware even though CoreMark's hot\\\\nloop mostly needs plain low-half `MUL`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still pays for three parallel multiply datapaths on\\\\nevery EX cycle: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. The\\\\nlast MULH offload attempt showed that adding new long-op control can erase\\\\nthe timing win, but the existing `alu.sv` structure is still a strong Fmax\\\\nrisk because it duplicates wide multiply hardware even though CoreMark's hot\\\\nloop mostly needs plain low-half `MUL`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework `rtl/alu.sv` so all four multiply-family ops stay single-cycle, but\\\\nare implemented from one correctly-sized shared 32x32 product datapath plus\\\\nsmall sign-correction logic for the high-half variants instead of three\\\\nseparate products. This keeps the divider/store-slot wins intact, avoids new\\\\nEX stall or result-retime machinery, and attacks the same remaining RV32M\\\\ntiming cost that motivated the failed MULH split with a much lighter control\\\\nfootprint. If Yosys/nextpnr currently map the three products into duplicated\\\\nDSP or LUT fabric, collapsing them into one multiplier should improve Fmax\\\\nand area with essentially no CoreMark IPC cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the current three-parallel multiply implementation with one shared RV32M product path and signed-high correction logic, while preserving exact MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU results and the existing ALTO...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-Deep RAM Store Retirement Queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The first MEM-side store slot already proved that this snapshot gains more\\\\nfrom data-side decoupling than from extra frontend logic. The remaining\\\\nstructural bottleneck is that the current one-entry pending-store design\\\\nstill serializes every second normal-RAM store behind the first pending\\\\none, even though both stores already have fully computed address, mask, and\\\\nwrite data and neither produces a register result.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The first MEM-side store slot already proved that this snapshot gains more\\\\nfrom data-side decoupling than from extra frontend logic. The remaining\\\\nstructural bottleneck is that the current one-entry pending-store design\\\\nstill serializes every second normal-RAM store behind the first pending\\\\none, even though both stores already have fully computed address, mask, and\\\\nwrite data and neither produces a register result.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-entry pending-store slot in `mem_stage.sv` with a tiny\\\\ntwo-entry oldest-first queue for legal, aligned normal-RAM stores. Keep the\\\\nzero-wait store fast path unchanged, retire a store once when it is\\\\nenqueued, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while one or two\\\\nqueued stores drain onto dmem. Loads, MMIO/marker accesses, and any later\\\\nmemory op must still wait behind the queue for ordering and RVFI\\\\ncorrectness. This extends the only structural direction that has already\\\\nimproved fitness, while staying off the timing-sensitive IF and EX paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single pending-store slot with a two-entry FIFO of buffered store metadata plus enqueue, drain, and full/empty control, while keeping buffered retirement restricted to legal aligned normal-RAM stores and preserving strict ordering for loads and MMIO.\\\"}...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-Entry Loop Target Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core still pays a full control-flow bubble whenever a hot loop\\\\nbackedge is taken, but the two prior predictor attempts showed that this\\\\ndesign cannot afford much extra logic on the fetch critical path. The next\\\\npredictor should therefore recover only the highest-value target predictions\\\\nwhile keeping IF hardware smaller than both an instruction-driven predictor\\\\nand a multi-entry BTB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core still pays a full control-flow bubble whenever a hot loop\\\\nbackedge is taken, but the two prior predictor attempts showed that this\\\\ndesign cannot afford much extra logic on the fetch critical path. The next\\\\npredictor should therefore recover only the highest-value target predictions\\\\nwhile keeping IF hardware smaller than both an instruction-driven predictor\\\\nand a multi-entry BTB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry loop-target predictor that remembers only the most recent\\\\ntaken backward branch and predicts again only on an exact PC match. IF would\\\\nconsult one registered `{valid, branch_pc, target_pc}` entry and choose the\\\\nremembered target instead of `pc+4` on a hit, with no `imem_data` decode,\\\\nimmediate construction, or table RAM lookup on the hot path. EX remains the\\\\npoint of truth: it validates the carried prediction, redirects only on\\\\nmispredicts, and updates or invalidates the single entry when a backward\\\\nbranch is taken or falls through. This keeps the predictor focused on\\\\nCoreMark's dominant tight loops while making the IF cost much smaller than\\\\nthe previously-regressing BTB approach.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with compact prediction metadata so EX can validate a loop-target hit against the resolved next PC.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":7,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20260514-002-r2s1|hyp-20260514-002-r3s1|hyp-20260514-003-r2s2|hyp-20260514-003-r3s2|hyp-20260514-001-r1s0|hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778813706830,\"end\":1778813706861}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0da088cafca4df7d016a068b0aaf448198b162d29a28bb9cef\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e298f223e001JLrNQ8PmsvYCtP\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e298ee8650015RCHpkcJ0T1VRN\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep1/agent.log:319:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778813796816,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_qCIhRC1S9qmMpiZ7XZx2TQyu\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260514-002-r4s1.log\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":300},\"output\":\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20260514-002-r4s1.log</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"step_start\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":1778813693312,\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"part\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"prt_e298eed7e001ikvPczCnDv1vpv\\\",\\\"messageID\\\":\\\"msg_e298ee8650015RCHpkcJ0T1VRN\\\",\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"snapshot\\\":\\\"3e3b915f7f7acc24182ecdba30a18bbfa16f22bb\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"step-start\\\"}}\\n2: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":1778813706789,\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"part\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"prt_e298f1565001LR2IalQ2ij0L5g\\\",\\\"messageID\\\":\\\"msg_e298ee8650015RCHpkcJ0T1VRN\\\",\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Reviewing prior `bench` lessons and structural experiment history first so the hypothesis stays novel and targeted.\\\",\\\"time\\\":{\\\"start\\\":1778813703525,\\\"end\\\":1778813706787},\\\"metadata\\\":{\\\"openai\\\":{\\\"itemId\\\":\\\"msg_0da088cafca4df7d016a068b0759ec8198ab307466b34420e6\\\",\\\"phase\\\":\\\"commentary\\\"}}}}\\n3: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":1778813706843,\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"part\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"read\\\",\\\"callID\\\":\\\"call_7sv4kR9HaWtBwnfP7syhTVov\\\",\\\"state\\\":{\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md\\\",\\\"offset\\\":1,\\\"limit\\\":300},\\\"output\\\":\\\"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\\\\n<type>file</type>\\\\n<content>\\\\n1: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r1s0 (improvement, +21.46%): Moving DIV/REM off the combinational EX ALU into a busy/stall iterative unit can improve FPGA fitness when CoreMark's timed loop is divider-cold, because shortening the add/shift/branch critical path buys more Fmax than the rare long-op stalls cost.\\\\n2: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r1s1 (regression, +16.65%): On this core's combinational IF/ID frontend, even a tiny stall-only replay buffer plus `fetch_available` hazard gating cost more than the synthetic `io_imemReady` bubbles it hid, so fetch-side caching is not a free IPC win.\\\\n3: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-003-r1s2 (regression, -15.17%): On this bench core, pushing even a static backward-branch predictor into IF by decoding `imem_data`, building the B-immediate, and muxing `next_pc` there costs more fitness than the loop-bubble savings, so frontend critical-path growth is a tighter constraint than branch IPC.\\\\n4: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-001-r2s0 (regression, +6.35%): Splitting `MULH`/`MULHU`/`MULHSU` out of `alu.sv` into an `ex_long_busy` helper regressed, so cold-op offloading only helps when timing reports show those variants are actually on the critical path and not when the extra EX stall/result-retime control becomes the new cost.\\\\n5: - 2026-05-14 hyp-20260514-002-r2s1 (improvement, +12.09%): When `io_dmemReady` bubbles dominate and IF timing is already tight, a one-entry MEM-side pending-store slot for aligned normal-RAM stores can improve fitness... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n4: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":1778813706862,\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"part\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"grep\\\",\\\"callID\\\":\\\"call_IFD2dAFijEow1Mksh2r2KCjn\\\",\\\"state\\\":{\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"\\\\\\\"category\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\s*:\\\\\\\\s*\\\\\\\"structural\\\\\\\"|hyp-20260514-002-r2s1|hyp-20260514-002-r3s1|hyp-20260514-003-r2s2|hyp-20260514-003-r3s2|hyp-20260514-001-r1s0|hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/experiments\\\",\\\"include\\\":\\\"log.jsonl\\\"},\\\"output\\\":\\\"Found 7 matches\\\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\\\\n  Line 2: {\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"hyp-20260514-001-r1s0\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Move DIV/REM Off The ALU Critical Path\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"category\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"micro_opt\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"motivation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The current EX-stage ALU is fully combinational and includes RV32M divide\\\\\\\\nand remainder operators in its worst-case path. On the Tang Nano 20K that\\\\\\\\nlogic is likely much slower than the add/shift/branch hot path, yet\\\\\\\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by list/state/matrix work and appears\\\\\\\\nto use division only in cold setup code. If the divider stops limiting Fmax,\\\\\\\\na small stall cost on rare DIV/REM instructions should buy a net fitness\\\\\\\\ngain.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"hypothesis\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"motivation\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"The current EX-stage ALU is fully combinational and includes RV32M divide\\\\\\\\nand remainder operators in its worst-case path. On the Tang Nano 20K that\\\\\\\\nlogic is likely much slower than the add/shift/branch hot path, yet\\\\\\\\nCoreMark's timed region is dominated by list/state/matrix work and appears\\\\\\\\nto use division only in cold setup code. If the divider stops limiting Fmax,\\\\\\\\na small stall cost on rare DIV/REM instructions should buy a net fitness\\\\\\\\ngain.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"hypothesis\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Replace the single-cycle combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU logic with a small\\\\\\\\niterative divider in EX, while keeping ADD/logic/shift/branch and the\\\\\\\\nmultiply family on the existing fast path. EX woul... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\\n5: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"step_finish\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":1778813706962,\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"part\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"prt_e298f22d0001lAKe6jzpd7lAZ6\\\",\\\"reason\\\":\\\"tool-calls\\\",\\\"snapshot\\\":\\\"3e3b915f7f7acc24182ecdba30a18bbfa16f22bb\\\",\\\"messageID\\\":\\\"msg_e298ee8650015RCHpkcJ0T1VRN\\\",\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"step-finish\\\",\\\"tokens\\\":{\\\"total\\\":41457,\\\"input\\\":24860,\\\"output\\\":256,\\\"reasoning\\\":469,\\\"cache\\\":{\\\"write\\\":0,\\\"read\\\":15872}},\\\"cost\\\":0}}\\n6: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"step_start\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":1778813708426,\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"part\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"prt_e298f2888001Nu1b4xA04v13G0\\\",\\\"messageID\\\":\\\"msg_e298f233a001qmJw3wcJyFF7Du\\\",\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"snapshot\\\":\\\"3e3b915f7f7acc24182ecdba30a18bbfa16f22bb\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"step-start\\\"}}\\n7: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":1778813788087,\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"part\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"prt_e29905989001jzx3i6NbW3aWvv\\\",\\\"messageID\\\":\\\"msg_e298f233a001qmJw3wcJyFF7Du\\\",\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_1d6711882fferWL7z1VmpBD1VN\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The strongest remaining structural angle is to hide load and divide stalls without touching IF: a tiny in-order issn/zero-extended\\\\nmultiply products in the one-cycle EX datapath, and each product is written\\\\nas a 64-bit-by-64-bit expression even though the ISA operands are only 32\\\\nbits wide. That is a strong candidate for the remaining Fmax limiter after\\\\nDIV/REM was moved out of the hot path. CoreMark's timed region is multiply\\\\nheavy because of the matrix kernel, but it overwhelmingly cares about plain\\\\nlow-half `MUL`; the high-half multiply variants are correctness-critical yet\\\\nbenchmark-cold.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep plain `MUL` as the hot single-cycle multiply, but remove `MULH`,\\\\n`MULHU`, and `MULHSU` from the always-hot combinational ALU path. In\\\\n`rtl/alu.sv`, collapse the current triple-product implementation so the fast\\\\npath only needs one correctly-sized low-half multiply for `MUL` plus the\\\\nexisting simple ALU ops. In `rtl/ex_stage.sv`, add a small cold helper for\\\\nthe high-half multiply variants, reusing the existing long-op stall pattern\\\\nused by DIV/REM so those rare instructions remain architecturally correct\\\\nwithout capping global Fmax. This should preserve CoreMark IPC much better\\\\nthan making all multiply ops iterative, while still buying timing slack by\\\\ndeleting the widest multiply logic from the common EX res...\\n  Line 6: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-Deep Stalled-Store Retirement Slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core already got its biggest measured win by shortening the EX hot\\\\npath with the DIV/REM side unit, and the two failed bench structural attempts\\\\nboth showed that adding more logic to IF is a bad trade on this snapshot.\\\\nThe cleaner remaining structural loss is on the data side: every\\\\n`io_dmemReady=0` cycle for a STORE still freezes the whole pipeline even\\\\nthough the store has no register result and its address, byte mask, and write\\\\ndata are already known in MEM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core already got its biggest measured win by shortening the EX hot\\\\npath with the DIV/REM side unit, and the two failed bench structural attempts\\\\nboth showed that adding more logic to IF is a bad trade on this snapshot.\\\\nThe cleaner remaining structural loss is on the data side: every\\\\n`io_dmemReady=0` cycle for a STORE still freezes the whole pipeline even\\\\nthough the store has no register result and its address, byte mask, and write\\\\ndata are already known in MEM.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only for legal,\\\\naligned normal-RAM stores when they reach MEM and the external dmem port is\\\\nstalled. Keep the existing zero-wait store fast path unchanged. On a stalled\\\\nRAM store, capture the store's byte address, replicated write data, byte\\\\nmask, and RVFI-visible memory fields into the slot, retire that store once on\\\\nchannel 0, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while the slot\\\\ndrains oldest-first onto dmem. Loads, later memory operations, and all\\\\nMMIO/UART/marker accesses must still wait behind the slot so ordering,\\\\nCoreMark timing markers, and RVFI semantics stay intact. This targets the\\\\ntournament's random dStall bubbles without reopening the frontend critical\\\\npath or putting load data onto the EX operand muxes.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"con...\\n  Line 7: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r2s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Tiny PC-Indexed Taken BTB\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The last predictor attempt lost fitness because it lengthened the frontend\\\\ncritical path with `imem_data` decode and branch-immediate construction in IF.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core waits until EX to correct any\\\\ntaken control flow, so hot loops and repeated calls keep paying wrong-path\\\\nfetch bubbles. The most promising predictor angle is therefore one that keeps\\\\nprediction state PC-indexed and decode-free on the fetch path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The last predictor attempt lost fitness because it lengthened the frontend\\\\ncritical path with `imem_data` decode and branch-immediate construction in IF.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core waits until EX to correct any\\\\ntaken control flow, so hot loops and repeated calls keep paying wrong-path\\\\nfetch bubbles. The most promising predictor angle is therefore one that keeps\\\\nprediction state PC-indexed and decode-free on the fetch path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer that is consulted by PC\\\\nalone and predicts only previously-taken control-flow sites. IF should use a\\\\n`valid/tag/target` BTB hit to fetch the remembered target instead of `pc+4`,\\\\nwithout decoding the fetched instruction or rebuilding immediates on the hot\\\\npath. EX remains the point of truth for branch direction, JAL/JALR targets,\\\\ntraps, and RVFI; it simply compares the resolved next PC against the carried\\\\nprediction metadata, redirects on misses, and updates or invalidates the BTB\\\\nentry. This should recover much of the loop/call bubble cost while avoiding\\\\nthe timing regression seen from instruction-driven IF prediction logic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with compact prediction metadata so EX can validate a BTB hit against the...\\n  Line 8: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r3s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Share One RV32M Multiplier Datapath\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still pays for three parallel multiply datapaths on\\\\nevery EX cycle: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. The\\\\nlast MULH offload attempt showed that adding new long-op control can erase\\\\nthe timing win, but the existing `alu.sv` structure is still a strong Fmax\\\\nrisk because it duplicates wide multiply hardware even though CoreMark's hot\\\\nloop mostly needs plain low-half `MUL`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still pays for three parallel multiply datapaths on\\\\nevery EX cycle: signed*signed, unsigned*unsigned, and signed*unsigned. The\\\\nlast MULH offload attempt showed that adding new long-op control can erase\\\\nthe timing win, but the existing `alu.sv` structure is still a strong Fmax\\\\nrisk because it duplicates wide multiply hardware even though CoreMark's hot\\\\nloop mostly needs plain low-half `MUL`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework `rtl/alu.sv` so all four multiply-family ops stay single-cycle, but\\\\nare implemented from one correctly-sized shared 32x32 product datapath plus\\\\nsmall sign-correction logic for the high-half variants instead of three\\\\nseparate products. This keeps the divider/store-slot wins intact, avoids new\\\\nEX stall or result-retime machinery, and attacks the same remaining RV32M\\\\ntiming cost that motivated the failed MULH split with a much lighter control\\\\nfootprint. If Yosys/nextpnr currently map the three products into duplicated\\\\nDSP or LUT fabric, collapsing them into one multiplier should improve Fmax\\\\nand area with essentially no CoreMark IPC cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the current three-parallel multiply implementation with one shared RV32M product path and signed-high correction logic, while preserving exact MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU results and the existing ALTO...\\n  Line 9: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r3s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Two-Deep RAM Store Retirement Queue\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The first MEM-side store slot already proved that this snapshot gains more\\\\nfrom data-side decoupling than from extra frontend logic. The remaining\\\\nstructural bottleneck is that the current one-entry pending-store design\\\\nstill serializes every second normal-RAM store behind the first pending\\\\none, even though both stores already have fully computed address, mask, and\\\\nwrite data and neither produces a register result.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The first MEM-side store slot already proved that this snapshot gains more\\\\nfrom data-side decoupling than from extra frontend logic. The remaining\\\\nstructural bottleneck is that the current one-entry pending-store design\\\\nstill serializes every second normal-RAM store behind the first pending\\\\none, even though both stores already have fully computed address, mask, and\\\\nwrite data and neither produces a register result.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the one-entry pending-store slot in `mem_stage.sv` with a tiny\\\\ntwo-entry oldest-first queue for legal, aligned normal-RAM stores. Keep the\\\\nzero-wait store fast path unchanged, retire a store once when it is\\\\nenqueued, and let younger non-memory instructions continue while one or two\\\\nqueued stores drain onto dmem. Loads, MMIO/marker accesses, and any later\\\\nmemory op must still wait behind the queue for ordering and RVFI\\\\ncorrectness. This extends the only structural direction that has already\\\\nimproved fitness, while staying off the timing-sensitive IF and EX paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the single pending-store slot with a two-entry FIFO of buffered store metadata plus enqueue, drain, and full/empty control, while keeping buffered retirement restricted to legal aligned normal-RAM stores and preserving strict ordering for loads and MMIO.\\\"}...\\n  Line 10: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-Entry Loop Target Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core still pays a full control-flow bubble whenever a hot loop\\\\nbackedge is taken, but the two prior predictor attempts showed that this\\\\ndesign cannot afford much extra logic on the fetch critical path. The next\\\\npredictor should therefore recover only the highest-value target predictions\\\\nwhile keeping IF hardware smaller than both an instruction-driven predictor\\\\nand a multi-entry BTB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core still pays a full control-flow bubble whenever a hot loop\\\\nbackedge is taken, but the two prior predictor attempts showed that this\\\\ndesign cannot afford much extra logic on the fetch critical path. The next\\\\npredictor should therefore recover only the highest-value target predictions\\\\nwhile keeping IF hardware smaller than both an instruction-driven predictor\\\\nand a multi-entry BTB.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a single-entry loop-target predictor that remembers only the most recent\\\\ntaken backward branch and predicts again only on an exact PC match. IF would\\\\nconsult one registered `{valid, branch_pc, target_pc}` entry and choose the\\\\nremembered target instead of `pc+4` on a hit, with no `imem_data` decode,\\\\nimmediate construction, or table RAM lookup on the hot path. EX remains the\\\\npoint of truth: it validates the carried prediction, redirects only on\\\\nmispredicts, and updates or invalidates the single entry when a backward\\\\nbranch is taken or falls through. This keeps the predictor focused on\\\\nCoreMark's dominant tight loops while making the IF cost much smaller than\\\\nthe previously-regressing BTB approach.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads with compact prediction metadata so EX can validate a loop-target hit against the resolved next PC.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/if_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"...\\n  Line 11: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-001-r4s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"DSP-Friendly RV32M Multiplier Sizing\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still encodes its multiply family as three always-live\\\\n64-bit-extended products, even though RV32M only needs 32x32 operand widths.\\\\nThe last multiplier experiments showed that adding new long-op control or\\\\nsign-correction chains is a poor trade here, so the cleaner remaining angle\\\\nis to keep the existing single-cycle behavior but make the multiplier coding\\\\nfriendlier to Gowin DSP inference and shorter routing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench ALU still encodes its multiply family as three always-live\\\\n64-bit-extended products, even though RV32M only needs 32x32 operand widths.\\\\nThe last multiplier experiments showed that adding new long-op control or\\\\nsign-correction chains is a poor trade here, so the cleaner remaining angle\\\\nis to keep the existing single-cycle behavior but make the multiplier coding\\\\nfriendlier to Gowin DSP inference and shorter routing.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rewrite `rtl/alu.sv` so `MUL`, `MULH`, `MULHU`, and `MULHSU` are driven by\\\\ncorrectly sized 32x32 or 33x33 signed/unsigned products instead of the\\\\ncurrent sign/zero-extended 64-bit operand expressions. Preserve the present\\\\none-cycle RV32M contract, the ALTOPS branch, and the existing simple-ALU\\\\nfast path; the change is purely about expressing the multiply datapaths in a\\\\nform that lets Yosys/nextpnr infer smaller, faster hardware without adding\\\\nany new EX stalls or high-half post-processing. If the current coding style\\\\nis blocking clean DSP mapping, this should improve Fmax with essentially no\\\\nCoreMark IPC penalty.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/alu.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the current sign/zero-extended 64-bit multiply expressions with DSP-friendly correctly sized RV32M product forms while keeping MUL-family latency, results, and ALTOPS behavior unchang...\\n  Line 12: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-002-r4s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"One-Deep Ordered Long-Op Shadow Slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Frontend-side structural ideas have already regressed on this core, while the\\\\none-entry RAM-store slot improved fitness by exploiting the benchmark's\\\\nsynthetic dmem stalls without touching IF. The remaining structural waste is\\\\nthat any stalled LOAD or in-flight DIV/REM still freezes the whole machine\\\\neven when the next instruction is an independent short ALU op whose result\\\\ncould be computed and parked cheaply.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Frontend-side structural ideas have already regressed on this core, while the\\\\none-entry RAM-store slot improved fitness by exploiting the benchmark's\\\\nsynthetic dmem stalls without touching IF. The remaining structural waste is\\\\nthat any stalled LOAD or in-flight DIV/REM still freezes the whole machine\\\\neven when the next instruction is an independent short ALU op whose result\\\\ncould be computed and parked cheaply.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny in-order decoupling layer around the current EX/MEM->MEM/WB path:\\\\nkeep one pending older long-latency op slot for LOAD or DIV/REM, plus one\\\\nyounger short-op shadow result slot for an independent non-memory,\\\\nnon-redirect instruction behind it. Hazard logic would treat the pending rd as\\\\nbusy, block later memory operations, and preserve strict RVFI/channel-0 order\\\\nby retiring the older long op first and releasing the shadowed younger result\\\\non the following cycle. This stays off the timing-sensitive IF path, extends\\\\nthe already-successful data-side decoupling direction beyond stores, and\\\\ncould turn a meaningful fraction of random single-cycle dmem bubbles into\\\\nuseful ALU work with limited new state.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add compact metadata for a pending older long op and one younger shadow result so the pipeline can hold execution state without vi...\\n  Line 13: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260514-003-r4s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Direction-Only Backward-Branch Hold Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every prior bench predictor lost fitness because it inserted compare, target,\\\\nor decode logic directly into the IF `pc -> next_pc` path. But this core\\\\nstill wastes two sequential fetch slots on taken branches before EX resolves\\\\nthem. A predictor that reduces that waste without changing IF target\\\\nselection is the cleanest remaining control-flow lever.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every prior bench predictor lost fitness because it inserted compare, target,\\\\nor decode logic directly into the IF `pc -> next_pc` path. But this core\\\\nstill wastes two sequential fetch slots on taken branches before EX resolves\\\\nthem. A predictor that reduces that waste without changing IF target\\\\nselection is the cleanest remaining control-flow lever.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the existing EX redirect point, but add a very cheap static direction\\\\npredictor that treats legal backward conditional branches as likely taken.\\\\nWhen such a branch reaches EX, `hazard_unit` should hold the frontend for one\\\\ncycle and inject a bubble into ID instead of allowing a second wrong-path\\\\nsequential fetch. If the branch is actually taken, EX redirects exactly as it\\\\ndoes today and the branch penalty drops from two wrong-path fetches to one.\\\\nIf the branch falls through, the only cost is a single bubble on the rare\\\\nloop exit. This stays off the timing-sensitive IF target path because it adds\\\\nno BTB lookup, no tag compare, and no target mux on `pc+4` selection.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a predictor-driven frontend-hold mode for backward conditional branches already in EX: stall PC, emit an IF NOP, and bubble ID for one cycle so taken branches fetch at most one wrong-path instruction.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the existing `id_ex_w` branch/i...\\n  Line 14: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r5s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Decode-Aware Load-Use Stall Filter\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core has already shown that small, timing-friendly changes\\\\ncan beat larger frontend ideas, but its load-use hazard check is still more\\\\nconservative than the ISA requires. `hazard_unit.sv` stalls whenever a load\\\\nin EX matches raw `instr[19:15]` or `instr[24:20]` in IF/ID, even if the\\\\nyounger opcode does not actually read that source. In a load-heavy CoreMark\\\\nmix, that creates avoidable bubbles after immediates, jumps, LUI/AUIPC, and\\\\nother single-source instructions whose unused bitfields alias the older\\\\nload's `rd`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core has already shown that small, timing-friendly changes\\\\ncan beat larger frontend ideas, but its load-use hazard check is still more\\\\nconservative than the ISA requires. `hazard_unit.sv` stalls whenever a load\\\\nin EX matches raw `instr[19:15]` or `instr[24:20]` in IF/ID, even if the\\\\nyounger opcode does not actually read that source. In a load-heavy CoreMark\\\\nmix, that creates avoidable bubbles after immediates, jumps, LUI/AUIPC, and\\\\nother single-source instructions whose unused bitfields alias the older\\\\nload's `rd`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Refine the load-use detector so it only stalls when the younger IF/ID\\\\ninstruction truly consumes the matching source register. The minimal form is\\\\nan opcode-aware `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` decode in `hazard_unit.sv`, wired\\\\nfrom the current IF/ID instruction, while keeping the existing redirect,\\\\nimem stall, dmem stall, and long-op precedence unchanged. This stays off the\\\\nfetch `pc -> next_pc` critical path, avoids the new retirement machinery\\\\nthat hurt prior structural attempts, and should recover IPC by removing\\\\npurely false one-cycle bubbles without meaningfully changing Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Gate load-use ...\\n  Line 15: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r5s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Registered IF/ID Frontend Retime\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core's biggest proven gains have come from shortening hot timing\\\\npaths, and the repeated predictor/replay regressions point at the frontend\\\\nas especially fragile when extra logic lands on `pc -> next_pc`. This core\\\\nstill has a long fetch-to-decode path because `if_stage` emits a\\\\ncombinational IF/ID payload that immediately drives decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile addressing, and the ID/EX register in the same cycle.\\\\nA pure stage retime attacks that path directly without adding new prediction\\\\nlogic to IF.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core's biggest proven gains have come from shortening hot timing\\\\npaths, and the repeated predictor/replay regressions point at the frontend\\\\nas especially fragile when extra logic lands on `pc -> next_pc`. This core\\\\nstill has a long fetch-to-decode path because `if_stage` emits a\\\\ncombinational IF/ID payload that immediately drives decode, immediate\\\\ngeneration, regfile addressing, and the ID/EX register in the same cycle.\\\\nA pure stage retime attacks that path directly without adding new prediction\\\\nlogic to IF.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a real IF/ID pipeline register and split fetch from decode/regfile so\\\\nthe frontend becomes a cleaner 6-stage flow with `PC/imem -> IF/ID flop ->\\\\ndecode+regfile -> ID/EX flop`. Keep branch and jump resolution in EX and\\\\nkeep the `pc+4` / redirect selection logic simple, but retime the current\\\\nstall/flush rules around the extra register so the RVFI contract and\\\\nsingle-retire behavior stay unchanged. This will slightly increase control\\\\nlatency, but it should buy more Fmax than it costs in IPC because it removes\\\\ndecode/regfile work from the already timing-sensitive fetch side instead of\\\\nadding more compare/mux logic there.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"...\\n  Line 16: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r5s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-Stage One-Bit Loop Redirect Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every predictor tried so far lost fitness because it touched the IF\\\\n`pc -> next_pc` path with decode, compare, or target-selection logic.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core does not know a\\\\nconditional branch is taken until EX, so tight CoreMark backedges keep\\\\nwasting fetch bandwidth. The most promising remaining predictor angle is\\\\nto move prediction later, into decode, where the branch opcode,\\\\nimmediate, and PC are already available off the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every predictor tried so far lost fitness because it touched the IF\\\\n`pc -> next_pc` path with decode, compare, or target-selection logic.\\\\nBut the branch penalty is still real: this core does not know a\\\\nconditional branch is taken until EX, so tight CoreMark backedges keep\\\\nwasting fetch bandwidth. The most promising remaining predictor angle is\\\\nto move prediction later, into decode, where the branch opcode,\\\\nimmediate, and PC are already available off the fetch critical path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny PC-indexed one-bit branch-history table that is consulted only\\\\nin ID for legal conditional branches, and let ID issue an early redirect\\\\nfor predicted-taken branches using the already-generated branch\\\\nimmediate. EX remains architecturally authoritative: it recomputes the\\\\nreal branch outcome, redirects on mismatches, and updates the history\\\\nentry. This avoids the regressions seen from IF-stage BTB/target logic,\\\\nand unlike the EX-stage hold predictor it does not inject a guaranteed\\\\nbubble on every loop-closing branch. Correctly learned loop backedges\\\\nshould therefore recover one cycle of control waste with much smaller\\\\nFmax risk than any fetch-path predictor tried so far.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend `id_ex_t` with compac...\\n  Line 17: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r6s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Opcode-Qualified Load-Use Stall Filter\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector stalls on any raw `instr[19:15]` or\\\\n`instr[24:20]` alias behind a load in EX. On this core that means many\\\\nI-type, JAL, LUI/AUIPC, FENCE, and illegal-instruction cases take a false\\\\none-cycle bubble even though the younger instruction never consumes the\\\\nloaded value. The prior attempt identified the right bottleneck but broke\\\\nthe contract by editing decode-stage source handling instead of only\\\\nrefining the local hazard predicate.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current load-use detector stalls on any raw `instr[19:15]` or\\\\n`instr[24:20]` alias behind a load in EX. On this core that means many\\\\nI-type, JAL, LUI/AUIPC, FENCE, and illegal-instruction cases take a false\\\\none-cycle bubble even though the younger instruction never consumes the\\\\nloaded value. The prior attempt identified the right bottleneck but broke\\\\nthe contract by editing decode-stage source handling instead of only\\\\nrefining the local hazard predicate.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the optimization fully local to hazard detection: pass the raw IF/ID\\\\ninstruction into `hazard_unit.sv`, derive compact opcode-level `uses_rs1`\\\\nand `uses_rs2` bits there, and only assert the load-use bubble when the\\\\nyounger instruction architecturally reads the matching source. This\\\\npreserves the existing IF/ID payload, regfile/RVFI source reporting,\\\\nredirect rules, and long-op/memory stall precedence while recovering IPC\\\\nfrom false load-use bubbles with essentially no new timing on the fetch\\\\n`pc -> next_pc` path.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Pass the full IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit so the load-use detector can qualify source usage without altering decode-stage or RVFI source fields.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the raw rs1/rs2 ...\\n  Line 18: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r6s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Non-Aliasing Load Bypass Around Store Slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only structural change that has already improved this core is the\\\\none-entry MEM-side RAM store slot, which confirms that data-side decoupling\\\\npays while frontend work does not. The remaining conservative bottleneck is\\\\nthat once the slot is occupied, every younger memory op stalls behind it\\\\neven if the next op is a normal-RAM load to a different word and the single\\\\ndmem port is ready.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The only structural change that has already improved this core is the\\\\none-entry MEM-side RAM store slot, which confirms that data-side decoupling\\\\npays while frontend work does not. The remaining conservative bottleneck is\\\\nthat once the slot is occupied, every younger memory op stalls behind it\\\\neven if the next op is a normal-RAM load to a different word and the single\\\\ndmem port is ready.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the existing one-deep buffered-store retirement model, but stop\\\\nletting a pending normal-RAM store monopolize MEM. When the slot holds an\\\\nolder buffered RAM store, allow a younger legal aligned normal-RAM load\\\\nwith a different word-aligned address to issue on ready cycles ahead of the\\\\ndrain, while same-word loads, later stores, MMIO, and marker/UART accesses\\\\nstill wait. This reuses the already-profitable store-slot structure, adds\\\\nno IF-path logic and no extra retirement slots, and should recover IPC from\\\\nrandom dStall windows without the placement or timing cost of a deeper\\\\nqueue or long-op shadow machinery.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Rework pending-store-slot arbitration so a full slot does not always own dmem; permit younger aligned legal normal-RAM loads at a different word address to use the port on ready cycles, while conservatively blocking aliasing loads, later stores, and all MMIO.\\\"}, {\\\"fi...\\n  Line 19: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r6s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Static ID-Stage Backedge Redirect Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every predictor that touched IF `pc -> next_pc` lost badly, but the\\\\ncontrol-flow penalty is still real and the recent branch experiments also\\\\nshowed that learned tables or guaranteed hold bubbles are too expensive for\\\\nthis frontend. The remaining promising gap is a table-free predictor that\\\\nacts one stage later than IF but still one stage earlier than EX.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every predictor that touched IF `pc -> next_pc` lost badly, but the\\\\ncontrol-flow penalty is still real and the recent branch experiments also\\\\nshowed that learned tables or guaranteed hold bubbles are too expensive for\\\\nthis frontend. The remaining promising gap is a table-free predictor that\\\\nacts one stage later than IF but still one stage earlier than EX.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Reuse the decode work already done in ID to recognize legal backward\\\\nconditional branches and issue a static predicted-taken redirect there,\\\\nusing the existing B-immediate path and no BTB, BHT, or RAS state. Split\\\\nthe current EX `redirect` mechanism into \\\\\\\"set next PC\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"kill current\\\\nfetch\\\\\\\" so ID can steer the following fetch without squashing the branch\\\\ncurrently being decoded, then carry `predicted_taken` and\\\\n`predicted_target` into EX so untaken loop exits and other misses still\\\\nrecover precisely. This targets CoreMark's hot backward loop branches while\\\\navoiding the IF-side tag compares and history-table logic that previously\\\\nhurt Fmax.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend `id_ex_t` with compact branch-prediction metadata so EX can validate an ID-stage static backedge prediction against the resolved next PC.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect legal backward conditional branches from the current IF/ID instruction, reuse the existing immediate genera...\\n  Line 20: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r7s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Latched-Instruction Load-Use Stall Filter\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest remaining low-risk IPC loss in `cores/bench` is the overly\\\\nconservative load-use detector. After a LOAD in EX, `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\ncurrently stalls on any raw IF/ID `rs1` or `rs2` bitfield match, even when\\\\nthe younger opcode does not architecturally read that field. CoreMark's mix\\\\nincludes many I-type ALU ops, loads, JAL/JALR, LUI/AUIPC, FENCE, and illegal\\\\nor SYSTEM encodings, so those false matches can create avoidable one-cycle\\\\nbubbles without buying correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The strongest remaining low-risk IPC loss in `cores/bench` is the overly\\\\nconservative load-use detector. After a LOAD in EX, `hazard_unit.sv`\\\\ncurrently stalls on any raw IF/ID `rs1` or `rs2` bitfield match, even when\\\\nthe younger opcode does not architecturally read that field. CoreMark's mix\\\\nincludes many I-type ALU ops, loads, JAL/JALR, LUI/AUIPC, FENCE, and illegal\\\\nor SYSTEM encodings, so those false matches can create avoidable one-cycle\\\\nbubbles without buying correctness.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the optimization fully local to hazard detection: pass the latched\\\\nIF/ID instruction into `hazard_unit.sv`, derive compact `uses_rs1` and\\\\n`uses_rs2` bits from opcode and funct3 there, and only raise the load-use\\\\nbubble when the younger instruction truly consumes the matching source\\\\nregister. Do not modify decode-stage source addresses or values, and do not\\\\ntouch RVFI source reporting. This stays off the fetch `pc -> next_pc`\\\\ncritical path, preserves the existing redirect and memory-stall precedence,\\\\nand should finally realize the IPC gain that the earlier broken load-use\\\\nattempts were targeting without repeating their contract bug.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire the full latched IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit so sourc...\\n  Line 21: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r7s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Covered Same-Word Store-Slot Load Forwarding\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core has already shown that MEM-side decoupling pays, but\\\\nthe pending-store slot is still conservative for same-word aliases. When an\\\\nolder buffered RAM store already determines every byte a younger load wants,\\\\nthe core still stalls until the physical store drains, throwing away cycles\\\\nunder the benchmark's random dmem backpressure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core has already shown that MEM-side decoupling pays, but\\\\nthe pending-store slot is still conservative for same-word aliases. When an\\\\nolder buffered RAM store already determines every byte a younger load wants,\\\\nthe core still stalls until the physical store drains, throwing away cycles\\\\nunder the benchmark's random dmem backpressure.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing one-entry RAM store slot so a younger legal aligned\\\\nnormal-RAM load to the same word can complete directly from slot data when\\\\nthe slot's byte mask fully covers the load's requested bytes. Keep the\\\\ncurrent different-word bypass, keep MMIO and marker accesses unbuffered, and\\\\nkeep partial-overlap cases conservative by waiting behind the slot unless\\\\nevery loaded byte is known from the buffered store. This stays off the\\\\ntiming-sensitive frontend, adds no extra retirement slot or queue depth, and\\\\nturns more dStall windows into useful forward progress with strictly ordered\\\\nRVFI-visible memory behavior.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect same-word loads whose requested byte lanes are fully covered by the pending store slot, forward the load result from buffered store data, and keep partial-overlap, later-store, and MMIO cases on the existing conservative ordering path.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Replace the current same-word-load-must-wait ...\\n  Line 22: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r7s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Learned EX-Stage Loop Hold Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every predictor that steered fetch earlier regressed because this core's\\\\nfrontend is extremely sensitive to extra `pc -> next_pc` logic, yet the\\\\nbranch penalty is still real. The least-explored remaining predictor angle\\\\nis to leave target selection exactly where it is today and predict only\\\\nwhether a branch is worth pre-emptively holding for, so hot taken loop\\\\nbackedges lose less fetch bandwidth without paying a bubble on every static\\\\nbackedge.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every predictor that steered fetch earlier regressed because this core's\\\\nfrontend is extremely sensitive to extra `pc -> next_pc` logic, yet the\\\\nbranch penalty is still real. The least-explored remaining predictor angle\\\\nis to leave target selection exactly where it is today and predict only\\\\nwhether a branch is worth pre-emptively holding for, so hot taken loop\\\\nbackedges lose less fetch bandwidth without paying a bubble on every static\\\\nbackedge.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny learned direction-only predictor that is consulted only when a\\\\nconditional branch reaches EX. Instead of changing IF or ID target\\\\nselection, remember the most recent taken backward branch PC (or a similarly\\\\ntiny exact-PC taken history), and use that state to request the existing\\\\none-cycle frontend hold only for branches predicted taken. If the branch is\\\\nactually taken, the core keeps the current EX redirect path but avoids the\\\\nsecond wrong-path sequential fetch; if the branch falls through, the cost is\\\\nonly one bubble on a real misprediction rather than on every loop-closing\\\\nbranch. This stays off the fragile fetch critical path, removes the static\\\\nhold predictor's guaranteed-loop-exit penalty, and targets CoreMark's hot\\\\nrepeating loop backedges with much less control-path cost than IF- or\\\\nID-stage redirection.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"...\\n  Line 23: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r8s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Ready-Cycle MEM-to-EX Load Bypass\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent load-use-filter attempts suggest the bottleneck is real, but\\\\nqualifying source usage in `hazard_unit.sv` adds logic on the fragile\\\\nIF/ID control path and already regressed. The current core still pays a\\\\nguaranteed one-cycle bubble after every dependent load even when the older\\\\nload reaches MEM on a cycle where `io_dmemReady=1` and the data is already\\\\navailable.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The recent load-use-filter attempts suggest the bottleneck is real, but\\\\nqualifying source usage in `hazard_unit.sv` adds logic on the fragile\\\\nIF/ID control path and already regressed. The current core still pays a\\\\nguaranteed one-cycle bubble after every dependent load even when the older\\\\nload reaches MEM on a cycle where `io_dmemReady=1` and the data is already\\\\navailable.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Replace the fixed load-use bubble with a live MEM-to-EX forwarding path for\\\\nready loads. When a load is in MEM and `io_dmemReady=1`, `mem_stage.sv`\\\\nshould expose the aligned/sign-extended load result directly to EX, and the\\\\nforwarding muxes should prefer that value for matching rs1/rs2 instead of\\\\nthe EX/MEM address result. `hazard_unit.sv` can then stop injecting an\\\\nunconditional one-cycle load-use bubble and rely on the existing `mem_wait`\\\\nfreeze only when the load actually stalls on dmem. This keeps the change off\\\\nthe `pc -> next_pc` fetch path, reuses the already-correct MEM stall\\\\nmachinery, and should improve CoreMark IPC on hot dependent load chains\\\\nwithout the control-path cost that sank the earlier load-use filter ideas.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 5, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Wire a live MEM-stage load-forward channel into EX/forwarding control and stop treating every load-use pair as an automatic one-cycle bubble.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Expose ...\\n  Line 24: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r8s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Pending-Slot Same-Word Store Coalescing\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Frontend structural ideas have consistently hurt this core, while the only\\\\nrepeatable wins have come from keeping work off IF and exploiting the\\\\nexisting one-entry RAM store slot. After the recent different-word load\\\\nbypass improvement, the remaining conservative data-side stall is clustered\\\\nnormal-RAM stores to the same aligned word: once the slot is full, every\\\\nlater store waits even though its effect could be folded into the already\\\\nbuffered word without touching the timing-sensitive load or fetch paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Frontend structural ideas have consistently hurt this core, while the only\\\\nrepeatable wins have come from keeping work off IF and exploiting the\\\\nexisting one-entry RAM store slot. After the recent different-word load\\\\nbypass improvement, the remaining conservative data-side stall is clustered\\\\nnormal-RAM stores to the same aligned word: once the slot is full, every\\\\nlater store waits even though its effect could be folded into the already\\\\nbuffered word without touching the timing-sensitive load or fetch paths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing one-entry pending-store slot so a younger legal aligned\\\\nnormal-RAM store to the same word can retire by merging into the buffered\\\\nslot instead of stalling for the older drain. The slot would keep the oldest\\\\nword address, update its byte mask and per-byte data with younger-store\\\\nprecedence, and still block loads, MMIO, markers, and different-word stores\\\\nbehind the slot to preserve in-order visible memory behavior and RVFI\\\\nretirement semantics. This keeps the profitable single-slot structure,\\\\navoids the deeper queue and load-forwarding logic that already regressed,\\\\nand should recover dStall cycles on store clusters with much smaller routing\\\\ncost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem...\\n  Line 25: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r8s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-Stage Return-Address Stack Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every predictor aimed at conditional branches has regressed because it either\\\\ntouched the fragile IF `pc -> next_pc` path or paid a steady bubble cost.\\\\nReturns are a narrower control-flow class: CoreMark still executes many\\\\nfunction calls, but a return target can come from a tiny RAS instead of\\\\nbranch-immediate decode, BTB tag compare, or a learned hold heuristic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every predictor aimed at conditional branches has regressed because it either\\\\ntouched the fragile IF `pc -> next_pc` path or paid a steady bubble cost.\\\\nReturns are a narrower control-flow class: CoreMark still executes many\\\\nfunction calls, but a return target can come from a tiny RAS instead of\\\\nbranch-immediate decode, BTB tag compare, or a learned hold heuristic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Add a tiny non-speculative return-address stack and use it only for canonical\\\\ncall/return patterns (`JAL`/`JALR` writing `x1`/`x5`, and `JALR x0,x1/x5,0`\\\\nreturns). Update the RAS only from EX-resolved calls/returns, but let ID\\\\nissue an early next-PC override for predicted returns when the stack is\\\\nvalid. This keeps EX architecturally authoritative, avoids the failed\\\\nbackedge/BTB/hold schemes, and adds much less hot-path logic than general\\\\nbranch prediction because prediction is only consulted on decoded return\\\\ninstructions and uses a stored target instead of immediate construction or\\\\nbranch comparison.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend `id_ex_t` with compact prediction metadata such as `predicted_taken` and `predicted_target` so EX can validate an early return prediction.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect canonical return encodings in decode, consult a tiny RAS top supplied from top level, and emit an early return-only next-PC override only...\\n  Line 26: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r9s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Trim EX Redirect and DIV Snapshot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core already won by moving DIV/REM off the always-hot ALU\\\\npath, but EX still carries extra routing and state that no longer buys IPC.\\\\n`ex_stage.sv` snapshots a full `id_ex_t` across divides even though only a\\\\nsmall subset is consumed on completion, and `ex_mem_t` still includes branch\\\\nmetadata that never reaches later logic or RVFI. On a small FPGA, that kind\\\\nof dead payload and duplicate control-path arithmetic can cost Fmax even when\\\\narchitectural behavior is unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The current bench core already won by moving DIV/REM off the always-hot ALU\\\\npath, but EX still carries extra routing and state that no longer buys IPC.\\\\n`ex_stage.sv` snapshots a full `id_ex_t` across divides even though only a\\\\nsmall subset is consumed on completion, and `ex_mem_t` still includes branch\\\\nmetadata that never reaches later logic or RVFI. On a small FPGA, that kind\\\\nof dead payload and duplicate control-path arithmetic can cost Fmax even when\\\\narchitectural behavior is unchanged.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Shrink the EX-stage bookkeeping around redirects and divide completion rather\\\\nthan chasing more frontend or load-hazard IPC. Add a compact long-op metadata\\\\nbundle that stores only the fields a completed DIV/REM actually needs, remove\\\\nthe unused `branch_taken` and `branch_target` payload from `ex_mem_t`, and\\\\ncollapse the duplicate JALR target add path by reusing the existing `rs1+imm`\\\\nresult already formed in EX for that opcode. This should preserve the current\\\\ndivider and store-slot IPC gains while buying a modest Fmax increase from a\\\\nnarrower EX/MEM register, fewer long routed signals, and less duplicate EX\\\\ncontrol arithmetic.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Remove dead EX/MEM branch payload and add a compact di...\\n  Line 27: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r9s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Drain-Refill Store Slot Handoff\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Frontend structural ideas have repeatedly regressed, while the one-entry RAM\\\\nstore slot and the later different-word load bypass both improved fitness.\\\\nThe remaining data-side loss is that a full slot still inserts a bubble in\\\\nfront of every younger bufferable store, even on a cycle where the older\\\\nslot entry is already draining successfully.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Frontend structural ideas have repeatedly regressed, while the one-entry RAM\\\\nstore slot and the later different-word load bypass both improved fitness.\\\\nThe remaining data-side loss is that a full slot still inserts a bubble in\\\\nfront of every younger bufferable store, even on a cycle where the older\\\\nslot entry is already draining successfully.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep the profitable single pending-store slot, but let it rotate in one\\\\ncycle: when an older buffered normal-RAM store owns dmem and `dmem_ready=1`,\\\\nallow a younger legal aligned normal-RAM store in MEM to retire by\\\\nimmediately refilling the slot as the older entry drains. This is a\\\\nlighter-weight alternative to the failed two-entry queue and same-word\\\\ncoalescing ideas, because it preserves one-entry state, adds no per-byte\\\\nmerge logic, keeps loads/MMIO/markers ordered behind the slot, and targets\\\\nthe specific back-to-back store bubble that the current design still pays.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Rework pending-store-slot control so a draining slot can be refilled by the current younger legal aligned normal-RAM store in the same cycle, and relax `mem_wait` only for that oldest-drain/youngest-enqueue handoff while preserving single-entry ordering for loads and MMIO.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"test/test_pipeline.py\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Add a cocotb case for two consecutive bufferable RAM stores with injected `dmem_ready` sta...\\n  Line 28: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r9s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Speculative EX Backedge Redirect\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every prior predictor that tried to steer fetch before EX regressed, which\\\\npoints to the current combinational IF/ID control loop as the wrong place to\\\\nspend predictor logic. But conditional branches are still likely part of the\\\\nhot timing path because EX redirect currently depends on forwarded operands,\\\\nbranch comparison, and next-PC selection in the same cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every prior predictor that tried to steer fetch before EX regressed, which\\\\npoints to the current combinational IF/ID control loop as the wrong place to\\\\nspend predictor logic. But conditional branches are still likely part of the\\\\nhot timing path because EX redirect currently depends on forwarded operands,\\\\nbranch comparison, and next-PC selection in the same cycle.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Keep prediction entirely out of IF and ID. In EX, treat backward conditional\\\\nbranches as speculatively taken for redirect timing only: drive the same-cycle\\\\nredirect from the already-available backward target instead of waiting on the\\\\nfull branch-condition result, then use the registered EX/MEM branch metadata\\\\non the following cycle to issue a correction redirect only on mismatches.\\\\nThis turns prediction into a timing optimization rather than an early-fetch\\\\nscheme, so the common taken-backedge case can shorten the EX-to-PC path while\\\\nrare loop exits and forward-taken branches pay the extra recovery cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"low\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Generate speculative redirects for backward conditional branches from EX without waiting on the resolved branch condition, keep the actual branch outcome and architectural `pc_next` in EX/MEM, and add a correction-flush input so a later-stage mispredict kills wrong-path EX work instead of launching or retiring it.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core.sv\\\", \\\"de...\\n  Line 29: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r10s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Reset-Light Write-First Register File\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core still carries a synchronous clear across the entire 32x32\\\\ninteger register file, and the current unit test suite even enforces that\\\\nnon-architectural reset-all-zero behavior. On a small FPGA, that means more\\\\nthan a thousand datapath bits sit on the reset network and may block a\\\\ndenser LUTRAM-style implementation or at least add reset mux/routing cost.\\\\nCoreMark's startup code explicitly initializes the live-in software state\\\\n(`sp`, `a0`, `a1`), so paying that reset cost on every GPR is a poor fit\\\\nfor a fitness metric that is purely Fmax times steady-state IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core still carries a synchronous clear across the entire 32x32\\\\ninteger register file, and the current unit test suite even enforces that\\\\nnon-architectural reset-all-zero behavior. On a small FPGA, that means more\\\\nthan a thousand datapath bits sit on the reset network and may block a\\\\ndenser LUTRAM-style implementation or at least add reset mux/routing cost.\\\\nCoreMark's startup code explicitly initializes the live-in software state\\\\n(`sp`, `a0`, `a1`), so paying that reset cost on every GPR is a poor fit\\\\nfor a fitness metric that is purely Fmax times steady-state IPC.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework `rtl/reg_file.sv` so only the architecturally required x0 behavior is\\\\nguaranteed at reset, while x1-x31 become resetless storage with the existing\\\\nwrite-first bypass and x0 write-drop semantics unchanged. The expected win is\\\\nlower reset fanout, fewer reset muxes, and possibly better memory inference\\\\nor placement for the 2R1W GPR array, with no timed-loop IPC cost because the\\\\nbenchmark and selftest initialize the registers they rely on before first\\\\nuse. Update the cocotb contract accordingly: test x0 hardwiring, explicit\\\\nwrites, and boot-time correctness rather than insisting that every GPR read\\\\nas zero immediately after reset...\\n  Line 30: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r10s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-Precomputed PC-Relative Redirects\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core's structural wins have come from removing work from hot paths\\\\nwithout paying extra control latency, while frontend-heavy retimes and\\\\npredictors have consistently regressed. The current EX redirect path still\\\\ndoes more than it needs to: every branch and JAL recomputes `pc+imm`, and\\\\nevery jump/writeback path also regenerates `pc+4`, even though both values\\\\nare known one stage earlier from decode-time `pc` and `imm`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core's structural wins have come from removing work from hot paths\\\\nwithout paying extra control latency, while frontend-heavy retimes and\\\\npredictors have consistently regressed. The current EX redirect path still\\\\ndoes more than it needs to: every branch and JAL recomputes `pc+imm`, and\\\\nevery jump/writeback path also regenerates `pc+4`, even though both values\\\\nare known one stage earlier from decode-time `pc` and `imm`.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Repartition the pipeline so ID precomputes the PC-relative constants that do\\\\nnot depend on forwarded operands, then carry them through `id_ex_t` into EX.\\\\nSpecifically, add registered `pc_plus_4` and `pc_plus_imm` fields so EX keeps\\\\nbranch resolution in the same stage but no longer burns adders on the common\\\\nbranch/JAL redirect path; only JALR keeps its dedicated `rs1+imm` target add.\\\\nThis is a structural retime rather than a predictor: it stays off the fragile\\\\nIF `pc -> next_pc` path, avoids the extra redirect latency that hurt the full\\\\nIF/ID register attempt, and should buy Fmax by shortening the forwarded\\\\ncompare/redirect cone that CoreMark hits frequently in loop branches and\\\\ncalls.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Extend the ID/EX payload with precomputed PC-relative fields such as `pc_plus_4` and `pc_plus_imm` so the retimed r...\\n  Line 31: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r10s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Stall-Coalesced Backedge Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every bench predictor so far has regressed when it changed the always-hot\\\\nfetch/redirect path or injected a steady bubble, but this core already wins\\\\nwhen it exploits cycles that were going to be lost to backpressure anyway.\\\\nA branch predictor that only engages while decode is already pinned behind\\\\n`mem_wait` or a long EX op can try to hide taken-loop recovery inside those\\\\npre-existing dead cycles instead of paying extra ready-cycle timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Every bench predictor so far has regressed when it changed the always-hot\\\\nfetch/redirect path or injected a steady bubble, but this core already wins\\\\nwhen it exploits cycles that were going to be lost to backpressure anyway.\\\\nA branch predictor that only engages while decode is already pinned behind\\\\n`mem_wait` or a long EX op can try to hide taken-loop recovery inside those\\\\npre-existing dead cycles instead of paying extra ready-cycle timing cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Detect legal backward conditional branches in ID, but do not let them steer\\\\nfetch on normal flowing cycles. Instead, when such a branch is sitting in\\\\ndecode and the frontend is already frozen by `mem_wait` or `ex_long_busy`,\\\\nuse that otherwise-dead cycle to speculatively repoint the PC to the\\\\nbackedge target and carry `predicted_taken` plus `predicted_target` in\\\\n`id_ex_t`. When the stall clears, fetch resumes from the predicted target;\\\\nEX later validates the branch and suppresses its normal redirect on a\\\\ncorrect predicted-taken backedge, or issues a single correction redirect to\\\\n`pc+4` on loop exit or any miss. This keeps prediction off the fragile\\\\nready-cycle IF path, avoids the guaranteed bubble cost of the earlier hold\\\\npredictors, and specifically tries to turn existing dmem/div stall windows\\\\ninto hidden branch-recovery time.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"lo...\\n  Line 32: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-001-r11s0\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Control-Only Reset For Pipeline Payloads\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The regfile reset-light win strongly suggests this FPGA build is sensitive\\\\nto reset fanout and reset muxing, not just steady-state datapath logic.\\\\n`cores/bench` still resets several wide pipeline and helper registers a\\\\nfull-struct-at-a-time even though most of those 32-bit payload fields only\\\\nmatter when a separate valid/control bit says the entry is live.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The regfile reset-light win strongly suggests this FPGA build is sensitive\\\\nto reset fanout and reset muxing, not just steady-state datapath logic.\\\\n`cores/bench` still resets several wide pipeline and helper registers a\\\\nfull-struct-at-a-time even though most of those 32-bit payload fields only\\\\nmatter when a separate valid/control bit says the entry is live.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Rework the interstage and helper state so reset only initializes the bits\\\\nthat can cause architectural work or false hazards: valid flags, control\\\\nfields, destination/source tags, divider active state, pending-store valid,\\\\nand RVFI order. Leave the wide datapath payloads resetless and mask them\\\\nwhenever the corresponding entry is invalid. This keeps the existing\\\\nsingle-issue/RVFI contract and steady-state IPC unchanged, but should cut\\\\nreset routing and reset-data muxing across hundreds of bits, which is a\\\\ngood fit for the Tang Nano 20K after the successful reset-light regfile\\\\nchange.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 6, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Change the ID/EX reset and flush behavior to clear only safe control/tag bits plus `valid`, instead of zeroing the entire payload bundle.\\\"}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Apply the same control-only reset strategy to EX/MEM and divider snapshot state, keeping only live/side-effect metadata resettable while leaving wide operand/result payloads resetles...\\n  Line 33: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-002-r11s1\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"Different-Word Store Bypass Around Pending Slot\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"structural\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Frontend structural ideas have repeatedly regressed on this core, while the\\\\nonly durable structural wins came from the MEM-side pending-store slot and\\\\nits later different-word load bypass. The remaining obvious stall pattern is\\\\nback-to-back normal-RAM stores: once one stalled store occupies the slot, a\\\\nyounger different-word store still bubbles even on a later cycle when dmem\\\\nis ready, despite having no register result and no alias with the buffered\\\\nword.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"Frontend structural ideas have repeatedly regressed on this core, while the\\\\nonly durable structural wins came from the MEM-side pending-store slot and\\\\nits later different-word load bypass. The remaining obvious stall pattern is\\\\nback-to-back normal-RAM stores: once one stalled store occupies the slot, a\\\\nyounger different-word store still bubbles even on a later cycle when dmem\\\\nis ready, despite having no register result and no alias with the buffered\\\\nword.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Extend the existing one-entry buffered-store machinery so a pending older\\\\nnormal-RAM store no longer blocks every younger store. Keep the current\\\\ndifferent-word load bypass, and add the symmetric different-word store case:\\\\nwhen the slot is full, allow a younger legal aligned normal-RAM store to a\\\\ndifferent word to issue directly on ready cycles ahead of the drain, while\\\\nsame-word stores, aliasing loads, MMIO/UART/marker accesses, and unready\\\\ncycles still remain ordered behind the slot. This reuses the already\\\\nprofitable non-aliasing memory-bypass idea without adding a second queue\\\\nentry, same-cycle drain/refill coupling, or frontend logic, so it could\\\\nrecover store-cluster dStall bubbles with much less placement risk than the\\\\nfailed deeper or smarter store-buffer variants.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 4, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"...\\n  Line 34: {\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20260515-003-r11s2\\\", \\\"title\\\": \\\"ID-Stage Exact JAL Redirect Predictor\\\", \\\"category\\\": \\\"predictor\\\", \\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core's predictor regressions all came from spending extra logic on\\\\nconditional control flow: IF-path decode/BTB/RAS lookups, ID-stage branch\\\\nfeedback, or EX-stage hold/correction machinery. `JAL` is a much cleaner\\\\npredictor target because it is unconditionally taken and its target is known\\\\nexactly from `pc + imm`, so the core can recover call and unconditional-jump\\\\nbubbles without paying branch-compare or history-table cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": {\\\"motivation\\\": \\\"The bench core's predictor regressions all came from spending extra logic on\\\\nconditional control flow: IF-path decode/BTB/RAS lookups, ID-stage branch\\\\nfeedback, or EX-stage hold/correction machinery. `JAL` is a much cleaner\\\\npredictor target because it is unconditionally taken and its target is known\\\\nexactly from `pc + imm`, so the core can recover call and unconditional-jump\\\\nbubbles without paying branch-compare or history-table cost.\\\\n\\\", \\\"hypothesis\\\": \\\"Split the redirect path so decoded `JAL` instructions can steer fetch one\\\\nstage earlier from ID, while conditional branches and `JALR` stay on the\\\\ncurrent EX-resolution path. The key point is that this is an exact predictor,\\\\nnot a speculative one: ID already has the opcode, immediate, and PC, so it\\\\ncan issue a guaranteed-correct redirect for `JAL` without adding BTB/BHT/RAS\\\\nstate or the correction plumbing that hurt prior branch-predictor attempts.\\\\nIf CoreMark's helper calls and unconditional jumps are frequent enough, this\\\\nshould recover a control-flow bubble at low timing cost because the new logic\\\\nstays off the fragile IF `pc -> next_pc` path on ordinary fallthrough cycles\\\\nand avoids the branch-direction machinery that repeatedly regressed.\\\\n\\\", \\\"expected_impact\\\": {\\\"fitness_delta_pct\\\": 3, \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"medium\\\"}, \\\"changes\\\": [{\\\"file\\\": \\\"rtl/id_stage.sv\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Detect `JAL` in decode, form an early exact ...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":34,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"\\\"id\\\":\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20260515-003-r(7|8|9|10|11)s2\\\"|implementation_notes|outcome|fitness\",\"time\":{\"start\":1778828994155,\"end\":1778828994170}},\"metadata\":{\"openai\":{\"itemId\":\"fc_0e49bb6ccc2dd79a016a06c6c1fb388199a1d4b2b3d32212bb\"}},\"id\":\"prt_e2a786669001eedQ5V3xkWHS14\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d588058affeZWwx64D7A1sL53\",\"messageID\":\"msg_e2a78456e001fjtMVEAvQ3ogX6\"}}\n./bench/gpt-5_4_xhigh/rep1/agent.log:1488:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1778829106700,\"sessionID\":\"ses_1d588058affeZWwx64D7A1sL53\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_0Z3Jsj4nSlfPZnejDInc8o6H\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"CoreMark|coremark|ITERATIONS|start_time|stop_time\",\"path\":\"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/bench/programs\",\"include\":\"*\"},\"output\":\"Found 238 matches (showing first 100)\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/bench/programs/coremark/zephyr/module.yml:\\n  Line 1: name: coremark\\n\\n\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep1/bench/programs/coremark/simple/core_portme.c:\\n  Line 21: #include \\\"coremark.h\\\"\\n\\n  Line 38: volatile ee_s32 seed4_volatile = ITERATIONS;\\n\\n  Line 63: static CORETIMETYPE start_time_val, stop_time_val;\\n\\n  Line 65: /* Function : start_time\\n\\n  Line 74: start_time(void)\\n\\n  Line 76:     GETMYTIME(&start_time_val);\\n\\n  Line 78: /* Function : stop_time\\n\\n  Line 87: stop_time(void)\\n\\n  Line 89:     GETMYTIME(&stop_time_val);\\n\\n  Line 104:         = (CORE_TICKS)(MYTIMEDIFF(stop_time_val, start_time_val));\\n\\n\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4_xhigh-rep