s fail across the ISA.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Retirement-trained ABI return stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current core resolves JALR returns in EX, so every correctly formed\\nfunction return first fetches sequential instructions and then flushes the\\ntwo-entry queue.  General BTB/BHT and broad static prediction already hurt\\nthis fixture through timing or exit-branch mispredictions.  ABI returns are\\na narrower opportunity because their target is an exact call-stack value.\\nThe previous RAS attempts trained from speculative queue activity; keeping\\npredictor state tied to retirement should avoid that desynchronization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core resolves JALR returns in EX, so every correctly formed\\nfunction return first fetches sequential instructions and then flushes the\\ntwo-entry queue.  General BTB/BHT and broad static prediction already hurt\\nthis fixture through timing or exit-branch mispredictions.  ABI returns are\\na narrower opportunity because their target is an exact call-stack value.\\nThe previous RAS attempts trained from speculative queue activity; keeping\\npredictor state tied to retirement should avoid that desynchronization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small return-address stack used only for the exact RV32 ABI return\\nform JALR x0, x1, 0.  Push pc+4 when a legal JAL ra instruction retires and\\npop when a legal ABI return retires, so nested calls, stalls, and wrong-path\\nqueue contents cannot corrupt the predictor state.  At queue-head consume,\\nuse a nonempty RAS top to atomically replace the younger sequential queue\\nentries and set fetch_pc_q to the predicted return target; carry a\\npredicted-return marker through ID/EX and suppress EX recovery only when\\nthe resolved aligned JALR target exactly matches the prediction.  If the\\nstack is empty or the target mismatches, retain the existing EX redirect\\npath and architectural result.  Do not predict conditional branches,\\ndirect JALs, or other JALR forms, and keep predictor target selection out of\\nthe normal fetch lookup by using the registered queue transaction path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a compact resettable return-address stack with retirement push/pop updates, exact ABI-return qualification, top-of-stack prediction, and empty-stack handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add return-prediction-valid and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect retirement-based call/return training, queue-head return lookup, atomic prediction control, and EX validation without changing the RVFI or memory interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered, atomic queue replacement for a predicted ABI return, updating fetch_pc_q and queued PCs consistently while preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the return-prediction metadata with the consumed instruction and expose the exact return qualification needed by the predictor.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate predicted return targets against the resolved JALR target and issue recovery only on mismatch, preserving alignment traps and RVFI pc_next semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a compact resettable return-address stack with retirement push/pop updates, exact ABI-return qualification, top-of-stack prediction, and empty-stack handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add return-prediction-valid and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect retirement-based call/return training, queue-head return lookup, atomic prediction control, and EX validation without changing the RVFI or memory interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered, atomic queue replacement for a predicted ABI return, updating fetch_pc_q and queued PCs consistently while preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the return-prediction metadata with the consumed instruction and expose the exact return qualification needed by the predictor.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate predicted return targets against the resolved JALR target and issue recovery only on mismatch, preserving alignment traps and RVFI pc_next semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Carry PC plus four into execute\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The EX stage recomputes the sequential next PC and the JAL return address\\nwith 32-bit additions for every pipeline instruction.  Those additions\\nsit alongside the ALU, forwarding, and control-flow logic in the stage that\\nis most sensitive to timing and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX stage recomputes the sequential next PC and the JAL return address\\nwith 32-bit additions for every pipeline instruction.  Those additions\\nsit alongside the ALU, forwarding, and control-flow logic in the stage that\\nis most sensitive to timing and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute PC+4 once while the instruction is entering ID and carry that\\nvalue in the ID/EX bundle.  Use the registered value for sequential\\npc_next, JAL's rd writeback value, and the sequential side of the existing\\nmisalignment-trap bookkeeping, while leaving branch and JALR target\\nformation, forwarding, flushes, and retirement unchanged.  Moving this\\nadder out of EX should shorten the critical path with only a small amount\\nof pipeline storage and no IPC or ISA change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a PC+4 field to the ID/EX pipeline bundle so execute receives the precomputed sequential next-PC value.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and register in.pc + 32'd4 alongside the decoded instruction, resetting it with the existing ID/EX bubble state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace EX-stage sequential PC and JAL return-address additions with the carried PC+4 value while preserving branch/JALR targets, traps, and RVFI pc_next behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a PC+4 field to the ID/EX pipeline bundle so execute receives the precomputed sequential next-PC value.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and register in.pc + 32'd4 alongside the decoded instruction, resetting it with the existing ID/EX bubble state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace EX-stage sequential PC and JAL return-address additions with the carried PC+4 value while preserving branch/JALR targets, traps, and RVFI pc_next behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 374.68, \"delta_pct\": -19.0, \"vs_baseline\": 32.48, \"fmax_mhz\": 162.97, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10238, \"ff\": 2028, \"seeds\": [154.08, 162.97, 170.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:19:15.282649Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (regression, -19.00%): Moving the PC+4 adder from EX into ID and carrying a 32-bit result through ID/EX regressed CoreMark, showing that stage relocation can shift timing/routing pressure into decode and bundle transport instead of shortening the critical path.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed two-slot elastic fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two-entry prefetch queue is the strongest structural result so far,\\nreaching 462.59 fitness, but its compact-count implementation performs\\ncount arithmetic, full/empty decoding, and slot-promotion control on every\\nfetch transaction.  The failed four-entry FIFO shows that adding pointer\\nwidth and indexed muxing is not a good trade on this FPGA.  A smaller\\ncontrol topology can target the fetch-side timing and routing cost while\\nretaining the proven queue depth and stall overlap.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two-entry prefetch queue is the strongest structural result so far,\\nreaching 462.59 fitness, but its compact-count implementation performs\\ncount arithmetic, full/empty decoding, and slot-promotion control on every\\nfetch transaction.  The failed four-entry FIFO shows that adding pointer\\nwidth and indexed muxing is not a good trade on this FPGA.  A smaller\\ncontrol topology can target the fetch-side timing and routing cost while\\nretaining the proven queue depth and stall overlap.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the count-based two-entry queue control with a fixed two-slot\\nelastic buffer using explicit valid bits and direct slot-0/slot-1\\ntransitions.  Keep the independent sequential fetch PC and the existing\\none-word-per-cycle refill behavior, but eliminate count increment/decrement\\nand general FIFO indexing from the normal path.  A normal consume promotes\\nslot 1 only when valid, a consume-plus-refill replaces or appends directly,\\nand redirect atomically clears both slots while accepting the target word\\ninto slot 0 when io_imemReady is high.  Preserve the exact queue-head PC and\\ninstruction pairing, imem-stall behavior, redirect priority, and all valid\\nsemantics so the backend and RVFI timing remain unchanged.  The reduced\\ncontrol fanout and simpler next-state muxing should improve Fmax without\\npaying the area and timing cost of a deeper queue.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Restructure the two-entry prefetch queue around explicit slot-valid state and direct normal, simultaneous refill, and redirect transitions while preserving PC/instruction atomicity and handshake behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Restructure the two-entry prefetch queue around explicit slot-valid state and direct normal, simultaneous refill, and redirect transitions while preserving PC/instruction atomicity and handshake behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 310.21, \"delta_pct\": -32.94, \"vs_baseline\": 9.68, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.93, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9742, \"ff\": 1999, \"seeds\": [134.93, 136.72, 132.31], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:16:05.076645Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (regression, -32.94%): Replacing compact two-entry queue counting with explicit valid-bit pop/push promotion logic regressed CoreMark, showing that extra slot-valid decode and simultaneous-transition muxing can outweigh removing small arithmetic on FPGA fetch control paths.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Split the RVFI retirement order counter\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The retirement order is maintained by a single 64-bit incrementer in the\\nwriteback/RVFI boundary.  That counter is not part of architectural\\nexecution, but its feedback path can still consume a long carry chain and\\nLUT routing resources on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The retirement order is maintained by a single 64-bit incrementer in the\\nwriteback/RVFI boundary.  That counter is not part of architectural\\nexecution, but its feedback path can still consume a long carry chain and\\nLUT routing resources on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the monolithic 64-bit order increment with separate low and high\\n32-bit counters.  On each valid retirement, increment the low half and\\nincrement the high half only when the low half is 0xffffffff; expose their\\nconcatenation as the unchanged 64-bit rvfi_order_0.  This should shorten\\nthe common feedback path to a 32-bit carry chain and reduce RVFI-side LUT\\npressure without changing retirement timing, pipeline behavior, or the\\nrequired exact +1 sequence across 64-bit rollover.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Split rvfi_order_q into low/high 32-bit state with explicit rollover carry, preserving reset behavior and the exact 64-bit RVFI order output.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Split rvfi_order_q into low/high 32-bit state with explicit rollover carry, preserving reset behavior and the exact 64-bit RVFI order output.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 325.08, \"delta_pct\": -29.73, \"vs_baseline\": 14.94, \"fmax_mhz\": 141.4, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10166, \"ff\": 1981, \"seeds\": [141.4, 135.67, 144.32], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:35:04.081415Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r10s0 (regression, -29.73%): Splitting the RVFI 64-bit counter into low/high halves did not guarantee a shorter synthesized feedback path, and the low-half terminal compare plus high-half enable can add enough FPGA control/routing cost to overwhelm the intended carry-chain reduction.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Response-gated load-use execute bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The five-stage core currently inserts a full front-end bubble for every\\nload-use pair, even when the data bus returns the load in its first\\navailable cycle.  CoreMark's pointer-heavy code creates enough dependent\\nloads that this costs iterations/cycle, while the existing EX/MEM-to-EX\\nbypass already provides the natural structural point for a response path.\\nThe prior direct-load-bypass attempt was unsafe because it released the\\ninterlock without proving that a usable same-cycle response existed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The five-stage core currently inserts a full front-end bubble for every\\nload-use pair, even when the data bus returns the load in its first\\navailable cycle.  CoreMark's pointer-heavy code creates enough dependent\\nloads that this costs iterations/cycle, while the existing EX/MEM-to-EX\\nbypass already provides the natural structural point for a response path.\\nThe prior direct-load-bypass attempt was unsafe because it released the\\ninterlock without proving that a usable same-cycle response existed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the unconditional one-cycle load-use bubble with a response-gated\\ninterlock.  Let the dependent instruction enter ID/EX, then hold that\\ninstruction and the outstanding EX/MEM load only while io_dmemReady is low.\\nWhen the load is valid, aligned, addressed to a nonzero destination, and\\nio_dmemReady is high, forward the already extracted load value directly\\nfrom MEM to the dependent EX operands; otherwise retain the stall or use\\nthe normal register/MEM-WB path.  Keep EX/MEM ALU forwarding disabled for\\nloads, qualify the bypass with the exact memory response and load width,\\nand preserve misalignment traps, redirect behavior, RVFI ordering, and\\nstore/load bus side effects.  Removing the avoidable load-use bubble should\\nraise CoreMark IPC with only a small response mux and control change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with a response-aware hold that stalls only when an EX/MEM load has no valid dmem response, while retaining backend stall and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a distinct EX/MEM load-response forwarding selection and prevent the ordinary ALU-result bypass from forwarding a load address as data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a highest-priority, response-valid load-data operand path for dependent rs1/rs2 values, falling back safely when the load is misaligned or the bus is stalled.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the existing width/sign/alignment-correct load extraction as a same-cycle bypass value, qualified by a valid aligned load response.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect dmemReady, EX/MEM load metadata, the MEM response bypass, and the retimed hazard/forwarding controls without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the unconditional load-use bubble with a response-aware hold that stalls only when an EX/MEM load has no valid dmem response, while retaining backend stall and redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a distinct EX/MEM load-response forwarding selection and prevent the ordinary ALU-result bypass from forwarding a load address as data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a highest-priority, response-valid load-data operand path for dependent rs1/rs2 values, falling back safely when the load is misaligned or the bus is stalled.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the existing width/sign/alignment-correct load extraction as a same-cycle bypass value, qualified by a valid aligned load response.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect dmemReady, EX/MEM load metadata, the MEM response bypass, and the retimed hazard/forwarding controls without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Confidence-gated loop target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Conditional branches currently resolve in EX, so a taken loop branch\\ndiscards younger instructions from the two-entry fetch queue.  Static\\nbackward prediction mispredicts exits, while the earlier BTB/BHT attempt\\nput an expensive lookup on the fetch timing path.  CoreMark's repeated\\nloops make a small, trained target cache a narrower opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Conditional branches currently resolve in EX, so a taken loop branch\\ndiscards younger instructions from the two-entry fetch queue.  Static\\nbackward prediction mispredicts exits, while the earlier BTB/BHT attempt\\nput an expensive lookup on the fetch timing path.  CoreMark's repeated\\nloops make a small, trained target cache a narrower opportunity.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a two-entry tagged loop-target table trained only by EX-resolved\\nconditional branches.  Each entry stores the branch PC, its resolved\\ntaken target, and a saturating confidence state; predict taken only after\\nrepeated same-PC taken outcomes, and stop predicting after a not-taken\\noutcome has decayed the confidence.  The queue-head lookup uses the\\nregistered target from the table rather than computing a PC-plus-immediate\\nadder on the ordinary imem address path.  On a confident hit, atomically\\nreplace younger queue contents and advance fetch to the stored target;\\ncarry the prediction with the branch and let EX recover only when the\\nresolved next PC differs.  Keep reset, imem stalls, alignment traps,\\nRVFI pc_next, and predictor training coordinated with the same queue\\ntransaction so untrained, exit, and unstable branches remain sequential.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a resettable two-entry tagged loop-target table with saturating confidence, stored targets, queue-head lookup, and EX outcome training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-valid, predicted-taken, and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the predictor, including queue-head lookup, EX training, prediction mismatch recovery, and complete RTL source integration.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform an atomic confident-loop prediction transaction that flushes younger entries and updates fetch_pc_q from the stored target while preserving redirect and imem-ready priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate loop prediction metadata alongside the consumed instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the stored prediction with the resolved conditional-branch next PC and redirect only on mismatch, preserving traps and RVFI semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a resettable two-entry tagged loop-target table with saturating confidence, stored targets, queue-head lookup, and EX outcome training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-valid, predicted-taken, and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the predictor, including queue-head lookup, EX training, prediction mismatch recovery, and complete RTL source integration.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform an atomic confident-loop prediction transaction that flushes younger entries and updates fetch_pc_q from the stored target while preserving redirect and imem-ready priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate loop prediction metadata alongside the consumed instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the stored prediction with the resolved conditional-branch next PC and redirect only on mismatch, preserving traps and RVFI semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 196.39, \"delta_pct\": -57.55, \"vs_baseline\": -30.56, \"fmax_mhz\": 85.7, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4363691, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 11393, \"ff\": 2156, \"seeds\": [85.7, 93.35, 84.61], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:42:31.493583Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -57.55%): Confidence gating did not make a two-entry loop-target predictor cheap enough because queue-head `if_head_*` lookup, `predict_fire` target selection, and carried prediction metadata still widen the fetch/redirect control path; predictor selectivity cannot offset front-end timing overhead unless the prediction transaction is isolated from ordinary `imem_addr` selection.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:33:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Register the memory request/response boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage leaves the EX/MEM address on the external data-memory\\nport and feeds the combinational RAM result, lane extraction, and MEM/WB\\ncapture in one long timing path.  That path is especially costly on the\\nTang Nano wrapper, where the dmem array is part of the synthesized design.\\nA small elastic boundary can isolate address generation from RAM response\\ntiming without adding storage to the instruction front end.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage leaves the EX/MEM address on the external data-memory\\nport and feeds the combinational RAM result, lane extraction, and MEM/WB\\ncapture in one long timing path.  That path is especially costly on the\\nTang Nano wrapper, where the dmem array is part of the synthesized design.\\nA small elastic boundary can isolate address generation from RAM response\\ntiming without adding storage to the instruction front end.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split memory access into a registered request stage and a response stage.\\nCapture the aligned address, write data, byte enables, load width/sign\\nmetadata, and the complete RVFI payload in a one-entry memory-request\\nregister; drive the dmem ports only from that stable register and capture\\nthe returned word in the following response register.  Keep a bypass path\\nfor non-memory instructions and use valid/ready interlocks so memory\\nrequests remain ordered, held stable across io_dmemReady stalls, and can\\naccept one request per cycle after filling.  Preserve precise traps,\\nload/store masks, RVFI retirement order, and the existing two-entry fetch\\nqueue while shortening the EX-to-RAM and RAM-to-writeback timing cones.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the registered memory-request/response payload and valid metadata needed to preserve ordering and all RVFI fields across the split memory boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the elastic request and response stages, stable dmem signaling during stalls, registered load extraction, and precise misalignment handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate memory-stage ready/valid flow with the existing EX/MEM, MEM/WB, forwarding, and single-channel RVFI retirement boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add conservative backpressure for a full or waiting memory request while allowing the prefetch queue and independent non-memory pipeline flow to continue safely.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the registered memory-request/response payload and valid metadata needed to preserve ordering and all RVFI fields across the split memory boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the elastic request and response stages, stable dmem signaling during stalls, registered load extraction, and precise misalignment handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate memory-stage ready/valid flow with the existing EX/MEM, MEM/WB, forwarding, and single-channel RVFI retirement boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add conservative backpressure for a full or waiting memory request while allowing the prefetch queue and independent non-memory pipeline flow to continue safely.\"}], \"outcome\": \"