ed and unsigned RV32M forms. Even though the software\\nselects only one operation, synthesis can retain several wide divider cones,\\nincreasing LUT usage and the EX critical path on the Tang Nano 20K. The\\nprevious divide-sharing attempt did not remove those operators, so a more\\nexplicit shared datapath is needed.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Normalize the operands to unsigned magnitudes and compute one unsigned\\nquotient for all DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU operations. Recover the remainder with\\nan algebraic magnitude multiply-subtract, then apply the required quotient\\nand remainder signs, divide-by-zero behavior, and INT_MIN/-1 overflow rule.\\nKeep the existing shared multiply result for MUL-family operations and leave\\nthe RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged. Removing redundant divider\\ncones should reduce LUT/routing pressure and improve Fmax without changing\\npipeline latency or CoreMark instruction scheduling.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the independent signed/unsigned DIV and REM operators with one unsigned magnitude quotient and derived remainder, preserving exact RV32M corner semantics and the ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend RV32M vectors to cover signed magnitude normalization, quotient/remainder sign restoration, divide-by-zero, and INT_MIN divided by -1.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the independent signed/unsigned DIV and REM operators with one unsigned magnitude quotient and derived remainder, preserving exact RV32M corner semantics and the ALTOPS branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend RV32M vectors to cover signed magnitude normalization, quotient/remainder sign restoration, divide-by-zero, and INT_MIN divided by -1.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 130.52, \"delta_pct\": -71.78, \"vs_baseline\": -53.85, \"fmax_mhz\": 56.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 8090, \"ff\": 1985, \"seeds\": [57.31, 56.77, 55.55], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T18:18:41.233173Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r12s0 (regression, -71.78%): Reconstructing REM with a quotient-times-divisor multiply after a shared `/` added another wide arithmetic cone plus sign/magnitude logic, so algebraic divider sharing can worsen FPGA timing unless the quotient and remainder are produced by one genuinely shared or iterative datapath.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:37:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Fetch-response direct-JAL target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current front end fetches sequentially until EX resolves every JAL,\\nthen flushes the two-entry queue and pays a redirect recovery penalty.\\nBroad BTB/BHT and loop predictors previously hurt Fmax because lookup and\\ntarget selection were placed on the ordinary imem address path.  Direct\\nJALs are an attractive narrower target because their direction and target\\nare encoded in the accepted instruction itself, with no training or aliasing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current front end fetches sequentially until EX resolves every JAL,\\nthen flushes the two-entry queue and pays a redirect recovery penalty.\\nBroad BTB/BHT and loop predictors previously hurt Fmax because lookup and\\ntarget selection were placed on the ordinary imem address path.  Direct\\nJALs are an attractive narrower target because their direction and target\\nare encoded in the accepted instruction itself, with no training or aliasing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a zero-table direct-JAL predictor in the fetch-response transaction.\\nWhen an imem word is accepted and is a legal, aligned JAL, compute its\\ntarget in the sequential queue-update path, discard only queue entries\\nyounger than that JAL, and set the independent fetch PC to the target for\\nthe next request.  Carry a predicted-valid bit and target with the JAL\\nthrough every queue pop, refill, and ID/EX transfer; EX must suppress its\\nnormal JAL redirect only when that marker and target match, while retaining\\nthe normal redirect and precise misalignment behavior for all misses.\\nThis removes direct-call bubbles without adding a tag lookup or mux to the\\nsteady-state imem address path, and the atomic queue update avoids the\\ndesynchronization seen in earlier direct-JAL experiments.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-valid and predicted-target metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX queue payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize accepted aligned JAL responses, atomically invalidate younger queue entries, and redirect fetch_pc_q to the computed target without changing the normal imem address mux path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the direct-JAL prediction marker and target with the decoded instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the carried direct-JAL prediction with the resolved target, suppress only an exact predicted redirect, and preserve trap, pc_next, and recovery behavior on misses.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-valid and predicted-target metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX queue payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize accepted aligned JAL responses, atomically invalidate younger queue entries, and redirect fetch_pc_q to the computed target without changing the normal imem address mux path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the direct-JAL prediction marker and target with the decoded instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the carried direct-JAL prediction with the resolved target, suppress only an exact predicted redirect, and preserve trap, pc_next, and recovery behavior on misses.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 451.97, \"delta_pct\": -2.3, \"vs_baseline\": 59.81, \"fmax_mhz\": 194.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4296081, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10302, \"ff\": 2090, \"seeds\": [195.12, 193.39, 194.17], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T18:22:11.241176Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r12s2 (regression, -2.30%): A table-free direct-JAL predictor still regressed when every accepted response added opcode/target arithmetic plus prediction metadata through the two-entry queue and ID/EX, showing that response-side speculation must earn back its queue-control and bundle-routing cost even when it leaves the steady-state imem address path untouched.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:38:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r13s0\", \"title\": \"Partition the fast ALU path from RV32M\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current combinational ALU puts simple RV32I operations and the wide\\nshared multiplier/divider results behind one operation-selection cone.\\nCoreMark is dominated by the simple path, while the RV32M hardware still\\ncontributes substantial routing and select fan-in on the EX critical path.\\nA targeted datapath partition can improve timing without adding a pipeline\\nstage or changing instruction latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current combinational ALU puts simple RV32I operations and the wide\\nshared multiplier/divider results behind one operation-selection cone.\\nCoreMark is dominated by the simple path, while the RV32M hardware still\\ncontributes substantial routing and select fan-in on the EX critical path.\\nA targeted datapath partition can improve timing without adding a pipeline\\nstage or changing instruction latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split alu.sv into an explicitly separated fast result for ADD/SUB, logical,\\ncompare, shift, and LUI operations and an RV32M result containing the\\nexisting shared multiplier/sign-correction and exact DIV/REM behavior.\\nSelect between those two results with a validated M-operation range, while\\nretaining the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions and all divide corner cases.\\nThis should keep the common EX path away from the wide M-extension result\\nmuxing and improve Tang Nano timing with no IPC or RVFI changes.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Partition the ALU into fast RV32I and RV32M result cones with an explicit M-operation select, preserving shared multiply, divide/remainder semantics, ALTOPS behavior, and the combinational interface.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Partition the ALU into fast RV32I and RV32M result cones with an explicit M-operation select, preserving shared multiply, divide/remainder semantics, ALTOPS behavior, and the combinational interface.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 355.34, \"delta_pct\": -23.18, \"vs_baseline\": 25.64, \"fmax_mhz\": 154.56, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10341, \"ff\": 1986, \"seeds\": [154.56, 159.92, 146.78], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T18:41:17.116549Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r13s0 (regression, -23.18%): Splitting RV32I and RV32M into separate `always_comb` result cones did not isolate the CoreMark fast path because the wide multiply/divide hardware remains active and the explicit M-range result mux adds routing, so datapath partitioning must be validated against synthesized cones and placement rather than module boundaries.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:39:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Fall-through fetch response into decode\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The successful two-entry prefetch queue hides sustained instruction-bus\\nstalls, but its empty-to-nonempty transition still costs a bubble: a ready\\ninstruction is first written into slot zero and can only be consumed on the\\nfollowing cycle.  Under the benchmark's randomized iStall pattern, queue\\ndrains and redirect recovery make this refill latency recurring.  The core\\nhas substantial Fmax headroom, so recovering these front-end cycles is a\\nbetter structural target than adding more queue state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful two-entry prefetch queue hides sustained instruction-bus\\nstalls, but its empty-to-nonempty transition still costs a bubble: a ready\\ninstruction is first written into slot zero and can only be consumed on the\\nfollowing cycle.  Under the benchmark's randomized iStall pattern, queue\\ndrains and redirect recovery make this refill latency recurring.  The core\\nhas substantial Fmax headroom, so recovering these front-end cycles is a\\nbetter structural target than adding more queue state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a fall-through response path in the existing two-entry fetch queue.  When\\nthe queue is empty, the bus is ready, and decode is able to accept an\\ninstruction, present the current fetch response directly as the IF/ID view\\nand advance fetch_pc_q without storing and re-reading that word.  If decode\\nstalls, retain the normal enqueue behavior; if EX redirects, suppress the\\nbypass and atomically refill from the redirect target.  Keep the bypass\\ndisabled during redirect and propagate the effective availability signal to\\nhazard logic so load-use bubbles cannot consume or drop an unheld response.\\nThis removes empty-queue refill bubbles without changing the top-level bus,\\nsingle-retirement RVFI contract, or the proven compact queue steady state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an empty-queue, ready-response fall-through path with atomic consume-versus-enqueue handling, while preserving redirect flush and fetch PC updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the fall-through IF/ID valid view for consume and connect its effective availability to the existing pipeline control without changing RVFI ports.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat a ready fall-through instruction as non-empty for stall and flush decisions, retaining load-use, dmem-stall, and redirect priority.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an empty-queue, ready-response fall-through path with atomic consume-versus-enqueue handling, while preserving redirect flush and fetch PC updates.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the fall-through IF/ID valid view for consume and connect its effective availability to the existing pipeline control without changing RVFI ports.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat a ready fall-through instruction as non-empty for stall and flush decisions, retaining load-use, dmem-stall, and redirect priority.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 299.09, \"delta_pct\": -35.34, \"vs_baseline\": 5.75, \"fmax_mhz\": 123.02, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4113199, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10259, \"ff\": 1986, \"seeds\": [131.56, 118.82, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T18:44:09.288366Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r13s1 (regression, -35.34%): A fall-through fetch response from `imem_data`/`imem_ready` into IF/ID, plus effective-availability hazard logic, can widen the combinational fetch/decode control path enough to outweigh eliminating empty-queue refill bubbles, so bypasses must be validated against post-synthesis timing and benchmark IPC.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:40:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Transaction-safe return stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark contains frequent ABI function returns, but the current EX-only\\nJALR resolution flushes younger sequential fetches. Broad target predictors\\npreviously hurt Fmax, while the prior return-stack attempt was functionally\\nbroken because stack state and prediction metadata lost instruction identity\\nacross queue stalls and flushes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark contains frequent ABI function returns, but the current EX-only\\nJALR resolution flushes younger sequential fetches. Broad target predictors\\npreviously hurt Fmax, while the prior return-stack attempt was functionally\\nbroken because stack state and prediction metadata lost instruction identity\\nacross queue stalls and flushes.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a compact four-entry return-address stack that predicts only the exact\\nABI return form JALR x0, x1, 0 when a valid queue-head transaction has a\\nregistered nonempty stack top. Train it only at retirement: push the retiring\\npc+4 for direct JAL x1 calls and pop only for a retiring matching return, so\\nwrong-path instructions cannot mutate predictor state. Carry a prediction\\ntoken and target atomically with each queue entry through pop/refill and\\nID/EX; use one effective target for fetch PC, queue contents, and recovery.\\nEX suppresses redirect only for an aligned exact match, and all misses,\\nstalls, traps, and non-ABI JALRs retain the conservative path. Keep lookup\\noff the ordinary sequential imem-address critical path by issuing the\\nregistered target request only as an atomic return-prediction transaction.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a resettable four-entry retirement-trained RAS with strict call/return qualification, nonempty top lookup, and push/pop ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add prediction token, target, and transaction identity fields to IF/ID and ID/EX payloads so stalls and queue replacement cannot retag a return.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the RAS lookup and retirement training while preserving single-issue RVFI ordering, precise traps, and source-list inclusion.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add an atomic queue-head return-prediction request that uses the registered RAS target, invalidates younger entries, and keeps fetch PC and queued target identity consistent.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the return prediction token and target with the consumed instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare the resolved JALR return target with the carried prediction token and redirect only on mismatch or an unpredicted return, retaining alignment-trap behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a resettable four-entry retirement-trained RAS with strict call/return qualification, nonempty top lookup, and push/pop ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\"