{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"micro_opt\\\"|micro_opt|forward|prefetch|pipeline|critical|ALU|decode|hazard' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 120\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product and deriving MULH/MULHSU with sign-correction subtractions preserves RV32M results while eliminating duplicated multiplier hardware without adding pipeline latency.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Splitting EX into EX1/EX2 changes producer availability, so forwarding, valid/flush, and load-use hazard logic must be derived from the exact new stage timing or dependent instructions can corrupt architectural register writes.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 (regression, -12.37%): Static BTFNT in IF was too aggressive for CoreMark—backward-branch exits and weakly taken backward branches trigger EX-time redirect/flush penalties, so fetch-side speculation must be benchmark-profiled or made selective.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Gating load-use stalls with decoder-generated rs1/rs2-use metadata can remove a mandatory interlock and corrupt architectural registers; operand-use classification must be proven against every instruction's actual reads and IF/ID validity.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.29%): A two-entry instruction prefetch queue with an independent fetch PC lets instruction-bus backpressure overlap backend/data stalls, but redirect handling must atomically invalidate queued wrong-path entries.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -46.80%): A direct-mapped BTB/BHT can remain functionally correct yet lose heavily when its combinational tag/counter lookup and predicted-target mux sit on the IF next-PC path, so predictor timing must be isolated or kept off the fetch critical path.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.84%): Writing shared signed divide/remainder selection around separate unsigned `/` and `%` operators still computes both wide cones and adds magnitude/sign-correction logic, so RTL-level operand sharing is not sufficient to reduce divider cost without an explicitly shared or iterative datapath.\n- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (regression, -29.56%): Deepening the prefetch queue to a four-entry circular FIFO regressed despite preserving streaming semantics, showing that the indexed head mux and added pointer/count control can outweigh stall absorption when extra buffering is not the bottleneck.\n1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-a2934e6\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T14:36:03.815332Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share the multiplier across RV32M high-half operations\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently computes three independent 64-bit products on every\\ncombinational evaluation: signed-by-signed, unsigned-by-unsigned, and\\nsigned-by-unsigned. This duplicates multiplier hardware and contributes to\\nboth the LUT budget and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently computes three independent 64-bit products on every\\ncombinational evaluation: signed-by-signed, unsigned-by-unsigned, and\\nsigned-by-unsigned. This duplicates multiplier hardware and contributes to\\nboth the LUT budget and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 32x32 64-bit product.\\nDerive MULH and MULHSU high halves by subtracting the appropriate sign\\ncorrection terms from that product, while retaining the same low-half MUL\\nresult and the existing ALTOPS behavior. Sharing the multiplier should\\nreduce area and routing congestion, improving Fmax without adding cycles or\\nchanging the pipeline's RV32M latency.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one unsigned 64-bit product and sign-correction logic for MULH and MULHSU instead of three parallel multipliers, preserving ALTOPS and exact RV32M results.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one unsigned 64-bit product and sign-correction logic for MULH and MULHSU instead of three parallel multipliers, preserving ALTOPS and exact RV32M results.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 404.74, \"delta_pct\": 43.11, \"vs_baseline\": 43.11, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10073, \"ff\": 1860, \"seeds\": [181.79, 175.38, 185.53], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T14:43:31.330052Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product and deriving MULH/MULHSU with sign-correction subtractions preserves RV32M results while eliminating duplicated multiplier hardware without adding pipeline latency.\"}\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Split execute datapath into two pipeline stages\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline P&R critical path runs from the ID/EX register through the\\nforwarded-operand muxes and 32-bit EX arithmetic into EX/MEM, consuming\\nroughly 4.1-4.4 ns and limiting the worst seed to 127.03 MHz.  CoreMark is\\nalready close to the LUT budget, so improving timing through retiming is a\\nmore promising structural lever than widening the machine.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline P&R critical path runs from the ID/EX register through the\\nforwarded-operand muxes and 32-bit EX arithmetic into EX/MEM, consuming\\nroughly 4.1-4.4 ns and limiting the worst seed to 127.03 MHz.  CoreMark is\\nalready close to the LUT budget, so improving timing through retiming is a\\nmore promising structural lever than widening the machine.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retiming the execute path into EX1 and EX2 with an EX1-to-EX2 register will\\nseparate forwarding/operand selection from the ALU and branch-result cone.\\nKeep EX2 fully pipelined so it still accepts one instruction per cycle, and\\nupdate valid, redirect, load-use, and forwarding bookkeeping for the extra\\nstage.  The shorter combinational paths should raise Fmax enough to offset\\nthe additional taken-branch recovery cycle on CoreMark.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Retiming and control integration for the new EX1/EX2 pipeline stages, including redirect and retirement alignment.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the inter-stage payload needed to carry decoded operands, control, PC, and RVFI metadata across EX1.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split operand forwarding/selection from arithmetic and branch resolution with a registered EX1-to-EX2 boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retimed stall and flush rules for the extra execute stage and its delayed branch recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Adjust producer visibility and forwarding priorities for the retimed execute pipeline.\"}]}, 