\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Gating load-use stalls with decoder-generated rs1/rs2-use metadata can remove a mandatory interlock and corrupt architectural registers; operand-use classification must be proven against every instruction's actual reads and IF/ID validity.\"}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Add a decoupled instruction prefetch queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current fetch stage has no storage between the instruction bus and\\ndecode: every cycle with io_imemReady low injects a front-end bubble, even\\nwhen the backend is otherwise able to make progress.  The benchmark also\\ninjects independent data-bus stalls, during which the current pipeline stops\\nfetching and cannot use those cycles to build up useful instruction supply.\\nA small queue targets this structural coupling without changing retirement\\nwidth or the architectural memory behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current fetch stage has no storage between the instruction bus and\\ndecode: every cycle with io_imemReady low injects a front-end bubble, even\\nwhen the backend is otherwise able to make progress.  The benchmark also\\ninjects independent data-bus stalls, during which the current pipeline stops\\nfetching and cannot use those cycles to build up useful instruction supply.\\nA small queue targets this structural coupling without changing retirement\\nwidth or the architectural memory behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the direct combinational IF-to-ID path with a two-entry instruction\\nprefetch queue and an independent fetch PC.  Fetch into an available queue\\nslot whenever the instruction bus is ready, while decode consumes the head\\nwhenever the pipeline is not stalled; simultaneous dequeue/enqueue keeps the\\nqueue streaming at one instruction per cycle.  A redirect must atomically\\ninvalidate both queued entries and restart fetching at the resolved target,\\nand an empty queue must stall decode rather than capture an invalid\\ninstruction.  This should hide a portion of random instruction-bus latency\\nbehind data stalls and other backend bubbles, improving CoreMark\\niterations/cycle with only a small amount of front-end state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the fetch-queue valid/consume handshake so decode stalls only when the queue is empty, while preserving redirect priority and existing RVFI retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single PC/combinational IF payload with a two-entry prefetch queue, independent fetch pointer, simultaneous fill/drain logic, and redirect flush/restart handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget front-end stall and flush decisions to the queue-empty/consume handshake, keeping load-use, dmem-stall, and wrong-path invalidation behavior correct.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the fetch-queue valid/consume handshake so decode stalls only when the queue is empty, while preserving redirect priority and existing RVFI retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single PC/combinational IF payload with a two-entry prefetch queue, independent fetch pointer, simultaneous fill/drain logic, and redirect flush/restart handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget front-end stall and flush decisions to the queue-empty/consume handshake, keeping load-use, dmem-stall, and wrong-path invalidation behavior correct.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 462.59, \"delta_pct\": 14.29, \"vs_baseline\": 63.56, \"fmax_mhz\": 201.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10155, \"ff\": 1990, \"seeds\": [198.57, 209.34, 201.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T15:12:39.953390Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.29%): A two-entry instruction prefetch queue with an independent fetch PC lets instruction-bus backpressure overlap backend/data stalls, but redirect handling must atomically invalidate queued wrong-path entries.\"}\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny trained branch target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The prior static backward-taken predictor reduced fitness because it\\npredicted loop exits and weakly taken backward branches incorrectly, while\\nits IF-side target calculation also created a severe timing regression.\\nCoreMark still has repeated conditional branches where trained direction and\\na cached target can remove the EX redirect bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The prior static backward-taken predictor reduced fitness because it\\npredicted loop exits and weakly taken backward branches incorrectly, while\\nits IF-side target calculation also created a severe timing regression.\\nCoreMark still has repeated conditional branches where trained direction and\\na cached target can remove the EX redirect bubbles.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped branch target buffer with a tag, cached target,\\nand two-bit saturating direction counter per entry.  IF predicts a\\nconditional branch only on a valid BTB hit whose counter is weakly or\\nstrongly taken, so cold branches and recently observed exits remain\\nsequential; the target comes from the BTB rather than a new IF adder.\\nUpdate the entry and counter at EX resolution, carry the prediction through\\nthe existing pipeline metadata, and redirect only on a prediction mismatch.\\nThis should capture stable CoreMark loops while limiting misprediction and\\npreserving the baseline timing more closely than static BTFNT.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped BTB with branch-PC tags, cached targets, two-bit saturating counters, reset state, combinational lookup, and EX-resolution training signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted-taken and predicted-target metadata needed to validate conditional-branch predictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the BTB/BHT lookup and EX update path, while preserving the existing imem/dmem and RVFI contracts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select a trained BTB target for eligible conditional branches, with redirect, stall, flush, and sequential-fetch priority rules intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the IF prediction metadata through the ID/EX register alongside decoded instruction state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved branch next-PC behavior against the carried prediction and generate redirects only for conditional-branch mispredictions, retaining jump and trap behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped BTB with branch-PC tags, cached targets, two-bit saturating counters, reset state, combinational lookup, and EX-resolution training signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend IF/ID and ID/EX bundles with predicted-taken and predicted-target metadata needed to validate conditional-branch predictions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the BTB/BHT lookup and EX update path, while preserving the existing imem/dmem and RVFI contracts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select a trained BTB target for eligible conditional branches, with redirect, stall, flush, and sequential-fetch priority rules intact.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the IF prediction metadata through the ID/EX register alongside decoded instruction state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved branch next-PC behavior against the carried prediction and generate redirects only for conditional-branch mispredictions, retaining jump and trap behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 215.32, \"delta_pct\": -46.8, \"vs_baseline\": -23.87, \"fmax_mhz\": 91.75, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4261180, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12793, \"ff\": 2888, \"seeds\": [91.75, 92.78, 87.38], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T15:10:35.729542Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 (regression, -46.80%): A direct-mapped BTB/BHT can remain functionally correct yet lose heavily when its combinational tag/counter lookup and predicted-target mux sit on the IF next-PC path, so predictor timing must be isolated or kept off the fetch critical path.\"}\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Share the RV32M divide datapath\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes separate signed division, unsigned division,\\nsigned remainder, and unsigned remainder operators.  Synthesis can therefore\\nbuild multiple large divider/remainder cones even though only one M-extension\\noperation is selected per cycle.  This adds LUT pressure and routing delay on\\nthe same timing-sensitive execute path that benefited from multiplier sharing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently describes separate signed division, unsigned division,\\nsigned remainder, and unsigned remainder operators.  Synthesis can therefore\\nbuild multiple large divider/remainder cones even though only one M-extension\\noperation is selected per cycle.  This adds LUT pressure and routing delay on\\nthe same timing-sensitive execute path that benefited from multiplier sharing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the four independent `/` and `%` expressions with one unsigned\\ndivide/remainder datapath selected by the current signedness and remainder\\noperation.  Convert signed operands to magnitudes before division, then apply\\nthe architectural quotient and remainder signs; explicitly retain divide-by-\\nzero and INT_MIN/-1 overflow behavior.  Keep the existing one-cycle ALU\\ninterface and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged.  Sharing the\\ndivider logic should reduce area and routing congestion and improve Fmax\\nwithout changing instruction throughput or pipeline timing.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Share a single unsigned quotient/remainder datapath across DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU, with sign correction and explicit RV32M edge-case handling while preserving ALTOPS behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Share a single unsigned quotient/remainder datapath across DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU, with sign correction and explicit RV32M edge-case handling while preserving ALTOPS behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 333.82, \"delta_pct\": -27.84, \"vs_baseline\": 18.03, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.2, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7965, \"ff\": 1986, \"seeds\": [161.92, 145.2, 143.23], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T15:22:29.395790Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r3s0 (regression, -27.84%): Writing shared signed divide/remainder selection around separate unsigned `/` and `%` operators still computes both wide cones and adds magnitude/sign-correction logic, so RTL-level operand sharing is not sufficient to reduce divider cost without an explicitly shared or iterative datapath.\"}\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Deepen the decoupled instruction prefetch FIFO\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two-entry prefetch queue was the only recent structural change that\\nimproved fitness, because it overlapped instruction-bus and backend/data\\nstalls.  With roughly 22% random stalls on both buses, a two-entry buffer\\nstill drains during longer bursts, exposing bubbles at decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two-entry prefetch queue was the only recent structural change that\\nimproved fitness, because it overlapped instruction-bus and backend/data\\nstalls.  With roughly 22% random stalls on both buses, a two-entry buffer\\nstill drains during longer bursts, exposing bubbles at decode.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the two-entry instruction queue with a four-entry elastic FIFO,\\nusing independent head/tail state and simultaneous enqueue/dequeue.  Keep\\nthe existing fetch PC, queue-empty handshake, and atomic redirect flush so\\nthe extra capacity absorbs longer instruction- and data-stall bursts while\\npreserving one-word-per-cycle streaming.  A circular organization avoids\\nshifting all entries on every consume and limits the structural cost to a\\nsmall head selection and four words of front-end storage.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expand the decoupled instruction buffer to four entries with head/tail pointers, full/empty accounting, simultaneous fill/drain, and redirect reset semantics while preserving the existing module interface.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expand the decoupled instruction buffer to four entries with head/tail pointers, full/empty accounting, simultaneous fill/drain, and redirect reset semantics while preserving the existing module interface.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 325.83, \"delta_pct\": -29.56, \"vs_baseline\": 15.21, \"fmax_mhz\": 141.38, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4339042, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10573, \"ff\": 2129, \"seeds\": [145.6, 129.1, 141.38], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T15:25:06.817671Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (regression, -29.56%): Deepening the prefetch queue to a four-entry circular FIFO regressed despite preserving streaming semantics, showing that the indexed head mux and added pointer/count control can outweigh stall absorption when extra buffering is not the bottleneck.\"}\n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Low-cost call and return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The two prior conditional predictors either guessed CoreMark loop exits\\nincorrectly or placed a BTB/BHT lookup and target mux on the fetch timing\\npath.  The five-stage core also resolves unconditional calls and ABI returns\\nin EX, flushing useful prefetch-queue entries even though direct calls are\\nexactly decodable and returns are highly regular.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two prior conditional predictors either guessed CoreMark loop exits\\nincorrectly or placed a BTB/BHT lookup and target mux on the fetch timing\\npath.  The five-stage core also resolves unconditional calls and ABI returns\\nin EX, flushing useful prefetch-queue entries even though direct calls are\\nexactly decodable and returns are highly regular.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a narrowly scoped call/return predictor rather than a general branch\\npredictor.  A fetch-side predecode should predict every legal direct JAL\\nfrom its encoded target, and a small return-address stack should predict\\nthe common CoreMark ABI return form JALR x0, ra, 0 using return addresses\\npushed by calls.  Carry the predicted next PC with each queued instruction;\\nEX compares it with the resolved next PC and flushes only on a genuine\\nmismatch, preserving the existing recovery path.  Keep conditional B-type\\nbranches and non-return JALR instructions sequential, and implement the\\nstack as compact registered state without a tag/counter lookup, limiting\\nboth misprediction exposure and Fmax/ LUT cost while removing exact call\\nand return redirect bubbles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact direct-JAL predecoder and return-address stack with call push, ABI-return pop, reset, prediction metadata, and resolved-control-flow recovery/update signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the prefetch-queue and ID/EX payloads with predicted next-PC validity and target metadata used to validate call/return speculation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate registered call/return prediction into queue fill and fetch-PC sequencing while keeping conditional branches sequential and preserving redirect priority and queue flush semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the queued prediction metadata into ID/EX without changing decoded architectural control signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved JAL/JALR next-PC behavior with the carried call/return prediction and issue redirects only for mismatches, retaining alignment traps and RVFI pc_wdata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup, training, and recovery across the existing fetch queue and execute stage without changing the RV32IM or RVFI interface.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact direct-JAL predecoder and return-address stack with call push, ABI-return pop, reset, prediction metadata, and resolved-control-flow recovery/update signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the prefetch-queue and ID/EX payloads with predicted next-PC validity and target metadata used to validate call/return speculation.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate registered call/return prediction into queue fill and fetch-PC sequencing while keeping conditional branches sequential and preserving redirect priority and queue flush semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the queued prediction metadata into ID/EX without changing decoded architectural control signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compare resolved JAL/JALR next-PC behavior with the carried call/return prediction and issue redirects only for mismatches, retaining alignment traps and RVFI pc_wdata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect predictor lookup, training, and recovery across the existing fetch queue and execute stage without changing the RV32IM or RVFI interface.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3}\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Constrain the shared multiplier to 32-bit operands\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The successful shared RV32M multiplier is intended to be a 32x32 product,\\nbut alu.sv currently zero-extends both operands to 64 bits before the\\nmultiply.  That expression can cause synthesis to preserve an unnecessarily\\nwide multiplier cone, increasing LUT usage and routing delay on the EX path.\\nThe current best is already slightly over the LUT budget, so removing this\\navoidable width is a high-leverage timing and area micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful shared RV32M multiplier is intended to be a 32x32 product,\\nbut alu.sv currently zero-extends both operands to 64 bits before the\\nmultiply.  That expression can cause synthesis to preserve an unnecessarily\\nwide multiplier cone, increasing LUT usage and routing delay on the EX path.\\nThe current best is already slightly over the LUT budget, so removing this\\navoidable width is a high-leverage timing and area micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the shared unsigned product expression so synthesis sees two\\nexplicitly 32-bit unsigned operands while retaining the full 64-bit result\\nneeded by MULH, MULHU, and the existing sign-correction equations for\\nMULHSU.  Preserve the real RV32M behavior, divide paths, and the\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions exactly.  A true 32x32 implementation\\nshould reduce multiplier resources and routing pressure without adding a\\npipeline cycle or changing forwarding and retirement timing.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Constrain the shared unsigned multiplier operands to 32 bits while preserving the full product, high-half sign corrections, ALTOPS branch, and RV32M edge behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Constrain the shared unsigned multiplier operands to 32 bits while preserving the full product, high-half sign corrections, ALTOPS branch, and RV32M edge behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 343.29, \"delta_pct\": -25.79, \"vs_baseline\": 21.38, \"fmax_mhz\": 149.32, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10571, \"ff\": 1985, \"seeds\": [141.12, 153.78, 149.32], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T15:40:09.218802Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -25.79%): Wrapping a 64-bit multiply result with explicitly 32-bit unsigned temporaries did not guarantee a narrower synthesized multiplier and sharply regressed CoreMark, so operand-width intent must be validated against post-synthesis timing and area rather than inferred from RTL types alone.\"}\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple stores with a one-entry write buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage freezes the whole backend whenever io_dmemReady is\\nlow, even when the blocked operation is a store whose address and data are\\nalready fully known.  The two-entry instruction queue can hide some of\\nthose cycles, but it eventually fills while the store remains in EX/MEM.\\nA small in-order store buffer targets this remaining structural coupling\\nwithout adding a second retirement channel or widening the core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM stage freezes the whole backend whenever io_dmemReady is\\nlow, even when the blocked operation is a store whose address and data are\\nalready fully known.  The two-entry instruction queue can hide some of\\nthose cycles, but it eventually fills while the store remains in EX/MEM.\\nA small in-order store buffer targets this remaining structural coupling\\nwithout adding a second retirement channel or widening the core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry registered store buffer between the EX/MEM producer and the\\ndata bus.  Capture a store's aligned address, replicated write data, byte\\nmask, and retirement metadata once it reaches MEM, then let the pipeline\\ncontinue with younger non-memory instructions while the buffered write waits\\nfor io_dmemReady.  Preserve strict memory ordering by backpressuring a\\nyounger load or store until the older buffered store drains, and keep the\\nexisting load path blocking until its response is accepted.  The buffer's\\nregistered bus drive removes store-induced backend bubbles under the\\nbenchmark's random dStall model, while its single-entry interface avoids the\\nindexed FIFO timing cost seen in the failed four-entry fetch experiment.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry in-order store buffer that holds address, data, byte mask, valid state, and ready-to-drain handshake for the dmem bus.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the minimal store-buffer payload or control fields needed to separate buffered stores from the normal MEM/WB retirement payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the store buffer between EX/MEM and dmem, allowing non-memory instructions to advance while preserving ordered blocking for younger memory operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split store acceptance from load response handling and emit store-buffer enqueue, drain, and busy signals without changing RVFI fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Stall only when a younger memory operation would violate the single-entry store buffer's ordering, while retaining load-use and data-stall interlocks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_buffer.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry in-order store buffer that holds address, data, byte mask, valid state, and ready-to-drain handshake for the dmem bus.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define the minimal store-buffer payload or control fields needed to separate buffered stores from the normal MEM/WB retirement payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the store buffer between EX/MEM and dmem, allowing non-memory instructions to advance while preserving ordered blocking for younger memory operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Split store acceptance from load response handling and emit store-buffer enqueue, drain, and busy signals without changing RVFI fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Stall only when a younger memory operation would violate the single-entry store buffer's ordering, while retaining load-use and data-stall interlocks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [null, null, null], \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Even a one-entry registered normal-RAM store buffer can exhaust Tang Nano 20K placement headroom\\u2014the added bus mux, buffer state, and hazard control left no BELs for the existing `dmem` `RAM16SDP4`, so store decoupling must be checked against post-synthesis resource margin rather than functional correctness alone.\"}\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Exact direct-JAL queue predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves unconditional JAL control flow in EX,\\nflushing prefetched sequential instructions even though a direct JAL target\\nis fully encoded in the instruction.  General BTFNT and BTB predictors have\\neither mispredicted CoreMark branches or damaged Fmax with IF-side lookup\\nlogic, so a narrower exact-target opportunity is more attractive.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current five-stage core resolves unconditional JAL control flow in EX,\\nflushing prefetched sequential instructions even though a direct JAL target\\nis fully encoded in the instruction.  General BTFNT and BTB predictors have\\neither mispredicted CoreMark branches or damaged Fmax with IF-side lookup\\nlogic, so a narrower exact-target opportunity is more attractive.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a decode-time exact predictor for legal, aligned direct JAL instructions.\\nCompute and carry the JAL target while the instruction is admitted to the\\nprefetch queue or consumed by decode, invalidate younger sequential queue\\nentries, and redirect the independent fetch PC to that target before EX\\nresolves the JAL.  Compare the carried prediction with the resolved next PC\\nfor recovery and preserve the existing misalignment/trap behavior.  Do not\\npredict conditional branches or indirect JALR returns.  Keeping this as a\\none-instruction predecode with no BTB tags, counters, or IF lookup mux should\\nremove direct-call bubbles while avoiding the prior predictor timing cliff.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add exact-next-PC prediction metadata to the prefetch and ID/EX payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predecode direct JALs at queue admission or head consumption, flush younger sequential entries, and redirect the independent fetch PC to the encoded target without a BTB lookup.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the direct-JAL prediction metadata into ID/EX with the decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the exact JAL prediction against the resolved next PC and retain alignment-trap and redirect semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the queue/decode prediction and recovery signals while preserving the existing memory and RVFI contracts.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add exact-next-PC prediction metadata to the prefetch and ID/EX payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predecode direct JALs at queue admission or head consumption, flush younger sequential entries, and redirect the independent fetch PC to the encoded target without a BTB lookup.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the direct-JAL prediction metadata into ID/EX with the decoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the exact JAL prediction against the resolved next PC and retain alignment-trap and redirect semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the queue/decode prediction and recovery signals while preserving the existing memory and RVFI contracts.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): An early direct-JAL redirect must update the prefetch queue and independent `fetch_pc_q` from the same predicted target; splitting `predict_fire` from that atomic state transition can desynchronize fetch and corrupt CoreMark.\"}\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Factor the EX control-flow target adder\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The EX stage independently forms branch targets, direct-JAL targets, and\\nJALR targets.  Branches and direct JALs both use the same PC-plus-immediate\\narithmetic, so keeping separate expressions can duplicate adder logic and\\nrouting on the redirect-critical path.  The current design is close to the\\nTang Nano 20K LUT and timing limits, making a local datapath simplification\\nmore attractive than adding state or speculative machinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX stage independently forms branch targets, direct-JAL targets, and\\nJALR targets.  Branches and direct JALs both use the same PC-plus-immediate\\narithmetic, so keeping separate expressions can duplicate adder logic and\\nrouting on the redirect-critical path.  The current design is close to the\\nTang Nano 20K LUT and timing limits, making a local datapath simplification\\nmore attractive than adding state or speculative machinery.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Factor the PC-plus-immediate result into one explicitly shared target\\ndatapath and reuse it for conditional branches and direct JALs; retain a\\nseparate rs1-plus-immediate path only for JALR, with bit-zero alignment\\nmasking applied after target formation.  Preserve the existing branch\\ncomparisons, misalignment traps, redirect priority, RVFI pc_wdata, and\\npipeline latency.  Removing redundant target arithmetic should lower EX\\nLUT/routing pressure and improve worst-seed Fmax without changing the\\nretired instruction stream or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Explicitly share the PC-plus-immediate control-flow target between branches and direct JALs, retain correct JALR target formation and alignment-trap behavior, and keep the existing EX/MEM and redirect interfaces unchanged.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Explicitly share the PC-plus-immediate control-flow target between branches and direct JALs, retain correct JALR target formation and alignment-trap behavior, and keep the existing EX/MEM and redirect interfaces unchanged.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 380.38, \"delta_pct\": -17.77, \"vs_baseline\": 34.5, \"fmax_mhz\": 165.45, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10089, \"ff\": 1989, \"seeds\": [172.0, 161.79, 165.45], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T15:58:50.412214Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r5s0 (regression, -17.77%): Explicitly naming a shared `pc + imm` target in `always_comb` did not guarantee EX adder/routing reduction and regressed CoreMark, so datapath factoring must be validated after synthesis rather than assumed from RTL common-subexpression structure.\"}\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Move division into an iterative EX sidecar\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current combinational ALU contains both signed and unsigned divide\\nand remainder operators on the EX critical path.  Those wide arithmetic\\ncones consume LUTs and routing even though CoreMark executes relatively\\nfew DIV/REM instructions, while every ordinary instruction pays their\\ntiming cost through the shared EX stage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current combinational ALU contains both signed and unsigned divide\\nand remainder operators on the EX critical path.  Those wide arithmetic\\ncones consume LUTs and routing even though CoreMark executes relatively\\nfew DIV/REM instructions, while every ordinary instruction pays their\\ntiming cost through the shared EX stage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 32-iteration restoring divider sidecar and make DIV, DIVU, REM, and\\nREMU multi-cycle EX operations.  On a divide issue, hold the in-order\\npipeline behind EX until the sidecar completes, then inject exactly one\\nresult into the existing EX/MEM path with normal forwarding and RVFI\\nretirement.  Keep ADD/branch/load/store/MUL operations on the existing\\nsingle-cycle path, preserve divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 behavior, and\\nretain the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS combinational substitutions so formal\\nchecking remains tractable.  Removing the synthesized / and % cones from\\nthe common path should raise Fmax with limited CoreMark cycle cost.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM operators from the common ALU path while retaining ALTOPS and all non-divider RV32M operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact iterative signed/unsigned quotient-remainder unit with start, busy, done, and RV32M edge-case handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Issue divider operations to the sidecar, hold the in-order execute boundary while busy, and return one completed result through the existing EX/MEM metadata path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate divider busy/complete control so decode and fetch stall only for the active multi-cycle divide while retirement remains single-issue and ordered.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a divider interlock with priority over normal decode progress and preserve load-use, data-bus, and redirect semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real DIV/REM operators from the common ALU path while retaining ALTOPS and all non-divider RV32M operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact iterative signed/unsigned quotient-remainder unit with start, busy, done, and RV32M edge-case handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Issue divider operations to the sidecar, hold the in-order execute boundary while busy, and return one completed result through the existing EX/MEM metadata path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate divider busy/complete control so decode and fetch stall only for the active multi-cycle divide while retirement remains single-issue and ordered.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a divider interlock with priority over normal decode progress and preserve load-use, data