{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r7s0.yaml && git status --short -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r7s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-001-r7s0\ntitle: \"Use explicit memory lane selection\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The MEM stage currently uses variable shifts for load extraction and a\n  variable shift for byte-mask generation.  Those operations can infer\n  general barrel-shifter logic on the dmem-to-MEM/WB path, adding LUTs and\n  routing delay even though RV32I only permits four byte positions and two\n  aligned halfword positions.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the variable load shift and mask shifts with explicit cases on\n  address low bits and access width.  Select the required byte or halfword\n  directly from the returned word, and emit the equivalent constant lane\n  masks while retaining the existing sign extension, misalignment gating,\n  RVFI aligned address/data fields, and zero-wait/stalled bus behavior.\n  This should shorten the memory-result path and reduce LUT/routing pressure\n  without adding state, changing retirement timing, or altering CoreMark\n  instruction cycles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use explicit address/width lane cases for load extraction and byte masks, preserving RV32I alignment traps, sign extension, dmem outputs, and RVFI memory fields.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
