{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r9s0.yaml && git diff -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r9s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\ntitle: \"Carry PC plus four into execute\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The EX stage recomputes the sequential next PC and the JAL return address\n  with 32-bit additions for every pipeline instruction.  Those additions\n  sit alongside the ALU, forwarding, and control-flow logic in the stage that\n  is most sensitive to timing and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute PC+4 once while the instruction is entering ID and carry that\n  value in the ID/EX bundle.  Use the registered value for sequential\n  pc_next, JAL's rd writeback value, and the sequential side of the existing\n  misalignment-trap bookkeeping, while leaving branch and JALR target\n  formation, forwarding, flushes, and retirement unchanged.  Moving this\n  adder out of EX should shorten the critical path with only a small amount\n  of pipeline storage and no IPC or ISA change.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a PC+4 field to the ID/EX pipeline bundle so execute receives the precomputed sequential next-PC value.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute and register in.pc + 32'd4 alongside the decoded instruction, resetting it with the existing ID/EX bubble state.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Replace EX-stage sequential PC and JAL return-address additions with the carried PC+4 value while preserving branch/JALR targets, traps, and RVFI pc_next behavior.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
