 valid dmem response, while retaining backend stall and redirect priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/forward_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a distinct EX/MEM load-response forwarding selection and prevent the ordinary ALU-result bypass from forwarding a load address as data.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a highest-priority, response-valid load-data operand path for dependent rs1/rs2 values, falling back safely when the load is misaligned or the bus is stalled.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Expose the existing width/sign/alignment-correct load extraction as a same-cycle bypass value, qualified by a valid aligned load response.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect dmemReady, EX/MEM load metadata, the MEM response bypass, and the retimed hazard/forwarding controls without changing the top-level IO or RVFI contract.\"}],\"lesson\":null}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"The proposal is a fall-through fetch-response bypass: when the proven two-entry queue is empty and the instruction bus is ready, feed that response directly into decode for the same edge instead of first enqueueing it and paying a refill bubble. It keeps the queue as the normal path, adds no predictor/table or backend storage, and explicitly preserves redirect, stall, and queue-ownership rules."}}
