{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n -i \"structural|r14|queue|pipeline|forward|hazard|retire|stage|memory\" cores/bench/LESSONS.md cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 160'","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md:1:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product and deriving MULH/MULHSU with sign-correction subtractions preserves RV32M results while eliminating duplicated multiplier hardware without adding pipeline latency.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:2:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 (broken, n/a): Splitting EX into EX1/EX2 changes producer availability, so forwarding, valid/flush, and load-use hazard logic must be derived from the exact new stage timing or dependent instructions can corrupt architectural register writes.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:5:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.29%): A two-entry instruction prefetch queue with an independent fetch PC lets instruction-bus backpressure overlap backend/data stalls, but redirect handling must atomically invalidate queued wrong-path entries.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:8:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 (regression, -29.56%): Deepening the prefetch queue to a four-entry circular FIFO regressed despite preserving streaming semantics, showing that the indexed head mux and added pointer/count control can outweigh stall absorption when extra buffering is not the bottleneck.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:10:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Even a one-entry registered normal-RAM store buffer can exhaust Tang Nano 20K placement headroom—the added bus mux, buffer state, and hazard control left no BELs for the existing `dmem` `RAM16SDP4`, so store decoupling must be checked against post-synthesis resource margin rather than functional correctness alone.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:11:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): An early direct-JAL redirect must update the prefetch queue and independent `fetch_pc_q` from the same predicted target; splitting `predict_fire` from that atomic state transition can desynchronize fetch and corrupt CoreMark.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:13:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 (regression, -5.85%): A 32-iteration DIV/REM EX sidecar can remove the combinational divider cone yet still lose on CoreMark because holding the in-order pipeline behind `divider_busy` serializes each relatively rare divide for its full latency; multi-cycle arithmetic must earn back its IPC cost in measured Fmax.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:14:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): Instantiating a new `branch_predictor` in `core.sv` without adding its RTL implementation to the build makes cosimulation fail before predictor behavior is tested, so pipeline experiments must verify module and source-list integration first.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:15:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r6s0 (regression, -36.41%): Replacing 2-bit forwarding selects with 32-bit priority-selected operands regressed CoreMark because moving the value muxing into the hazard network increased EX-path fanout/routing, so forwarding refactors must be judged on post-synthesis timing rather than mux-count alone.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:16:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): A direct MEM-to-EX load bypass must release the load-use interlock only when the same-cycle `dmem_ready` response is valid and aligned; gating the hazard solely on producer alignment can let dependents advance without a usable load value and break cosimulation.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:17:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 (broken, n/a): A predicted queue-head redirect must use one effective target consistently for `imem_addr`, `fetch_pc_q`, and the queued entry PC; mixing the predicted target with EX's resolved `redirect_target` corrupts branch RVFI PCs and fails `pc_fwd`.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:18:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r7s0 (regression, -7.59%): Replacing restricted-shift load extraction with nested explicit lane cases regressed CoreMark, showing that extra MEM-stage muxing and routing can outweigh the expected barrel-shifter savings and must be validated after synthesis.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:21:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Predecoded control and immediates become part of a fetch-queue entry's identity, so every push/pop, simultaneous refill, redirect refill, and flush must carry them atomically with the raw instruction and PC or formal instruction/RVFI checks fail across the ISA.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:22:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (regression, -19.00%): Moving the PC+4 adder from EX into ID and carrying a 32-bit result through ID/EX regressed CoreMark, showing that stage relocation can shift timing/routing pressure into decode and bundle transport instead of shortening the critical path.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:23:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (regression, -32.94%): Replacing compact two-entry queue counting with explicit valid-bit pop/push promotion logic regressed CoreMark, showing that extra slot-valid decode and simultaneous-transition muxing can outweigh removing small arithmetic on FPGA fetch control paths.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:26:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 (regression, -57.55%): Confidence gating did not make a two-entry loop-target predictor cheap enough because queue-head `if_head_*` lookup, `predict_fire` target selection, and carried prediction metadata still widen the fetch/redirect control path; predictor selectivity cannot offset front-end timing overhead unless the prediction transaction is isolated from ordinary `imem_addr` selection.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:27:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r11s0 (broken, n/a): A radix-4 early-out divider cannot be bolted onto EX by merely substituting `ALU_ADD` plus `divider_start/busy/done`; the ID/EX hold, EX/MEM commit, and hazard/RVFI-valid handshake must make the completed quotient or remainder one atomic result or cosimulation fails.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:28:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r11s1 (broken, n/a): A registered memory request/response boundary must distinguish request acceptance from returned-data validity and carry that state through load-use forwarding and RVFI retirement; conflating them can pass stores while every load form and dependent register check fail.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:29:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r11s2 (broken, n/a): Retirement-only RAS updates must remain aligned with the valid IF queue head and its carried prediction marker across stalls and flushes, or wrong-path calls/returns can consume a different stack top and break CoreMark cosimulation.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:31:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r12s2 (regression, -2.30%): A table-free direct-JAL predictor still regressed when every accepted response added opcode/target arithmetic plus prediction metadata through the two-entry queue and ID/EX, showing that response-side speculation must earn back its queue-control and bundle-routing cost even when it leaves the steady-state imem address path untouched.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:33:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r13s1 (regression, -35.34%): A fall-through fetch response from `imem_data`/`imem_ready` into IF/ID, plus effective-availability hazard logic, can widen the combinational fetch/decode control path enough to outweigh eliminating empty-queue refill bubbles, so bypasses must be validated against post-synthesis timing and benchmark IPC.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:34:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-003-r13s2 (broken, n/a): A registered RAS response cannot be treated as a decode-only `prediction_wait`; queue pop, prediction-token capture, fetch-target update, and EX recovery must share one valid transaction or a stalled return loses instruction identity and fails RVFI `pc_fwd` and register checks.\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share the multiplier across RV32M high-half operations\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU currently computes three independent 64-bit products on every\\ncombinational evaluation: signed-by-signed, unsigned-by-unsigned, and\\nsigned-by-unsigned. This duplicates multiplier hardware and contributes to\\nboth the LUT budget and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU currently computes three independent 64-bit products on every\\ncombinational evaluation: signed-by-signed, unsigned-by-unsigned, and\\nsigned-by-unsigned. This duplicates multiplier hardware and contributes to\\nboth the LUT budget and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 32x32 64-bit product.\\nDerive MULH and MULHSU high halves by subtracting the appropriate sign\\ncorrection terms from that product, while retaining the same low-half MUL\\nresult and the existing ALTOPS behavior. Sharing the multiplier should\\nreduce area and routing congestion, improving Fmax without adding cycles or\\nchanging the pipeline's RV32M latency.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one unsigned 64-bit product and sign-correction logic for MULH and MULHSU instead of three parallel multipliers, preserving ALTOPS and exact RV32M results.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Use one unsigned 64-bit product and sign-correction logic for MULH and MULHSU instead of three parallel multipliers, preserving ALTOPS and exact RV32M results.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 404.74, \"delta_pct\": 43.11, \"vs_baseline\": 43.11, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10073, \"ff\": 1860, \"seeds\": [181.79, 175.38, 185.53], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T14:43:31.330052Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r1s0 (improvement, +43.11%): Sharing one unsigned 32x32 product and deriving MULH/MULHSU with sign-correction subtractions preserves RV32M results while eliminating duplicated multiplier hardware without adding pipeline latency.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Split execute datapath into two pipeline stages\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline P&R critical path runs from the ID/EX register through the\\nforwarded-operand muxes and 32-bit EX arithmetic into EX/MEM, consuming\\nroughly 4.1-4.4 ns and limiting the worst seed to 127.03 MHz.  CoreMark is\\nalready close to the LUT budget, so improving timing through retiming is a\\nmore promising structural lever than widening the machine.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline P&R critical path runs from the ID/EX register through the\\nforwarded-operand muxes and 32-bit EX arithmetic into EX/MEM, consuming\\nroughly 4.1-4.4 ns and limiting the worst seed to 127.03 MHz.  CoreMark is\\nalready close to the LUT budget, so improving timing through retiming is a\\nmore promising structural lever than widening the machine.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retiming the execute path into EX1 and EX2 with an EX1-to-EX2 register will\\nseparate forwarding/operand selection from the ALU and branch-result cone.\\nKeep EX2 fully pipelined so it still accepts one instruction per cycle, and\\nupdate valid, redirect, load-use, and forwarding bookkeeping for the extra\\nstage.  The shorter combinational paths should raise Fmax enough to offset\\nthe additional taken-branch recovery cycle on CoreMark.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Retiming and control integration 