{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n x=json.loads(line)\n if x.get('category')=='structural' or ('branch' in x.get('title','').lower()):\n  print('\\\\n',x['id'], x['title'], x.get('outcome'), x.get('delta_pct'))\n  print(x.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis','')[:900])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n hyp-20260710-002-r1s1 Split execute datapath into two pipeline stages broken None\nRetiming the execute path into EX1 and EX2 with an EX1-to-EX2 register will\nseparate forwarding/operand selection from the ALU and branch-result cone.\nKeep EX2 fully pipelined so it still accepts one instruction per cycle, and\nupdate valid, redirect, load-use, and forwarding bookkeeping for the extra\nstage.  The shorter combinational paths should raise Fmax enough to offset\nthe additional taken-branch recovery cycle on CoreMark.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 Static backward-branch taken predictor regression -12.37\nAdd a decode-light static BTFNT predictor in IF for legal, word-aligned\nB-type conditional branches whose immediate is negative. Compute pc plus\nthe branch immediate in IF and fetch from that target immediately, while\ncarrying predicted_taken and predicted_target through IF/ID and ID/EX.\nEX should redirect only when the resolved architectural next PC differs from\nthe carried prediction; mispredictions still flush normally and RVFI\npc_wdata continues to report the resolved next PC. Keep JAL and JALR on\ntheir existing EX-resolution paths so this remains a single predictor change.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 Add a decoupled instruction prefetch queue improvement 14.29\nReplace the direct combinational IF-to-ID path with a two-entry instruction\nprefetch queue and an independent fetch PC.  Fetch into an available queue\nslot whenever the instruction bus is ready, while decode consumes the head\nwhenever the pipeline is not stalled; simultaneous dequeue/enqueue keeps the\nqueue streaming at one instruction per cycle.  A redirect must atomically\ninvalidate both queued entries and restart fetching at the resolved target,\nand an empty queue must stall decode rather than capture an invalid\ninstruction.  This should hide a portion of random instruction-bus latency\nbehind data stalls and other backend bubbles, improving CoreMark\niterations/cycle with only a small amount of front-end state.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 Tiny trained branch target predictor regression -46.8\nAdd a small direct-mapped branch target buffer with a tag, cached target,\nand two-bit saturating direction counter per entry.  IF predicts a\nconditional branch only on a valid BTB hit whose counter is weakly or\nstrongly taken, so cold branches and recently observed exits remain\nsequential; the target comes from the BTB rather than a new IF adder.\nUpdate the entry and counter at EX resolution, carry the prediction through\nthe existing pipeline metadata, and redirect only on a prediction mismatch.\nThis should capture stable CoreMark loops while limiting misprediction and\npreserving the baseline timing more closely than static BTFNT.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r3s1 Deepen the decoupled instruction prefetch FIFO regression -29.56\nReplace the two-entry instruction queue with a four-entry elastic FIFO,\nusing independent head/tail state and simultaneous enqueue/dequeue.  Keep\nthe existing fetch PC, queue-empty handshake, and atomic redirect flush so\nthe extra capacity absorbs longer instruction- and data-stall bursts while\npreserving one-word-per-cycle streaming.  A circular organization avoids\nshifting all entries on every consume and limits the structural cost to a\nsmall head selection and four words of front-end storage.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r4s1 Decouple stores with a one-entry write buffer placement_failed None\nAdd a one-entry registered store buffer between the EX/MEM producer and the\ndata bus.  Capture a store's aligned address, replicated write data, byte\nmask, and retirement metadata once it reaches MEM, then let the pipeline\ncontinue with younger non-memory instructions while the buffered write waits\nfor io_dmemReady.  Preserve strict memory ordering by backpressuring a\nyounger load or store until the older buffered store drains, and keep the\nexisting load path blocking until its response is accepted.  The buffer's\nregistered bus drive removes store-induced backend bubbles under the\nbenchmark's random dStall model, while its single-entry interface avoids the\nindexed FIFO timing cost seen in the failed four-entry fetch experiment.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r5s1 Move division into an iterative EX sidecar regression -5.85\nAdd a 32-iteration restoring divider sidecar and make DIV, DIVU, REM, and\nREMU multi-cycle EX operations.  On a divide issue, hold the in-order\npipeline behind EX until the sidecar completes, then inject exactly one\nresult into the existing EX/MEM path with normal forwarding and RVFI\nretirement.  Keep ADD/branch/load/store/MUL operations on the existing\nsingle-cycle path, preserve divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 behavior, and\nretain the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS combinational substitutions so formal\nchecking remains tractable.  Removing the synthesized / and % cones from\nthe common path should raise Fmax with limited CoreMark cycle cost.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r6s1 Bypass completed loads directly into execute broken None\nRepartition the MEM-to-EX boundary with a direct load-result bypass.  When a\nload in EX/MEM has a valid, aligned response and io_dmemReady is high, feed\nthe extracted byte/halfword/word value directly into the EX operand mux of\nthe younger dependent instruction and allow ID/EX to advance without the\nload-use bubble.  Keep the existing MEM/WB capture and forwarding path as\nthe architectural retirement path, and retain the interlock whenever the\nresponse is stalled, the access is misaligned, or the producer is not a\ncompleted load.  This should improve CoreMark iterations per cycle with\nonly a localized bypass mux and no additional pipeline storage.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 Queue-head adaptive conditional branch predictor broken None\nAdd a compact 2-bit saturating branch-history table indexed by low PC bits,\nconsulted only when the valid queue head is consumed.  For a conditional\nbranch, predict taken only in the strong/weak-taken states and compute its\nimmediate target from the queue-head instruction; if taken, atomically flush\nyounger queue entries and restart the registered fetch PC at that target.\nCarry the prediction through ID/EX, train the table from the EX-resolved\noutcome, and redirect on any predicted-versus-resolved next-PC mismatch.\nKeep JAL/JALR and non-branches on their existing path, preserve alignment\ntraps and RVFI next-PC behavior, and ensure reset, imem stalls, and queue\nreplacement use one coordinated state transition.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r7s1 Resolve conditional branches at decode broken None\nRepartition conditional-branch handling so a valid queue-head branch is\ncompared in ID using register-file operands plus narrowly scoped\nEX/MEM-to-ID and MEM/WB-to-ID forwarding.  On a taken branch, atomically\nconsume the branch, flush only younger queue entries, and restart the\nindependent fetch PC at the aligned target; on a not-taken branch, keep\nsequential fetch flowing without an EX-time recovery redirect.  Stall the\nbranch when an older load has not produced a usable value, preserve the\nexisting EX/MEM retirement payload, and carry an early-resolved marker so\nEX does not redirect or resolve the same branch twice.  JAL/JALR, illegal\ninstructions, misalignment traps, RVFI next-PC fields, and single-issue\nordering remain unchanged.  Removing the normal taken-branch recovery\nbubble should raise CoreMark IPC without a predictor table or a deeper\nfetch structure.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-001-r8s0 Share the EX branch comparator regression -16.09\nReplace the separate branch comparisons with one shared 33-bit subtract\nand derive equality, unsigned less-than, and signed less-than from its\nzero, borrow, and sign information.  Select only the resulting predicate\nfor the decoded branch operation, while preserving all six RV32I branch\nsemantics, forwarding inputs, branch-target computation, misalignment\ntraps, redirect timing, and RVFI pc_next behavior.  Sharing the compare\ndatapath should reduce EX logic and routing without adding a pipeline\nstage or changing retirement behavior.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r8s1 Predecode instructions in the fetch queue broken None\nMove decode and immediate generation to the instruction-accept side of the\nexisting two-entry queue.  Each queued entry will retain the raw instruction\nand PC for RVFI and hazard checks, plus its predecoded control bundle and\nimmediate.  ID will consume those registered fields and only perform the\nregister-file read and payload capture.  Decode results must be captured\nonly when imemReady accepts a word, including the redirect refill, and the\nqueue flush/empty paths must invalidate or zero all predecoded fields.  This\nkeeps instruction timing, branch resolution, load-use interlocks, memory\nbehavior, and RVFI metadata unchanged while shortening the queue-head to\nID/EX critical path; the extra queue-entry flops are expected to be cheaper\nthan the resulting timing margin.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 Retimed two-slot elastic fetch buffer regression -32.94\nReplace the count-based two-entry queue control with a fixed two-slot\nelastic buffer using explicit valid bits and direct slot-0/slot-1\ntransitions.  Keep the independent sequential fetch PC and the existing\none-word-per-cycle refill behavior, but eliminate count increment/decrement\nand general FIFO indexing from the normal path.  A normal consume promotes\nslot 1 only when valid, a consume-plus-refill replaces or appends directly,\nand redirect atomically clears both slots while accepting the target word\ninto slot 0 when io_imemReady is high.  Preserve the exact queue-head PC and\ninstruction pairing, imem-stall behavior, redirect priority, and all valid\nsemantics so the backend and RVFI timing remain unchanged.  The reduced\ncontrol fanout and simpler next-state muxing should improve Fmax without\npaying the area and timing cost of a deeper queue.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r10s1 Response-gated load-use execute bypass broken None\nReplace the unconditional one-cycle load-use bubble with a response-gated\ninterlock.  Let the dependent instruction enter ID/EX, then hold that\ninstruction and the outstanding EX/MEM load only while io_dmemReady is low.\nWhen the load is valid, aligned, addressed to a nonzero destination, and\nio_dmemReady is high, forward the already extracted load value directly\nfrom MEM to the dependent EX operands; otherwise retain the stall or use\nthe normal register/MEM-WB path.  Keep EX/MEM ALU forwarding disabled for\nloads, qualify the bypass with the exact memory response and load width,\nand preserve misalignment traps, redirect behavior, RVFI ordering, and\nstore/load bus side effects.  Removing the avoidable load-use bubble should\nraise CoreMark IPC with only a small response mux and control change.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r11s1 Register the memory request/response boundary broken None\nSplit memory access into a registered request stage and a response stage.\nCapture the aligned address, write data, byte enables, load width/sign\nmetadata, and the complete RVFI payload in a one-entry memory-request\nregister; drive the dmem ports only from that stable register and capture\nthe returned word in the following response register.  Keep a bypass path\nfor non-memory instructions and use valid/ready interlocks so memory\nrequests remain ordered, held stable across io_dmemReady stalls, and can\naccept one request per cycle after filling.  Preserve precise traps,\nload/store masks, RVFI retirement order, and the existing two-entry fetch\nqueue while shortening the EX-to-RAM and RAM-to-writeback timing cones.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r12s1 Decouple the backend around a single memory slot placement_failed None\nRestructure the backend as an in-order decoupled execute path plus a\none-entry memory transaction slot.  Capture a load/store request and its\ncomplete RVFI metadata in the slot, explicitly distinguish request\nacceptance from response-valid, and let independent non-memory results\nadvance into a small ordered completion holding register while the slot\nwaits.  Commit memory and younger results in original program order, block\nonly when the holding capacity or a memory dependency is exhausted, and\nkeep MMIO and misaligned accesses on the conservative blocking path.  This\nshould overlap dmem stalls with useful ALU/branch work without changing the\ntop-level buses, retirement width, or instruction-fetch queue.\n\n\n hyp-20260710-002-r13s1 Fall-through fetch response into decode regression -35.34\nAdd a fall-through response path in the existing two-entry fetch queue.  When\nthe queue is empty, the bus is ready, and decode is able to accept an\ninstruction, present the current fetch response directly as the IF/ID view\nand advance fetch_pc_q without storing and re-reading that word.  If decode\nstalls, retain the normal enqueue behavior; if EX redirects, suppress the\nbypass and atomically refill from the redirect target.  Keep the bypass\ndisabled during redirect and propagate the effective availability signal to\nhazard logic so load-use bubbles cannot consume or drop an unheld response.\nThis removes empty-queue refill bubbles without changing the top-level bus,\nsingle-retirement RVFI contract, or the proven compact queue steady state.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
