utcome\":\"improvement\",\"delta_pct\":14.29,\"fitness\":462.59,\"hypothesis\":\"Replace the direct combinational IF-to-ID path with a two-entry instruction\\nprefetch queue and an independent fetch PC.  Fetch into an available queue\\nslot whenever the instruction bus is ready, while decode consumes the head\\nwhenever the pipeline is not stalled; simultaneous dequeue/enqueue keeps the\\nqueue streaming at one instruction per cycle.  A redirect must atomically\\ninvalidate both queued entries and restart fetching at the resolved target,\\nand an empty queue must stall decode rather than capture an invalid\\ninstruction.  This should hide a portion of random instruction-bus latency\\nbehind data stalls and other backend bubbles, improving CoreMark\\niterations/cycle with only a small amount of front-end state.\\n\",\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Integrate the fetch-queue valid/consume handshake so decode stalls only when the queue is empty, while preserving redirect priority and existing RVFI retirement ordering.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Replace the single PC/combinational IF payload with a two-entry prefetch queue, independent fetch pointer, simultaneous fill/drain logic, and redirect flush/restart handling.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Retarget front-end stall and flush decisions to the queue-empty/consume handshake, keeping load-use, dmem-stall, and wrong-path invalidation behavior correct.\"}],\"implementation_notes\":\"\",\"error\":null}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-002-r3s1\",\"title\":\"Deepen the decoupled instruction prefetch FIFO\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"delta_pct\":-29.56,\"fitness\":325.83,\"hypothesis\":\"Replace the two-entry instruction queue with a four-entry elastic FIFO,\\nusing independent head/tail state and simultaneous enqueue/dequeue.  Keep\\nthe existing fetch PC, queue-empty handshake, and atomic redirect flush so\\nthe extra capacity absorbs longer instruction- and data-stall bursts while\\npreserving one-word-per-cycle streaming.  A circular organization avoids\\nshifting all entries on every consume and limits the structural cost to a\\nsmall head selection and four words of front-end storage.\\n\",\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Expand the decoupled instruction buffer to four entries with head/tail pointers, full/empty accounting, simultaneous fill/drain, and redirect reset semantics while preserving the existing module interface.\"}],\"implementation_notes\":\"\",\"error\":null}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-002-r4s1\",\"title\":\"Decouple stores with a one-entry write buffer\",\"outcome\":\"placement_failed\",\"delta_pct\":null,\"fitness\":null,\"hypothesis\":\"Add a one-entry registered store buffer between the EX/MEM producer and the\\ndata bus.  Capture a store's aligned address, replicated write data, byte\\nmask, and retirement metadata once it reaches MEM, then let the pipeline\\ncontinue with younger non-memory instructions while the buffered write waits\\nfor io_dmemReady.  Preserve strict memory ordering by backpressuring a\\nyounger load or store until the older buffered store drains, and keep the\\nexisting load path blocking until its response is accepted.  The buffer's\\nregistered bus drive removes store-induced backend bubbles under the\\nbenchmark's random dStall model, while its single-entry interface avoids the\\nindexed FIFO timing cost seen in the failed four-entry fetch experiment.\\n\",\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/store_buffer.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a one-entry in-order store buffer that holds address, data, byte mask, valid state, and ready-to-drain handshake for the dmem bus.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Define the minimal store-buffer payload or control fields needed to separate buffered stores from the normal MEM/WB retirement payload.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Integrate the store buffer between EX/MEM and dmem, allowing non-memory instructions to advance while preserving ordered blocking for younger memory operations.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Split store acceptance from load response handling and emit store-buffer enqueue, drain, and busy signals without changing RVFI fields.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Stall only when a younger memory operation would violate the single-entry store buffer's ordering, while retaining load-use and data-stall interlocks.\"}],\"implementation_notes\":null,\"error\":\"placement_failed\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-002-r5s1\",\"title\":\"Move division into an iterative EX sidecar\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"delta_pct\":-5.85,\"fitness\":435.51,\"hypothesis\":\"Add a 32-iteration restoring divider sidecar and make DIV, DIVU, REM, and\\nREMU multi-cycle EX operations.  On a divide issue, hold the in-order\\npipeline behind EX until the sidecar completes, then inject exactly one\\nresult into the existing EX/MEM path with normal forwarding and RVFI\\nretirement.  Keep ADD/branch/load/store/MUL operations on the existing\\nsingle-cycle path, preserve divide-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 behavior, and\\nretain the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS combinational substitutions so formal\\nchecking remains tractable.  Removing the synthesized / and % cones from\\nthe common path should raise Fmax with limited CoreMark cycle cost.\\n\",\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/alu.sv\",\"description\":\"Remove real DIV/REM operators from the common ALU path while retaining ALTOPS and all non-divider RV32M operations.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/divider.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a compact iterative signed/unsigned quotient-remainder unit with start, busy, done, and RV32M edge-case handling.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Issue divider operations to the sidecar, hold the in-order execute boundary while busy, and return one completed result through the existing EX/MEM metadata path.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Integrate divider busy/complete control so decode and fetch stall only for the active multi-cycle divide while retirement remains single-issue and ordered.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a divider interlock with priority over normal decode progress and preserve load-use, data-bus, and redirect semantics.\"}],\"implementation_notes\":\"\",\"error\":null}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-002-r6s1\",\"title\":\"Bypass completed loads directly into execute\",\"outcome\":\"broken\",\"delta_pct\":null,\"fitness\":null,\"hypothesis\":\"Repartition the MEM-to-EX boundary with a direct load-result bypass.  When a\\nload in EX/MEM has a valid, aligned response and io_dmemReady is high, feed\\nthe extracted byte/halfword/word value directly into the EX operand mux of\\nthe younger dependent instruction and allow ID/EX to advance without the\\nload-use bubble.  Keep the existing MEM/WB capture and forwarding path as\\nthe architectural retirement path, and retain the interlock whenever the\\nresponse is stalled, the access is misaligned, or the producer is not a\\ncompleted load.  This should improve CoreMark iterations per cycle with\\nonly a localized bypass mux and no additional pipeline storage.\\n\",\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Expose a valid direct-load-forward value using the existing load extraction and alignment checks, without changing MEM/WB or RVFI fields.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Add MEM-to-EX load-data selection with correct priority over ALU forwarding and preserve all normal operand, branch, and trap behavior.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Suppress the load-use bubble only for a completed aligned load response; continue stalling for dmem backpressure and unsafe cases.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire the load-forward value and valid handshake from MEM to EX and hazard control while preserving the top-level IO and single retirement channel.\"}],\"implementation_notes\":null,\"error\":\"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260710-002-r7s1\",\"title\":\"Resolve conditional branches at decode\",\"outcome\":\"broken\",\"delta_pct\":null,\"fitness\":null,\"hypothesis\":\"Repartition conditional-branch handling so a valid queue-head branch is\\ncompared in ID using register-file operands plus narrowly scoped\\nEX/MEM-to-ID and MEM/WB-to-ID forwarding.  On a taken branch, atomically\\nconsume the branch, flush only younger queue entries, and restart the\\nindependent fetch PC at the aligned target; on a not-taken branch, keep\\nsequential fetch flowing without an EX-time recovery redirect.  Stall the\\nbranch when an older load has not produced a usable value, preserve the\\nexisting EX/MEM retirement payload, and carry an early-resolved marker so\\nEX does not redirect or resolve the same branch twice.  JAL/JALR, illegal\\ninstructions, misalignment traps, RVFI next-PC fields, and single-issue\\nordering remain unchanged.  Removing the normal taken-branch recovery\\nbubble should raise CoreMark IPC without a predictor table or a deeper\\nfetch structure.\\n\",\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Integrate decode-stage branch resolution, early-target queue control, ID forwarding, and the existing EX redirect/trap signals while preserving RVFI retirement ordering.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add an early-branch-resolved marker and target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline payloads.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Add an atomic queue-head branch consume/flush operation that uses the decode-resolved target and keeps fetch_pc_q, imem_addr, and queued PCs consistent.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Expose branch source operands and decoded branch metadata for early compare/target generation while retaining normal branch retirement fields.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Add conservative ID branch interlocks for unresolved older producers, especially loads and data-bus stalls, without weakening existing load-use or redirect stalls.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Suppress duplicate EX-time conditional-branch redirects for early-resolved branches and preserve branch RVFI pc_wdata and misalignment-trap behavior.\"}],\"implementation_notes\":null,\"error\":\"formal_failed: pc_fwd_ch0\\nde=0)\\nSBY 13:44:52 [insn_slli_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 13:44:52 [insn_slli_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:05 (5)\\nSBY 13:44:52 [insn_slli_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:04 (4)\\nSBY 13:44:52 [insn_slli_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 13:44:52 [insn_slli_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY 13:44:52 [insn_slli_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY 13:45:34 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:56  Status: passed\\nSBY 13:45:34 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 13:45:34 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 13:45:34 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:56 (56)\\nSBY 13:45:34 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:54 (54)\\nSBY 13:45:34 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 13:45:34 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY 13:45:34 [reg_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\n\\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\\nFailed: pc_fwd_ch0\\n\\n--- pc_fwd_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\nSBY 13:44:38 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 8..\\nSBY 13:44:38 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\nSBY 13:44:38 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\\nSBY 13:44:38 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 11..\\nSBY 13:44:38 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 12..\\nSBY 13:44:38 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 13..\\nSBY 13:44:38 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skippin