{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'for id in hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 hyp-20260710-003-r8s2; do jq -r --arg id \"$id\" '\"'select(.id==\"'$id) | \"ID: '\"\\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nHYP:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nCHANGES:\\\\n\\\\(.changes // .hypothesis.changes)\\\\nOUTCOME: \\\\(.outcome)\\\\nERROR: \\\\(.error // \\\"\\\")\\\\n---\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl; done\"","aggregated_output":"ID: hyp-20260710-003-r3s2\nTITLE: Low-cost call and return predictor\nHYP:\nAdd a narrowly scoped call/return predictor rather than a general branch\npredictor.  A fetch-side predecode should predict every legal direct JAL\nfrom its encoded target, and a small return-address stack should predict\nthe common CoreMark ABI return form JALR x0, ra, 0 using return addresses\npushed by calls.  Carry the predicted next PC with each queued instruction;\nEX compares it with the resolved next PC and flushes only on a genuine\nmismatch, preserving the existing recovery path.  Keep conditional B-type\nbranches and non-return JALR instructions sequential, and implement the\nstack as compact registered state without a tag/counter lookup, limiting\nboth misprediction exposure and Fmax/ LUT cost while removing exact call\nand return redirect bubbles.\n\nCHANGES:\n[{\"file\":\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a compact direct-JAL predecoder and return-address stack with call push, ABI-return pop, reset, prediction metadata, and resolved-control-flow recovery/update signals.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Extend the prefetch-queue and ID/EX payloads with predicted next-PC validity and target metadata used to validate call/return speculation.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Integrate registered call/return prediction into queue fill and fetch-PC sequencing while keeping conditional branches sequential and preserving redirect priority and queue flush semantics.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Propagate the queued prediction metadata into ID/EX without changing decoded architectural control signals.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Compare resolved JAL/JALR next-PC behavior with the carried call/return prediction and issue redirects only for mismatches, retaining alignment traps and RVFI pc_wdata.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect predictor lookup, training, and recovery across the existing fetch queue and execute stage without changing the RV32IM or RVFI interface.\"}]\nOUTCOME: broken\nERROR: cosim_failed: coremark.elf\n---\nID: hyp-20260710-003-r5s2\nTITLE: Queue-safe return-address stack predictor\nHYP:\nAdd a small return-address stack trained only by legal direct JAL calls and\nused only for the exact ABI return form JALR x0, x1, 0.  Compute the return\ntarget from the RAS and the ID-stage register value when the queue head is\nconsumed, then atomically replace the queue contents and fetch PC with that\ntarget; leave conditional branches, indirect jumps, and direct JAL fetches\non the existing EX-resolution path.  Carry the prediction target through\nID/EX and suppress the EX redirect only when the resolved return target\nexactly matches it, otherwise use the normal recovery redirect.  Update\npush/pop state at the same queue transaction boundary and invalidate the\nprediction on reset or an empty RAS.  This should remove correctly\npredicted return bubbles while keeping predictor logic off the existing\nimem address critical path and avoiding the prior desynchronization bug.\n\nCHANGES:\n[{\"file\":\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement a compact resettable return-address stack with exact ABI-return qualification, call push/pop training, top-of-stack lookup, and one-cycle prediction metadata.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Extend the prefetch and ID/EX payloads with return-prediction-valid and predicted-target fields used for atomic queue control and EX validation.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect the queue-head decode operands, RAS training, prediction fire/target, and recovery signals without changing the memory or RVFI interfaces.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Add an atomic return-prediction queue transaction that discards younger sequential entries and advances fetch from the RAS target while preserving normal redirect and bus-stall priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch return-prediction metadata with the consumed instruction and expose the ID-stage return qualification/value needed by the predictor.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Validate predicted ABI returns against the resolved aligned JALR target and redirect only on a mismatch, retaining existing jump and trap behavior.\"}]\nOUTCOME: broken\nERROR: cosim_failed: coremark.elf\n---\nID: hyp-20260710-003-r6s2\nTITLE: Queue-head adaptive conditional branch predictor\nHYP:\nAdd a compact 2-bit saturating branch-history table indexed by low PC bits,\nconsulted only when the valid queue head is consumed.  For a conditional\nbranch, predict taken only in the strong/weak-taken states and compute its\nimmediate target from the queue-head instruction; if taken, atomically flush\nyounger queue entries and restart the registered fetch PC at that target.\nCarry the prediction through ID/EX, train the table from the EX-resolved\noutcome, and redirect on any predicted-versus-resolved next-PC mismatch.\nKeep JAL/JALR and non-branches on their existing path, preserve alignment\ntraps and RVFI next-PC behavior, and ensure reset, imem stalls, and queue\nreplacement use one coordinated state transition.\n\nCHANGES:\n[{\"file\":\"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement a small resettable 2-bit BHT with queue-head lookup, conditional-branch qualification, s