{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'current best|462.59|hyp-20260710-002-r2s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md | head -n 30\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md:5:- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.29%): A two-entry instruction prefetch queue with an independent fetch PC lets instruction-bus backpressure overlap backend/data stalls, but redirect handling must atomically invalidate queued wrong-path entries.\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Add a decoupled instruction prefetch queue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current fetch stage has no storage between the instruction bus and\\ndecode: every cycle with io_imemReady low injects a front-end bubble, even\\nwhen the backend is otherwise able to make progress.  The benchmark also\\ninjects independent data-bus stalls, during which the current pipeline stops\\nfetching and cannot use those cycles to build up useful instruction supply.\\nA small queue targets this structural coupling without changing retirement\\nwidth or the architectural memory behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current fetch stage has no storage between the instruction bus and\\ndecode: every cycle with io_imemReady low injects a front-end bubble, even\\nwhen the backend is otherwise able to make progress.  The benchmark also\\ninjects independent data-bus stalls, during which the current pipeline stops\\nfetching and cannot use those cycles to build up useful instruction supply.\\nA small queue targets this structural coupling without changing retirement\\nwidth or the architectural memory behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the direct combinational IF-to-ID path with a two-entry instruction\\nprefetch queue and an independent fetch PC.  Fetch into an available queue\\nslot whenever the instruction bus is ready, while decode consumes the head\\nwhenever the pipeline is not stalled; simultaneous dequeue/enqueue keeps the\\nqueue streaming at one instruction per cycle.  A redirect must atomically\\ninvalidate both queued entries and restart fetching at the resolved target,\\nand an empty queue must stall decode rather than capture an invalid\\ninstruction.  This should hide a portion of random instruction-bus latency\\nbehind data stalls and other backend bubbles, improving CoreMark\\niterations/cycle with only a small amount of front-end state.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the fetch-queue valid/consume handshake so decode stalls only when the queue is empty, while preserving redirect priority and existing RVFI retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single PC/combinational IF payload with a two-entry prefetch queue, independent fetch pointer, simultaneous fill/drain logic, and redirect flush/restart handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget front-end stall and flush decisions to the queue-empty/consume handshake, keeping load-use, dmem-stall, and wrong-path invalidation behavior correct.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the fetch-queue valid/consume handshake so decode stalls only when the queue is empty, while preserving redirect priority and existing RVFI retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the single PC/combinational IF payload with a two-entry prefetch queue, independent fetch pointer, simultaneous fill/drain logic, and redirect flush/restart handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Retarget front-end stall and flush decisions to the queue-empty/consume handshake, keeping load-use, dmem-stall, and wrong-path invalidation behavior correct.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 462.59, \"delta_pct\": 14.29, \"vs_baseline\": 63.56, \"fmax_mhz\": 201.21, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10155, \"ff\": 1990, \"seeds\": [198.57, 209.34, 201.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T15:12:39.953390Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r2s1 (improvement, +14.29%): A two-entry instruction prefetch queue with an independent fetch PC lets instruction-bus backpressure overlap backend/data stalls, but redirect handling must atomically invalidate queued wrong-path entries.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Constrain the shared multiplier to 32-bit operands\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The successful shared RV32M multiplier is intended to be a 32x32 product,\\nbut alu.sv currently zero-extends both operands to 64 bits before the\\nmultiply.  That expression can cause synthesis to preserve an unnecessarily\\nwide multiplier cone, increasing LUT usage and routing delay on the EX path.\\nThe current best is already slightly over the LUT budget, so removing this\\navoidable width is a high-leverage timing and area micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The successful shared RV32M multiplier is intended to be a 32x32 product,\\nbut alu.sv currently zero-extends both operands to 64 bits before the\\nmultiply.  That expression can cause synthesis to preserve an unnecessarily\\nwide multiplier cone, increasing LUT usage and routing delay on the EX path.\\nThe current best is already slightly over the LUT budget, so removing this\\navoidable width is a high-leverage timing and area micro-optimization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite the shared unsigned product expression so synthesis sees two\\nexplicitly 32-bit unsigned operands while retaining the full 64-bit result\\nneeded by MULH, MULHU, and the existing sign-correction equations for\\nMULHSU.  Preserve the real RV32M behavior, divide paths, and the\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions exactly.  A true 32x32 implementation\\nshould reduce multiplier resources and routing pressure without adding a\\npipeline cycle or changing forwarding and retirement timing.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Constrain the shared unsigned multiplier operands to 32 bits while preserving the full product, high-half sign corrections, ALTOPS branch, and RV32M edge behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Constrain the shared unsigned multiplier operands to 32 bits while preserving the full product, high-half sign corrections, ALTOPS branch, and RV32M edge behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 343.29, \"delta_pct\": -25.79, \"vs_baseline\": 21.38, \"fmax_mhz\": 149.32, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10571, \"ff\": 1985, \"seeds\": [141.12, 153.78, 149.32], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T15:40:09.218802Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r4s0 (regression, -25.79%): Wrapping a 64-bit multiply result with explicitly 32-bit unsigned temporaries did not guarantee a narrower synthesized multiplier and sharply regressed CoreMark, so operand-width intent must be validated against post-synthesis timing and area rather than inferred from RTL types alone.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed two-slot elastic fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two-entry prefetch queue is the strongest structural result so far,\\nreaching 462.59 fitness, but its compact-count implementation performs\\ncount arithmetic, full/empty decoding, and slot-promotion control on every\\nfetch transaction.  The failed four-entry FIFO shows that adding pointer\\nwidth and indexed muxing is not a good trade on this FPGA.  A smaller\\ncontrol topology can target the fetch-side timing and routing cost while\\nretaining the proven queue depth and stall overlap.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two-entry prefetch queue is the strongest structural result so far,\\nreaching 462.59 fitness, but its compact-count implementation performs\\ncount arithmetic, full/empty decoding, and slot-promotion control on every\\nfetch transaction.  The failed four-entry FIFO shows that adding pointer\\nwidth and indexed muxing is not a good trade on this FPGA.  A smaller\\ncontrol topology can target the fetch-side timing and routing cost while\\nretaining the proven queue depth and stall overlap.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the count-based two-entry queue control with a fixed two-slot\\nelastic buffer using explicit valid bits and direct slot-0/slot-1\\ntransitions.  Keep the independent sequential fetch PC and the existing\\none-word-per-cycle refill behavior, but eliminate count increment/decrement\\nand general FIFO indexing from the normal path.  A normal consume promotes\\nslot 1 only when valid, a consume-plus-refill replaces or appends directly,\\nand redirect atomically clears both slots while accepting the target word\\ninto slot 0 when io_imemReady is high.  Preserve the exact queue-head PC and\\ninstruction pairing, imem-stall behavior, redirect priority, and all valid\\nsemantics so the backend and RVFI timing remain unchanged.  The reduced\\ncontrol fanout and simpler next-state muxing should improve Fmax without\\npaying the area and timing cost of a deeper queue.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Restructure the two-entry prefetch queue around explicit slot-valid state and direct normal, simultaneous refill, and redirect transitions while preserving PC/instruction atomicity and handshake behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Restructure the two-entry prefetch queue around explicit slot-valid state and direct normal, simultaneous refill, and redirect transitions while preserving PC/instruction atomicity and handshake behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 310.21, \"delta_pct\": -32.94, \"vs_baseline\": 9.68, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.93, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9742, \"ff\": 1999, \"seeds\": [134.93, 136.72, 132.31], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:16:05.076645Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (regression, -32.94%): Replacing compact two-entry queue counting with explicit valid-bit pop/push promotion logic regressed CoreMark, showing that extra slot-valid decode and simultaneous-transition muxing can outweigh removing small arithmetic on FPGA fetch control paths.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor line in open(p):\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if x.get('category')=='predictor':\n        print('\\\\nID',x.get('id'),'outcome',x.get('outcome'),'fitness',x.get('fitness'),'fmax',x.get('fmax_mhz'),'delta',x.get('delta_pct'))\n        print('TITLE',x.get('title'))\n        print('HYP',x.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis','')[:1800])\n        print('CHANGES', [c.get('file') for c in x.get('changes',[])])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor line in open(p):\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if x.get('category')=='predictor':\n        print('\\\\nID',x.get('id'),'outcome',x.get('outcome'),'fitness',x.get('fitness'),'fmax',x.get('fmax_mhz'),'delta',x.get('delta_pct'))\n        print('TITLE',x.get('title'))\n        print('HYP',x.get('hypothesis',{}).get('hypothesis','')[:1800])\n        print('CHANGES', [c.get('file') for c in x.get('changes',[])])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nID hyp-20260710-003-r1s2 outcome regression fitness 247.83 fmax 106.8 delta -12.37\nTITLE Static backward-branch taken predictor\nHYP Add a decode-light static BTFNT predictor in IF for legal, word-aligned\nB-type conditional branches whose immediate is negative. Compute pc plus\nthe branch immediate in IF and fetch from that target immediately, while\ncarrying predicted_taken and predicted_target through IF/ID and ID/EX.\nEX should redirect only when the resolved architectural next PC differs from\nthe carried prediction; mispredictions still flush normally and RVFI\npc_wdata continues to report the resolved next PC. Keep JAL and JALR on\ntheir existing EX-resolution paths so this remains a single predictor change.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r2s2 outcome regression fitness 215.32 fmax 91.75 delta -46.8\nTITLE Tiny trained branch target predictor\nHYP Add a small direct-mapped branch target buffer with a tag, cached target,\nand two-bit saturating direction counter per entry.  IF predicts a\nconditional branch only on a valid BTB hit whose counter is weakly or\nstrongly taken, so cold branches and recently observed exits remain\nsequential; the target comes from the BTB rather than a new IF adder.\nUpdate the entry and counter at EX resolution, carry the prediction through\nthe existing pipeline metadata, and redirect only on a prediction mismatch.\nThis should capture stable CoreMark loops while limiting misprediction and\npreserving the baseline timing more closely than static BTFNT.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/branch_predictor.sv', 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/core.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r3s2 outcome broken fitness None fmax None delta None\nTITLE Low-cost call and return predictor\nHYP Add a narrowly scoped call/return predictor rather than a general branch\npredictor.  A fetch-side predecode should predict every legal direct JAL\nfrom its encoded target, and a small return-address stack should predict\nthe common CoreMark ABI return form JALR x0, ra, 0 using return addresses\npushed by calls.  Carry the predicted next PC with each queued instruction;\nEX compares it with the resolved next PC and flushes only on a genuine\nmismatch, preserving the existing recovery path.  Keep conditional B-type\nbranches and non-return JALR instructions sequential, and implement the\nstack as compact registered state without a tag/counter lookup, limiting\nboth misprediction exposure and Fmax/ LUT cost while removing exact call\nand return redirect bubbles.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/branch_predictor.sv', 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'rtl/core.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r4s2 outcome broken fitness None fmax None delta None\nTITLE Exact direct-JAL queue predictor\nHYP Add a decode-time exact predictor for legal, aligned direct JAL instructions.\nCompute and carry the JAL target while the instruction is admitted to the\nprefetch queue or consumed by decode, invalidate younger sequential queue\nentries, and redirect the independent fetch PC to that target before EX\nresolves the JAL.  Compare the carried prediction with the resolved next PC\nfor recovery and preserve the existing misalignment/trap behavior.  Do not\npredict conditional branches or indirect JALR returns.  Keeping this as a\none-instruction predecode with no BTB tags, counters, or IF lookup mux should\nremove direct-call bubbles while avoiding the prior predictor timing cliff.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'rtl/core.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r5s2 outcome broken fitness None fmax None delta None\nTITLE Queue-safe return-address stack predictor\nHYP Add a small return-address stack trained only by legal direct JAL calls and\nused only for the exact ABI return form JALR x0, x1, 0.  Compute the return\ntarget from the RAS and the ID-stage register value when the queue head is\nconsumed, then atomically replace the queue contents and fetch PC with that\ntarget; leave conditional branches, indirect jumps, and direct JAL fetches\non the existing EX-resolution path.  Carry the prediction target through\nID/EX and suppress the EX redirect only when the resolved return target\nexactly matches it, otherwise use the normal recovery redirect.  Update\npush/pop state at the same queue transaction boundary and invalidate the\nprediction on reset or an empty RAS.  This should remove correctly\npredicted return bubbles while keeping predictor logic off the existing\nimem address critical path and avoiding the prior desynchronization bug.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/branch_predictor.sv', 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/core.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r6s2 outcome broken fitness None fmax None delta None\nTITLE Queue-head adaptive conditional branch predictor\nHYP Add a compact 2-bit saturating branch-history table indexed by low PC bits,\nconsulted only when the valid queue head is consumed.  For a conditional\nbranch, predict taken only in the strong/weak-taken states and compute its\nimmediate target from the queue-head instruction; if taken, atomically flush\nyounger queue entries and restart the registered fetch PC at that target.\nCarry the prediction through ID/EX, train the table from the EX-resolved\noutcome, and redirect on any predicted-versus-resolved next-PC mismatch.\nKeep JAL/JALR and non-branches on their existing path, preserve alignment\ntraps and RVFI next-PC behavior, and ensure reset, imem stalls, and queue\nreplacement use one coordinated state transition.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/branch_predictor.sv', 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'rtl/core.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r7s2 outcome broken fitness None fmax None delta None\nTITLE Atomic direct-call queue predictor\nHYP Recognize only legal, word-aligned direct JAL instructions with rd=x1 at\nthe valid queue head and predict their immediate target when the head is\nconsumed.  Atomically discard younger sequential queue entries, fetch the\ntarget using the same effective target for imem_addr, fetch_pc, and queued\nentry metadata, and retain a pending target when imemReady is low.  Carry\nprediction metadata with the call so EX suppresses the later redirect only\nwhen the exact target was predicted; leave indirect jumps, conditional\nbranches, misaligned targets, and all normal recovery behavior unchanged.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r8s2 outcome broken fitness None fmax None delta None\nTITLE Retirement-trained ABI return stack predictor\nHYP Add a small return-address stack used only for the exact RV32 ABI return\nform JALR x0, x1, 0.  Push pc+4 when a legal JAL ra instruction retires and\npop when a legal ABI return retires, so nested calls, stalls, and wrong-path\nqueue contents cannot corrupt the predictor state.  At queue-head consume,\nuse a nonempty RAS top to atomically replace the younger sequential queue\nentries and set fetch_pc_q to the predicted return target; carry a\npredicted-return marker through ID/EX and suppress EX recovery only when\nthe resolved aligned JALR target exactly matches the prediction.  If the\nstack is empty or the target mismatches, retain the existing EX redirect\npath and architectural result.  Do not predict conditional branches,\ndirect JALs, or other JALR forms, and keep predictor target selection out of\nthe normal fetch lookup by using the registered queue transaction path.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/branch_predictor.sv', 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/core.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r9s2 outcome broken fitness None fmax None delta None\nTITLE Queue-head loop trip-count predictor\nHYP Add a tiny tagged loop predictor that learns the trip count of eligible\nbackward conditional branches from EX-resolved outcomes.  At queue-head\nconsumption, predict a trained loop branch taken until its learned final\niteration, then predict not-taken for the exit; untrained or unstable\nbranches remain sequential.  For a taken prediction, atomically replace\nyounger queue entries and advance the registered fetch PC to the encoded\nbranch target.  Carry prediction metadata through ID/EX and recover only\nwhen the resolved next PC differs, while training and all queue replacement\nstate transitions remain coordinated so RVFI PCs and stalls are unchanged.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/branch_predictor.sv', 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/core.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv']\n\nID hyp-20260710-003-r10s2 outcome regression fitness 196.39 fmax 85.7 delta -57.55\nTITLE Confidence-gated loop target predictor\nHYP Add a two-entry tagged loop-target table trained only by EX-resolved\nconditional branches.  Each entry stores the branch PC, its resolved\ntaken target, and a saturating confidence state; predict taken only after\nrepeated same-PC taken outcomes, and stop predicting after a not-taken\noutcome has decayed the confidence.  The queue-head lookup uses the\nregistered target from the table rather than computing a PC-plus-immediate\nadder on the ordinary imem address path.  On a confident hit, atomically\nreplace younger queue contents and advance fetch to the stored target;\ncarry the prediction with the branch and let EX recover only when the\nresolved next PC differs.  Keep reset, imem stalls, alignment traps,\nRVFI pc_next, and predictor training coordinated with the same queue\ntransaction so untrained, exit, and unstable branches remain sequential.\n\nCHANGES ['rtl/branch_predictor.sv', 'rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'rtl/core.sv', 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'rtl/ex_stage.sv']\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
