ion.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current core resolves JALR returns in EX, so every correctly formed\\nfunction return first fetches sequential instructions and then flushes the\\ntwo-entry queue.  General BTB/BHT and broad static prediction already hurt\\nthis fixture through timing or exit-branch mispredictions.  ABI returns are\\na narrower opportunity because their target is an exact call-stack value.\\nThe previous RAS attempts trained from speculative queue activity; keeping\\npredictor state tied to retirement should avoid that desynchronization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small return-address stack used only for the exact RV32 ABI return\\nform JALR x0, x1, 0.  Push pc+4 when a legal JAL ra instruction retires and\\npop when a legal ABI return retires, so nested calls, stalls, and wrong-path\\nqueue contents cannot corrupt the predictor state.  At queue-head consume,\\nuse a nonempty RAS top to atomically replace the younger sequential queue\\nentries and set fetch_pc_q to the predicted return target; carry a\\npredicted-return marker through ID/EX and suppress EX recovery only when\\nthe resolved aligned JALR target exactly matches the prediction.  If the\\nstack is empty or the target mismatches, retain the existing EX redirect\\npath and architectural result.  Do not predict conditional branches,\\ndirect JALs, or other JALR forms, and keep predictor target selection out of\\nthe normal fetch lookup by using the registered queue transaction path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a compact resettable return-address stack with retirement push/pop updates, exact ABI-return qualification, top-of-stack prediction, and empty-stack handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add return-prediction-valid and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect retirement-based call/return training, queue-head return lookup, atomic prediction control, and EX validation without changing the RVFI or memory interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered, atomic queue replacement for a predicted ABI return, updating fetch_pc_q and queued PCs consistently while preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the return-prediction metadata with the consumed instruction and expose the exact return qualification needed by the predictor.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate predicted return targets against the resolved JALR target and issue recovery only on mismatch, preserving alignment traps and RVFI pc_next semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a compact resettable return-address stack with retirement push/pop updates, exact ABI-return qualification, top-of-stack prediction, and empty-stack handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add return-prediction-valid and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect retirement-based call/return training, queue-head return lookup, atomic prediction control, and EX validation without changing the RVFI or memory interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered, atomic queue replacement for a predicted ABI return, updating fetch_pc_q and queued PCs consistently while preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the return-prediction metadata with the consumed instruction and expose the exact return qualification needed by the predictor.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate predicted return targets against the resolved JALR target and issue recovery only on mismatch, preserving alignment traps and RVFI pc_next semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Carry PC plus four into execute\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The EX stage recomputes the sequential next PC and the JAL return address\\nwith 32-bit additions for every pipeline instruction.  Those additions\\nsit alongside the ALU, forwarding, and control-flow logic in the stage that\\nis most sensitive to timing and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The EX stage recomputes the sequential next PC and the JAL return address\\nwith 32-bit additions for every pipeline instruction.  Those additions\\nsit alongside the ALU, forwarding, and control-flow logic in the stage that\\nis most sensitive to timing and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute PC+4 once while the instruction is entering ID and carry that\\nvalue in the ID/EX bundle.  Use the registered value for sequential\\npc_next, JAL's rd writeback value, and the sequential side of the existing\\nmisalignment-trap bookkeeping, while leaving branch and JALR target\\nformation, forwarding, flushes, and retirement unchanged.  Moving this\\nadder out of EX should shorten the critical path with only a small amount\\nof pipeline storage and no IPC or ISA change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a PC+4 field to the ID/EX pipeline bundle so execute receives the precomputed sequential next-PC value.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and register in.pc + 32'd4 alongside the decoded instruction, resetting it with the existing ID/EX bubble state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace EX-stage sequential PC and JAL return-address additions with the carried PC+4 value while preserving branch/JALR targets, traps, and RVFI pc_next behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a PC+4 field to the ID/EX pipeline bundle so execute receives the precomputed sequential next-PC value.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and register in.pc + 32'd4 alongside the decoded instruction, resetting it with the existing ID/EX bubble state.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace EX-stage sequential PC and JAL return-address additions with the carried PC+4 value while preserving branch/JALR targets, traps, and RVFI pc_next behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 374.68, \"delta_pct\": -19.0, \"vs_baseline\": 32.48, \"fmax_mhz\": 162.97, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10238, \"ff\": 2028, \"seeds\": [154.08, 162.97, 170.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:19:15.282649Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-001-r9s0 (regression, -19.00%): Moving the PC+4 adder from EX into ID and carrying a 32-bit result through ID/EX regressed CoreMark, showing that stage relocation can shift timing/routing pressure into decode and bundle transport instead of shortening the critical path.\"}\n27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Retimed two-slot elastic fetch buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The two-entry prefetch queue is the strongest structural result so far,\\nreaching 462.59 fitness, but its compact-count implementation performs\\ncount arithmetic, full/empty decoding, and slot-promotion control on every\\nfetch transaction.  The failed four-entry FIFO shows that adding pointer\\nwidth and indexed muxing is not a good trade on this FPGA.  A smaller\\ncontrol topology can target the fetch-side timing and routing cost while\\nretaining the proven queue depth and stall overlap.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The two-entry prefetch queue is the strongest structural result so far,\\nreaching 462.59 fitness, but its compact-count implementation performs\\ncount arithmetic, full/empty decoding, and slot-promotion control on every\\nfetch transaction.  The failed four-entry FIFO shows that adding pointer\\nwidth and indexed muxing is not a good trade on this FPGA.  A smaller\\ncontrol topology can target the fetch-side timing and routing cost while\\nretaining the proven queue depth and stall overlap.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the count-based two-entry queue control with a fixed two-slot\\nelastic buffer using explicit valid bits and direct slot-0/slot-1\\ntransitions.  Keep the independent sequential fetch PC and the existing\\none-word-per-cycle refill behavior, but eliminate count increment/decrement\\nand general FIFO indexing from the normal path.  A normal consume promotes\\nslot 1 only when valid, a consume-plus-refill replaces or appends directly,\\nand redirect atomically clears both slots while accepting the target word\\ninto slot 0 when io_imemReady is high.  Preserve the exact queue-head PC and\\ninstruction pairing, imem-stall behavior, redirect priority, and all valid\\nsemantics so the backend and RVFI timing remain unchanged.  The reduced\\ncontrol fanout and simpler next-state muxing should improve Fmax without\\npaying the area and timing cost of a deeper queue.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Restructure the two-entry prefetch queue around explicit slot-valid state and direct normal, simultaneous refill, and redirect transitions while preserving PC/instruction atomicity and handshake behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Restructure the two-entry prefetch queue around explicit slot-valid state and direct normal, simultaneous refill, and redirect transitions while preserving PC/instruction atomicity and handshake behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 310.21, \"delta_pct\": -32.94, \"vs_baseline\": 9.68, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.93, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4349632, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9742, \"ff\": 1999, \"seeds\": [134.93, 136.72, 132.31], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-10T17:16:05.076645Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-10 hyp-20260710-002-r9s1 (regression, -32.94%): Replacing compact two-entry queue counting with explicit valid-bit pop/push promotion logic regressed CoreMark, showing that extra slot-valid decode and simultaneous-transition muxing can outweigh removing small arithmetic on FPGA fetch control paths.\"}\n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260710-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Queue-head loop trip-count predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark contains stable-count backward loops, but broad static backward\\nprediction mispredicts loop exits and a general BTB/BHT lookup damaged the\\nfetch timing path.  A loop predictor can target the predictable taken\\niterations while learning the exact exit point, avoiding the systematic\\nexit penalties of BTFNT without adding a full branch target structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark contains stable-count backward loops, but broad static backward\\nprediction mispredicts loop exits and a general BTB/BHT lookup damaged the\\nfetch timing path.  A loop predictor can target the predictable taken\\niterations while learning the exact exit point, avoiding the systematic\\nexit penalties of BTFNT without adding a full branch target structure.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny tagged loop predictor that learns the trip count of eligible\\nbackward conditional branches from EX-resolved outcomes.  At queue-head\\nconsumption, predict a trained loop branch taken until its learned final\\niteration, then predict not-taken for the exit; untrained or unstable\\nbranches remain sequential.  For a taken prediction, atomically replace\\nyounger queue entries and advance the registered fetch PC to the encoded\\nbranch target.  Carry prediction metadata through ID/EX and recover only\\nwhen the resolved next PC differs, while training and all queue replacement\\nstate transitions remain coordinated so RVFI PCs and stalls are unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a compact tagged loop table with trip-count learning, confidence/stability tracking, queue-head prediction, reset, and EX outcome training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-valid, predicted-taken, and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect loop-predictor queue-head lookup, EX training, and prediction recovery without changing the RV32IM or RVFI interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform the registered queue-head loop prediction transaction atomically, flushing younger entries and updating fetch_pc_q to a predicted taken target while preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate loop prediction metadata with the consumed instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate predicted loop next-PC behavior against the resolved conditional branch, redirect on mismatch, and preserve alignment traps and RVFI pc_next semantics.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a compact tagged loop table with trip-count learning, confidence/stability tracking, queue-head prediction, reset, and EX outcome training.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-valid, predicted-taken, and predicted-target metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect loop-predictor queue-head lookup, EX training, and prediction recovery without changing the RV32IM or RVFI interfaces.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Perform the registered queue-head loop prediction transaction atomically, flushing younger entries and updating fetch_pc_q to a predicted taken target while preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate loop prediction metadata with the consumed instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate predicted loop next-PC behavior against the resolved conditional branch, redirect on mismatch, and preserve alignment traps and RVFI pc_next semantics.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: insn_beq_ch0\\ngine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 14:20:48 [insn_bne_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_insn_check_sv_198_153 at rvfi_insn_check.sv:198.6-198.31 step 20\\nSBY 14:20:48 [insn_sub_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY 14:20:48 [insn_bne_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\nSBY 14:20:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:01  waiting for solver (1 minute)\\nSBY 14:21:38 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:50  Status: passed\\nSBY 14:21:38 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 14:21:38 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 14:21:38 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed c