{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-003-r12s2.yaml && ruby -e 'require \\\"yaml\\\"; p YAML.load_file(ARGV[0])' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-003-r12s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260710-003-r12s2\ntitle: \"Fetch-response direct-JAL target predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current front end fetches sequentially until EX resolves every JAL,\n  then flushes the two-entry queue and pays a redirect recovery penalty.\n  Broad BTB/BHT and loop predictors previously hurt Fmax because lookup and\n  target selection were placed on the ordinary imem address path.  Direct\n  JALs are an attractive narrower target because their direction and target\n  are encoded in the accepted instruction itself, with no training or aliasing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a zero-table direct-JAL predictor in the fetch-response transaction.\n  When an imem word is accepted and is a legal, aligned JAL, compute its\n  target in the sequential queue-update path, discard only queue entries\n  younger than that JAL, and set the independent fetch PC to the target for\n  the next request.  Carry a predicted-valid bit and target with the JAL\n  through every queue pop, refill, and ID/EX transfer; EX must suppress its\n  normal JAL redirect only when that marker and target match, while retaining\n  the normal redirect and precise misalignment behavior for all misses.\n  This removes direct-call bubbles without adding a tag lookup or mux to the\n  steady-state imem address path, and the atomic queue update avoids the\n  desynchronization seen in earlier direct-JAL experiments.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add predicted-valid and predicted-target metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX queue payloads.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Recognize accepted aligned JAL responses, atomically invalidate younger queue entries, and redirect fetch_pc_q to the computed target without changing the normal imem address mux path.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Propagate the direct-JAL prediction marker and target with the decoded instruction into ID/EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compare the carried direct-JAL prediction with the resolved target, suppress only an exact predicted redirect, and preserve trap, pc_next, and recovery behavior on misses.\"\n{\"id\"=>\"hyp-20260710-003-r12s2\", \"title\"=>\"Fetch-response direct-JAL target predictor\", \"category\"=>\"predictor\", \"motivation\"=>\"The current front end fetches sequentially until EX resolves every JAL,\\nthen flushes the two-entry queue and pays a redirect recovery penalty.\\nBroad BTB/BHT and loop predictors previously hurt Fmax because lookup and\\ntarget selection were placed on the ordinary imem address path.  Direct\\nJALs are an attractive narrower target because their direction and target\\nare encoded in the accepted instruction itself, with no training or aliasing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\"=>\"Add a zero-table direct-JAL predictor in the fetch-response transaction.\\nWhen an imem word is accepted and is a legal, aligned JAL, compute its\\ntarget in the sequential queue-update path, discard only queue entries\\nyounger than that JAL, and set the independent fetch PC to the target for\\nthe next request.  Carry a predicted-valid bit and target with the JAL\\nthrough every queue pop, refill, and ID/EX transfer; EX must suppress its\\nnormal JAL redirect only when that marker and target match, while retaining\\nthe normal redirect and precise misalignment behavior for all misses.\\nThis removes direct-call bubbles without adding a tag lookup or mux to the\\nsteady-state imem address path, and the atomic queue update avoids the\\ndesynchronization seen in earlier direct-JAL experiments.\\n\", \"expected_impact\"=>{\"fitness_delta_pct\"=>3, \"confidence\"=>\"low\"}, \"changes\"=>[{\"file\"=>\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Add predicted-valid and predicted-target metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX queue payloads.\"}, {\"file\"=>\"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Recognize accepted aligned JAL responses, atomically invalidate younger queue entries, and redirect fetch_pc_q to the computed target without changing the normal imem address mux path.\"}, {\"file\"=>\"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Propagate the direct-JAL prediction marker and target with the decoded instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\"=>\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\"=>\"Compare the carried direct-JAL prediction with the resolved target, suppress only an exact predicted redirect, and preserve trap, pc_next, and recovery behavior on misses.\"}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
