{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files cores/bench/rtl | sort\nsed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-002-r1s1.yaml\nsed -n '1,80p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260710-001-r1s0.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/core.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/imm_gen.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv\ncores/bench/rtl/wb_stage.sv\nid: hyp-20260710-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Split execute datapath into two pipeline stages\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline P&R critical path runs from the ID/EX register through the\n  forwarded-operand muxes and 32-bit EX arithmetic into EX/MEM, consuming\n  roughly 4.1-4.4 ns and limiting the worst seed to 127.03 MHz.  CoreMark is\n  already close to the LUT budget, so improving timing through retiming is a\n  more promising structural lever than widening the machine.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retiming the execute path into EX1 and EX2 with an EX1-to-EX2 register will\n  separate forwarding/operand selection from the ALU and branch-result cone.\n  Keep EX2 fully pipelined so it still accepts one instruction per cycle, and\n  update valid, redirect, load-use, and forwarding bookkeeping for the extra\n  stage.  The shorter combinational paths should raise Fmax enough to offset\n  the additional taken-branch recovery cycle on CoreMark.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Retiming and control integration for the new EX1/EX2 pipeline stages, including redirect and retirement alignment.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add the inter-stage payload needed to carry decoded operands, control, PC, and RVFI metadata across EX1.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Split operand forwarding/selection from arithmetic and branch resolution with a registered EX1-to-EX2 boundary.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Retimed stall and flush rules for the extra execute stage and its delayed branch recovery.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Adjust producer visibility and forwarding priorities for the retimed execute pipeline.\"\nid: hyp-20260710-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Share the multiplier across RV32M high-half operations\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The ALU currently computes three independent 64-bit products on every\n  combinational evaluation: signed-by-signed, unsigned-by-unsigned, and\n  signed-by-unsigned. This duplicates multiplier hardware and contributes to\n  both the LUT budget and routing pressure on the Tang Nano 20K.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the three parallel products with one unsigned 32x32 64-bit product.\n  Derive MULH and MULHSU high halves by subtracting the appropriate sign\n  correction terms from that product, while retaining the same low-half MUL\n  result and the existing ALTOPS behavior. Sharing the multiplier should\n  reduce area and routing congestion, improving Fmax without adding cycles or\n  changing the pipeline's RV32M latency.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Use one unsigned 64-bit product and sign-correction logic for MULH and MULHSU instead of three parallel multipliers, preserving ALTOPS and exact RV32M results.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
